window.THEORY_BANK=[{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"How many complete cycles does a 60 Hz supply make per second?","o":["3,600","60","120","30"],"a":1,"e":"Hertz means cycles per second. 120 is the number of times the current changes direction per second (and 3,600 is cycles per minute).","r":"Frequency"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"Aluminum conductors have more voltage drop than copper of the same size because","o":["aluminum has a lower ampacity rating","aluminum has higher resistivity (K = 21.2 versus 12.9)","aluminum has more insulation","aluminum is heavier"],"a":1,"e":"K is the resistivity constant in ohm-CM per foot. Aluminum is about 64 percent more resistive than copper, so for the same size the drop is higher.","r":"K values"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":1,"q":"A 120 V source feeds a load through conductors with a 5.9 V drop. What voltage is available at the load?","o":["114.1 V","112.9 V","5.9 V","125.9 V"],"a":0,"e":"Load voltage = source - drop = 120 - 5.9 = 114.1 V. Recognise it: conductor resistance always costs voltage; subtract the drop from the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 125.9 V - added the drop; 112.9 V - treated the drop as a percent; 5.9 V - gave the drop itself.","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A 19.2 ohm heating element is connected to 240 V. What power does it dissipate, to the nearest watt?","o":["1,500 W","4,608 W","13 W","3,000 W"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: P = E squared / R. Substitute: P = 240 x 240 / 19.2 = 3,000.0 W, about 3,000 W. Recognise it: you have voltage and resistance but no current, so use E squared over R. Why the other choices are wrong: 13 W - did not square the voltage (E / R is amps); 1,500 W - used twice the resistance; 4,608 W - multiplied E x R.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"Power","d":3,"q":"A heater is rated 4,000 W at 240 V. If it is connected to 208 V instead (resistance unchanged), what power does it produce, to the nearest watt?","o":["3,004 W","3,467 W","5,325 W","4,000 W"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: R = E1 squared / P1 stays fixed, so P2 = P1 x (E2 / E1) squared. Substitute: P2 = 4,000 x (208 / 240) squared = 4,000 x 0.7511 = 3,004.4 W. Recognise it: a fixed-resistance load at a different voltage; power follows the square of the voltage ratio. Why the other choices are wrong: 3,467 W - scaled power linearly with voltage; 5,325 W - inverted the voltage ratio; 4,000 W - assumed nameplate watts never change.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the capacitive reactance of a 100 microfarad capacitor at 60 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["166.7 ohms","0.027 ohms","26.5 ohms","53.1 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: XC = 1 / (2 x pi x f x C), with C in farads (100 microfarads = 1.00e-04 F). XC = 1 / (6.2832 x 60 x 1.00e-04) = 26.53 ohms. Recognise it: capacitors oppose current less as frequency rises; convert microfarads to farads first. Why the other choices are wrong: 166.7 ohms - left the 2 pi out of the denominator; 0.027 ohms - treated microfarads as millifarads; 53.1 ohms - used pi instead of 2 pi.","r":"XC = 1/(2 pi f C)"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"Which has the higher heat output: a 5 kW electric heater or a 15,000 BTU/hr heater?","o":["They are exactly equal","The 5 kW heater, at about 17,060 BTU/hr","The 5 kW heater, at about 5,000 BTU/hr","The 15,000 BTU/hr heater"],"a":1,"e":"5 kW x 3,412 = 17,060 BTU/hr, which is more than 15,000. Convert both to the same unit before comparing. Reading 5 kW as 5,000 BTU/hr ignores the conversion factor.","r":"1 kW = 3,412 BTU/hr"},{"c":"Kirchhoff's laws","d":1,"q":"A 240 V source feeds a series circuit with three loads. Two drops measure 80 V and 100 V. What is the drop across the third load?","o":["420 V","180 V","160 V","60 V"],"a":3,"e":"Kirchhoff's voltage law: the drops around a series loop equal the source. Third drop = 240 - 80 - 100 = 60 V. Recognise it: a series loop with one unknown voltage; subtract the known drops from the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 420 V - added everything; 160 V - subtracted only one drop; 180 V - reported the sum of the known drops.","r":"Kirchhoff's voltage law"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a balanced three-phase circuit of 8 AWG copper (16,510 CM) carrying 40 A over 150 ft one way? Use K = 12.9 and 1.732. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["8.1 V","16.2 V","4.7 V","9.4 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: VD = 1.732 x K x I x L / CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 40 x 150 / 16,510 = 8.12 V. Recognise it: three-phase circuits replace the 2 with 1.732 because the phase currents partially cancel in the return. Why the other choices are wrong: 9.4 V - used 2 (single-phase) instead of 1.732; 4.7 V - used neither 2 nor 1.732; 16.2 V - doubled the length as well as using 1.732.","r":"VD = 1.732KIL/CM"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"What is the full-load current on the 480 V side of a 500 kVA three-phase transformer, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["1,041.7 A","1,804.2 A","601.4 A","347.2 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / (1.732 x E) = 500,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 601.42 A. Recognise it: a three-phase kVA rating to amps always includes 1.732 and the voltage of the winding you are asking about. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,041.7 A - forgot 1.732; 347.2 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 1,804.2 A - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A 24.0 ohm heating element is connected to 120 V. What power does it dissipate, to the nearest watt?","o":["300 W","600 W","5 W","2,880 W"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: P = E squared / R. Substitute: P = 120 x 120 / 24.0 = 600.0 W, about 600 W. Recognise it: you have voltage and resistance but no current, so use E squared over R. Why the other choices are wrong: 5 W - did not square the voltage (E / R is amps); 300 W - used twice the resistance; 2,880 W - multiplied E x R.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 480 V AC source feeds a series circuit of 40 ohms resistance and 30 ohms inductive reactance. What is the current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["12.0 A","16.0 A","6.9 A","9.6 A"],"a":3,"e":"Impedance Z = sqrt(40^2 + 30^2) = 50.00 ohms. Current I = E / Z = 480 / 50.00 = 9.60 A. Recognise it: Ohm's law for AC uses impedance instead of resistance; find Z first. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.9 A - added R and X directly; 12.0 A - ignored the reactance; 16.0 A - ignored the resistance.","r":"I = E / Z"},{"c":"Motors","d":1,"q":"The synchronous speed of an induction motor depends on","o":["slip and torque","voltage and horsepower","rotor resistance","supply frequency and number of poles"],"a":3,"e":"Ns = 120 x f / P. Voltage and load affect the current and slip, but not the speed of the rotating field.","r":"Ns = 120f/P"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"What is the smallest copper conductor (single-phase, K = 12.9) that limits the voltage drop to 3 percent of 240 V for 50 A over 125 ft one way? Use Chapter 9 Table 8 circular mils.","o":["4 AWG","6 AWG","3 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Allowed drop = 240 x 0.03 = 7.20 V. Required CM = 2 x K x I x L / VD = 2 x 12.9 x 50 x 125 / 7.20 = 22,396 CM. The smallest size with at least that is 6 AWG (26,240 CM). Recognise it: 'what size' for a voltage-drop limit means rearrange to CM = 2KIL / VD and pick the next size up from Table 8. Why the other choices are wrong: 8 AWG - next smaller size is below the required circular mils; 4 AWG - meets the limit but is not the smallest size that does; 3 AWG - meets the limit but is not the smallest size that does.","r":"CM = 2KIL/VD"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load has a power factor of 0.95. What is the phase angle between voltage and current, to the nearest degree?","o":["18 degrees","72 degrees","86 degrees","44 degrees"],"a":0,"e":"PF = cos(angle), so angle = arccos(0.95) = 18.2 degrees. Recognise it: power factor is the cosine of the phase angle; use the inverse cosine key. Why the other choices are wrong: 72 degrees - used the inverse sine instead of inverse cosine; 44 degrees - used the inverse tangent; 86 degrees - scaled the PF to 90 degrees.","r":"PF = cos(theta)"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"An ideal transformer steps 480 V down to 120 V. If the primary current is 5 A, what is the secondary current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["20.0 A","11.5 A","1.3 A","5.0 A"],"a":0,"e":"Ideal transformer: VA in = VA out, so Ep x Ip = Es x Is. Is = Ip x Ep / Es = 5 x 480 / 120 = 20.00 A. Recognise it: step-down means more current on the low-voltage side, in exact inverse proportion. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.3 A - used the voltage ratio the wrong way round; 5.0 A - assumed the currents are equal; 11.5 A - divided by 1.732 on a single-phase unit.","r":"Transformer current ratio"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A 3,000 sq ft dwelling has general lighting at 3 VA per sq ft, two 1,500 VA small-appliance circuits and one 1,500 VA laundry circuit. Applying the standard demand factors (first 3,000 VA at 100 percent, the rest at 35 percent), what is the demand load, to the nearest VA?","o":["4,725 VA","5,100 VA","6,675 VA","13,500 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Connected load = 3,000 x 3 = 9,000 + 3,000 + 1,500 = 13,500 VA. Demand = 3,000 + 0.35 x (13,500 - 3,000) = 3,000 + 3,675.0 = 6,675.0 VA. Recognise it: a dwelling lighting-and-receptacle demand problem; add the three loads, then take 100 percent of the first 3,000 VA and 35 percent of the remainder. Why the other choices are wrong: 13,500 VA - skipped the demand factors; 4,725 VA - applied 35 percent to the whole load; 5,100 VA - left out the small-appliance and laundry circuits.","r":"Dwelling demand factors"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"An ideal transformer steps 240 V down to 120 V. If the primary current is 12.5 A, what is the secondary current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["6.3 A","25.0 A","14.4 A","12.5 A"],"a":1,"e":"Ideal transformer: VA in = VA out, so Ep x Ip = Es x Is. Is = Ip x Ep / Es = 12.5 x 240 / 120 = 25.00 A. Recognise it: step-down means more current on the low-voltage side, in exact inverse proportion. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.3 A - used the voltage ratio the wrong way round; 12.5 A - assumed the currents are equal; 14.4 A - divided by 1.732 on a single-phase unit.","r":"Transformer current ratio"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A series circuit has 40 ohms resistance and 30 ohms inductive reactance. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["50.0 ohms","70.0 ohms","10.0 ohms","17.1 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Resistance and reactance are 90 degrees apart, so they add as a right triangle: Z = square root of (R squared + X squared) = sqrt(40^2 + 30^2) = sqrt(2500) = 50.00 ohms. Recognise it: R plus XL (or XC) in series means impedance by the Pythagorean theorem. Why the other choices are wrong: 70.0 ohms - added R and X arithmetically; 10.0 ohms - subtracted R and X; 17.1 ohms - used the parallel resistor formula.","r":"Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2)"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A balanced three-phase load draws 18 A at 480 V line-to-line with a power factor of 0.88. How many kilowatts is it using, to the nearest tenth? (Use 1.732.)","o":["13.2 kW","22.8 kW","15.0 kW","7.6 kW"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: P = 1.732 x E x I x PF = 1.732 x 480 x 18 x 0.88 = 13,169 W = 13.17 kW. Recognise it: a three-phase load with a stated PF asks for true power; include 1.732 and PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 7.6 kW - forgot 1.732; 15.0 kW - left out power factor (that is kVA); 22.8 kW - used 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"P = 1.732 E I PF"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A single-phase motor draws 25 A at 240 V with a power factor of 0.85. How many watts of real power is it using, to the nearest watt?","o":["7,059 W","8,833 W","6,000 W","5,100 W"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: P = E x I x PF = 240 x 25 x 0.85 = 5,100 W. Recognise it: a motor or any inductive load has PF below 1, and real watts equal volt-amps times PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 6,000 W - left out power factor (that is VA); 7,059 W - divided by power factor; 8,833 W - applied 1.732 to a single-phase load.","r":"P = E I PF"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A 4 microfarad and a 12 microfarad capacitor are connected in series. What is the total capacitance, to the nearest tenth of a microfarad?","o":["3.0 microfarads","4.0 microfarads","8.0 microfarads","16.0 microfarads"],"a":0,"e":"Capacitors in series combine like resistors in parallel: Ct = (C1 x C2) / (C1 + C2) = (4 x 12) / (4 + 12) = 3.00 microfarads. Recognise it: capacitors are the opposite of resistors - parallel adds, series is the reciprocal formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 16.0 microfarads - added them (that is the parallel result); 8.0 microfarads - averaged the values; 4.0 microfarads - assumed the smaller capacitor alone governs.","r":"Capacitors in series"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A 230 V source is connected across a 20 ohm resistive load. What is the current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["5.8 A","0.1 A","11.5 A","19.9 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = E / R. Substitute: I = 230 / 20 = 11.50 A, which rounds to 11.5 A. Recognise it: you know voltage and resistance and want current, so put the unknown on top of the Ohm's law triangle and divide the other two. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.1 A - inverted the formula (R / E); 19.9 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase calculation; 5.8 A - used twice the resistance.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a balanced three-phase circuit of 3 AWG copper (52,620 CM) carrying 85 A over 250 ft one way? Use K = 12.9 and 1.732. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["5.2 V","10.4 V","9.0 V","18.0 V"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: VD = 1.732 x K x I x L / CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 85 x 250 / 52,620 = 9.02 V. Recognise it: three-phase circuits replace the 2 with 1.732 because the phase currents partially cancel in the return. Why the other choices are wrong: 10.4 V - used 2 (single-phase) instead of 1.732; 5.2 V - used neither 2 nor 1.732; 18.0 V - doubled the length as well as using 1.732.","r":"VD = 1.732KIL/CM"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies three motors with table full-load currents of 65 A, 34 A and 27 A. What is the minimum feeder conductor ampacity (125 percent of the largest plus the others), to the nearest tenth?","o":["126.0 A","157.5 A","141.3 A","142.3 A"],"a":3,"e":"Largest FLC is 65 A: 65 x 1.25 = 81.25. Add the others: 34 + 27. Total = 142.25 A. Recognise it: several motors on one feeder; only the largest motor gets the extra 25 percent. Why the other choices are wrong: 157.5 A - applied 125 percent to all motors; 126.0 A - no 125 percent on the largest; 141.3 A - applied 125 percent to the wrong motors.","r":"430.24"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"In a balanced delta load each phase winding carries 28 A. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["16.2 A","48.5 A","84.0 A","28.0 A"],"a":1,"e":"Delta: I(line) = 1.732 x I(phase) = 1.732 x 28 = 48.50 A. Recognise it: in delta the line voltage equals phase voltage but line current is 1.732 times phase current. Why the other choices are wrong: 28.0 A - assumed line current equals phase current (that is wye); 16.2 A - divided by 1.732 instead of multiplying; 84.0 A - multiplied by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"In a resistive circuit, doubling the current through a fixed resistance causes the heat generated to","o":["double","decrease by half","stay the same","increase four times"],"a":3,"e":"P = I squared R, so doubling I multiplies P by 4. This is why overloaded conductors and loose connections heat up so quickly.","r":"P = I^2 R"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A 225 kVA transformer is supplying 200 kVA of load. What percent of its rating is in use, to the nearest percent?","o":["113 percent","0.89 percent","89 percent","11 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Percent loading = load / rating x 100 = 200 / 225 x 100 = 88.9 percent. Recognise it: compare actual load to nameplate; a result over 100 percent means overload. Why the other choices are wrong: 113 percent - inverted the ratio; 11 percent - reported the spare capacity; 0.89 percent - forgot to multiply by 100.","r":"Transformer loading"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 45 kW three-phase resistive heater is connected to 480 V. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["31.3 A","54.1 A","162.4 A","93.8 A"],"a":1,"e":"Resistive, so PF = 1. I = W / (1.732 x E) = 45,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 54.13 A. Recognise it: kW and three-phase voltage, find amps, so divide by 1.732 x E. Why the other choices are wrong: 93.8 A - treated it as single-phase; 31.3 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 162.4 A - multiplied by 1.732.","r":"I = P / (1.732 E)"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A 2,800 sq ft dwelling has general lighting at 3 VA per sq ft, two 1,500 VA small-appliance circuits and one 1,500 VA laundry circuit. Applying the standard demand factors (first 3,000 VA at 100 percent, the rest at 35 percent), what is the demand load, to the nearest VA?","o":["4,890 VA","6,465 VA","4,515 VA","12,900 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Connected load = 2,800 x 3 = 8,400 + 3,000 + 1,500 = 12,900 VA. Demand = 3,000 + 0.35 x (12,900 - 3,000) = 3,000 + 3,465.0 = 6,465.0 VA. Recognise it: a dwelling lighting-and-receptacle demand problem; add the three loads, then take 100 percent of the first 3,000 VA and 35 percent of the remainder. Why the other choices are wrong: 12,900 VA - skipped the demand factors; 4,515 VA - applied 35 percent to the whole load; 4,890 VA - left out the small-appliance and laundry circuits.","r":"Dwelling demand factors"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":3,"q":"A true-RMS meter is preferable to an average-responding meter when measuring","o":["only DC voltages","only resistive loads","only the neutral conductor at no load","non-sinusoidal waveforms from electronic loads"],"a":3,"e":"Average-responding meters assume a sine wave and read incorrectly with harmonics (LED drivers, VFDs). True-RMS meters calculate the real heating value.","r":"Meter accuracy"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 4-pole, 60 Hz induction motor has 2.5 percent slip at full load. What is the rotor speed, to the nearest RPM?","o":["1,845 RPM","1,755 RPM","1,800 RPM","1,798 RPM"],"a":1,"e":"Ns = 120 x 60 / 4 = 1,800 RPM. Rotor speed = Ns x (1 - slip) = 1,800 x 0.975 = 1755.0 RPM. Recognise it: an induction rotor always turns slower than the field, so reduce Ns by the slip fraction. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,845 RPM - added slip to synchronous speed; 1,798 RPM - subtracted the percent as RPM; 1,800 RPM - gave synchronous speed.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"What is the time for one complete cycle of a 120 Hz supply, in milliseconds to the nearest hundredth?","o":["4.17 ms","16.67 ms","8.33 ms","1.33 ms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: T = 1 / f = 1 / 120 = 0.00833 s = 8.33 ms. Recognise it: period is the inverse of frequency; at 60 Hz there are 60 cycles per second. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.33 ms - used 1/(2 pi f), the angular relationship; 16.67 ms - doubled the period; 4.17 ms - reported a half-cycle.","r":"T = 1/f"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A single-phase motor draws 18 A at 230 V with a power factor of 0.9. How many watts of real power is it using, to the nearest watt?","o":["6,453 W","4,600 W","3,726 W","4,140 W"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: P = E x I x PF = 230 x 18 x 0.9 = 3,726 W. Recognise it: a motor or any inductive load has PF below 1, and real watts equal volt-amps times PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 4,140 W - left out power factor (that is VA); 4,600 W - divided by power factor; 6,453 W - applied 1.732 to a single-phase load.","r":"P = E I PF"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"Compared to a single-phase circuit delivering the same power at the same voltage, a three-phase circuit uses","o":["the same current per conductor","more current per conductor","less current per conductor","no neutral current in any case"],"a":2,"e":"At the same kW, 1-phase current = P / E but 3-phase current = P / (1.732 E), about 58 percent as much. That is why large loads are three-phase and conductors can be smaller.","r":"Three-phase advantage"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":2,"q":"A digital multimeter set to measure current is accidentally connected across a 120 V receptacle. The likely result is","o":["a high resistance reading","a correct voltage reading","no effect, because meters are insulated","a short circuit that can blow the meter's fuse or injure the user"],"a":3,"e":"In current mode the meter has almost zero resistance, so the source is effectively shorted through it. Use voltage mode in parallel, current mode only in series.","r":"Meter safety"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the inductive reactance of a 0.1 H coil at 60 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["75.4 ohms","6.0 ohms","18.8 ohms","37.7 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: XL = 2 x pi x f x L = 6.2832 x 60 x 0.1 = 37.70 ohms. Recognise it: an inductor opposes current more as frequency rises; multiply 2 pi f by henries. Why the other choices are wrong: 75.4 ohms - doubled the 2 pi f L result; 18.8 ohms - left out the 2; 6.0 ohms - multiplied only f x L (left out 2 pi).","r":"XL = 2 pi f L"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"What is the equivalent resistance of 50 ohm and 75 ohm resistors in parallel, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["62.5 ohms","125.0 ohms","30.0 ohms","25.0 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: Rt = (R1 x R2) / (R1 + R2) = (50 x 75) / (50 + 75) = 3750 / 125 = 30.00 ohms. Recognise it: two resistors across the same two points is parallel; the answer must be less than the smaller resistor (50 ohms). Why the other choices are wrong: 125.0 ohms - added them as a series circuit; 62.5 ohms - averaged the two values; 25.0 ohms - halved the smaller resistor.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A 45 kVA transformer is supplying 36 kVA of load. What percent of its rating is in use, to the nearest percent?","o":["80 percent","20 percent","0.8 percent","125 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Percent loading = load / rating x 100 = 36 / 45 x 100 = 80.0 percent. Recognise it: compare actual load to nameplate; a result over 100 percent means overload. Why the other choices are wrong: 125 percent - inverted the ratio; 20 percent - reported the spare capacity; 0.8 percent - forgot to multiply by 100.","r":"Transformer loading"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A load uses 8 kW of true power and 10 kVA of apparent power. What is its power factor, to two decimals?","o":["2.00","0.60","1.25","0.80"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: PF = kW / kVA = 8 / 10 = 0.800, or 0.80. Recognise it: true power over apparent power; the answer can never exceed 1.0. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.25 - inverted the ratio; 2.00 - subtracted instead of dividing; 0.60 - reported the sine of the angle (reactive ratio).","r":"PF = kW / kVA"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A kilowatt-hour is a unit of","o":["demand","current","power","energy"],"a":3,"e":"A kW is power; multiplying by time (hours) gives energy: 1 kWh = 1,000 W for one hour. Utilities bill energy in kWh. Do not confuse with kW demand, which is how fast energy is used.","r":"kWh"},{"c":"Kirchhoff's laws","d":1,"q":"A 480 V source feeds a series circuit with three loads. Two drops measure 150 V and 210 V. What is the drop across the third load?","o":["360 V","120 V","840 V","330 V"],"a":1,"e":"Kirchhoff's voltage law: the drops around a series loop equal the source. Third drop = 480 - 150 - 210 = 120 V. Recognise it: a series loop with one unknown voltage; subtract the known drops from the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 840 V - added everything; 330 V - subtracted only one drop; 360 V - reported the sum of the known drops.","r":"Kirchhoff's voltage law"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A transformer has 600 primary turns and 150 secondary turns. With 480 V applied to the primary, what is the secondary voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["96 V","120 V","208 V","1,920 V"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: Es = Ep x (Ns / Np) = 480 x (150 / 600) = 120.0 V. Recognise it: voltage follows turns; the winding with more turns has the higher voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,920 V - inverted the turns ratio; 96 V - divided by the sum of the turns; 208 V - added a 1.732 factor to a single-phase turns calculation.","r":"Turns ratio"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"Transformer core losses (iron losses) are mostly caused by","o":["voltage drop in the supply conductors","hysteresis and eddy currents in the laminated core","motor starting current","resistance of the copper windings"],"a":1,"e":"Core losses exist whenever the transformer is energized, even with no load. Copper losses (I squared R in the windings) vary with load. Laminated cores reduce eddy currents.","r":"Transformer losses"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A sine wave peaks at 170 V. What is its average value over one half-cycle, to the nearest volt?","o":["240 V","85 V","108 V","120 V"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: Vavg = 0.637 x Vpeak = 0.637 x 170 = 108.3 V. Recognise it: three factors to remember - RMS 0.707, average 0.637, peak 1.414 (RMS to peak). Why the other choices are wrong: 120 V - used the RMS factor; 240 V - used the peak factor; 85 V - halved the peak.","r":"Sine wave values"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the inductive reactance of a 0.25 H coil at 50 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["39.3 ohms","12.5 ohms","157.1 ohms","78.5 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: XL = 2 x pi x f x L = 6.2832 x 50 x 0.25 = 78.54 ohms. Recognise it: an inductor opposes current more as frequency rises; multiply 2 pi f by henries. Why the other choices are wrong: 157.1 ohms - doubled the 2 pi f L result; 39.3 ohms - left out the 2; 12.5 ohms - multiplied only f x L (left out 2 pi).","r":"XL = 2 pi f L"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies three motors with table full-load currents of 27 A, 21 A and 14 A. What is the minimum feeder conductor ampacity (125 percent of the largest plus the others), to the nearest tenth?","o":["70.8 A","68.8 A","62.0 A","77.5 A"],"a":1,"e":"Largest FLC is 27 A: 27 x 1.25 = 33.75. Add the others: 21 + 14. Total = 68.75 A. Recognise it: several motors on one feeder; only the largest motor gets the extra 25 percent. Why the other choices are wrong: 77.5 A - applied 125 percent to all motors; 62.0 A - no 125 percent on the largest; 70.8 A - applied 125 percent to the wrong motors.","r":"430.24"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load draws 40 kW at 50 kVA. What is its reactive power, to the nearest tenth of a kVAR?","o":["30.0 kVAR","10.0 kVAR","64.0 kVAR","90.0 kVAR"],"a":0,"e":"The power triangle: kVA squared = kW squared + kVAR squared. kVAR = sqrt(50^2 - 40^2) = sqrt(900) = 30.00 kVAR. Recognise it: kVA is the hypotenuse, so the missing side comes from the difference of squares. Why the other choices are wrong: 10.0 kVAR - subtracted kW from kVA directly; 90.0 kVAR - added kW and kVA; 64.0 kVAR - added the squares instead of subtracting.","r":"Power triangle"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"What is the full-load secondary current of a 37.5 kVA single-phase transformer with a 240 V secondary, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["9.0 A","156.3 A","195.3 A","90.2 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / E = 37,500 / 240 = 156.25 A. Recognise it: single-phase transformer rating to amps is VA divided by volts, no 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 90.2 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase transformer; 195.3 A - assumed an 0.8 power factor; 9.0 A - multiplied instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"Motors","d":1,"q":"What is the synchronous speed of a 4-pole induction motor on a 60 Hz supply?","o":["3,600 RPM","900 RPM","1,800 RPM","8 RPM"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: Ns = 120 x f / P = 120 x 60 / 4 = 7,200 / 4 = 1,800 RPM. Recognise it: the question gives poles and frequency and asks for speed; the 120 already accounts for pole pairs. Why the other choices are wrong: 900 RPM - used 60 instead of 120; 8 RPM - inverted poles and frequency; 3,600 RPM - divided by pole pairs instead of poles.","r":"Ns = 120f/P"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A single-phase motor draws 40 A at 240 V with a power factor of 0.7. How many watts of real power is it using, to the nearest watt?","o":["11,639 W","9,600 W","13,714 W","6,720 W"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: P = E x I x PF = 240 x 40 x 0.7 = 6,720 W. Recognise it: a motor or any inductive load has PF below 1, and real watts equal volt-amps times PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 9,600 W - left out power factor (that is VA); 13,714 W - divided by power factor; 11,639 W - applied 1.732 to a single-phase load.","r":"P = E I PF"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has R = 6 ohms, XL = 18 ohms and XC = 10 ohms. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["20.6 ohms","28.6 ohms","34.0 ohms","10.0 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"XL and XC are opposite, so they subtract: net X = 18 - 10 = 8 ohms. Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2) = sqrt(36 + 64) = 10.00 ohms. Recognise it: three series elements; combine XL and XC first, then use the right-triangle formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 28.6 ohms - added XL and XC instead of subtracting; 34.0 ohms - added all three values; 20.6 ohms - left out the resistance.","r":"Series RLC"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"The NEC table full-load current for a motor is 11 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the branch-circuit conductors for this single continuous-duty motor (125 percent), to the nearest tenth?","o":["11.0 A","13.8 A","12.7 A","27.5 A"],"a":1,"e":"Conductor ampacity = 11 x 1.25 = 13.75 A. Recognise it: a conductor-sizing question for one motor uses 125 percent of table FLC; overload and short-circuit devices use different percentages. Why the other choices are wrong: 11.0 A - used 100 percent of FLC; 12.7 A - used the 115 percent overload factor; 27.5 A - used the 250 percent short-circuit factor.","r":"430.22"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"A 10 ohm resistor is in series with two resistors (20 ohm and 30 ohm) that are in parallel with each other. What is the total circuit resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["12.0 ohms","60.0 ohms","5.5 ohms","22.0 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Step 1, reduce the parallel pair: (20 x 30) / (20 + 30) = 12.00 ohms. Step 2, add the series resistor: 10 + 12.00 = 22.00 ohms. Recognise it: a mix of series and parallel; always collapse the parallel groups first, then add the series parts. Why the other choices are wrong: 60.0 ohms - treated everything as series; 5.5 ohms - treated everything as parallel; 12.0 ohms - forgot to add the series resistor.","r":"Combination circuit"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 300 kW load operates at 0.7 PF. How many kVAR of capacitors are needed to raise the power factor to 0.95, to the nearest kVAR?","o":["207 kVAR","306 kVAR","90 kVAR","75 kVAR"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: kVAR(cap) = kW x (tan of old angle - tan of new angle). Old angle = arccos(0.7) = 45.6 deg, tan = 1.020. New angle = arccos(0.95) = 18.2 deg, tan = 0.329. kVAR = 300 x (1.020 - 0.329) = 207.5. Recognise it: capacitors supply reactive power; subtract the reactive demand before and after correction. Why the other choices are wrong: 90 kVAR - used kW x (1 - PF); 75 kVAR - multiplied by the PF difference; 306 kVAR - removed all reactive power regardless of the target.","r":"PF correction"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"A 12 ohm resistor is in series with two resistors (6 ohm and 3 ohm) that are in parallel with each other. What is the total circuit resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["21.0 ohms","14.0 ohms","1.7 ohms","2.0 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Step 1, reduce the parallel pair: (6 x 3) / (6 + 3) = 2.00 ohms. Step 2, add the series resistor: 12 + 2.00 = 14.00 ohms. Recognise it: a mix of series and parallel; always collapse the parallel groups first, then add the series parts. Why the other choices are wrong: 21.0 ohms - treated everything as series; 1.7 ohms - treated everything as parallel; 2.0 ohms - forgot to add the series resistor.","r":"Combination circuit"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"How long can a 12 AWG copper (6,530 CM) single-phase circuit carrying 20 A on 120 V run (one way) before the drop exceeds 5 percent? Use K = 12.9 and round to the nearest foot.","o":["152 ft","38 ft","76 ft","88 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Allowed drop = 120 x 0.05 = 6.00 V. Rearranged: L = VD x CM / (2 x K x I) = 6.00 x 6,530 / (2 x 12.9 x 20) = 75.9 ft. Recognise it: a maximum-distance question; solve the voltage-drop formula for L. Why the other choices are wrong: 152 ft - left out the 2 in the formula; 38 ft - applied the round-trip factor twice; 88 ft - used 1.732 for a single-phase run.","r":"L = VD x CM / 2KI"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 30 kW three-phase resistive heater is connected to 480 V. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["20.8 A","36.1 A","62.5 A","108.3 A"],"a":1,"e":"Resistive, so PF = 1. I = W / (1.732 x E) = 30,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 36.09 A. Recognise it: kW and three-phase voltage, find amps, so divide by 1.732 x E. Why the other choices are wrong: 62.5 A - treated it as single-phase; 20.8 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 108.3 A - multiplied by 1.732.","r":"I = P / (1.732 E)"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"The tolerance on a resistor with a gold fourth band is","o":["plus or minus 1 percent","plus or minus 10 percent","plus or minus 20 percent","plus or minus 5 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Gold = 5 percent, silver = 10 percent, no band = 20 percent. Brown = 1 percent. Recognise it: the last band tells tolerance; the first bands give the value.","r":"Resistor color code"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":2,"q":"A wattmeter measures","o":["reactive power only","apparent power only","frequency","true power in watts"],"a":3,"e":"It combines voltage and current (and their phase angle), so it reads true power. A voltmeter times an ammeter reading gives VA, not necessarily watts.","r":"Wattmeter"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":2,"q":"Capacitors in electrical equipment can be dangerous after power is removed because","o":["they act as fuses","they raise the supply voltage","they can store a charge and discharge through a person","they always draw current from the ground"],"a":2,"e":"A charged capacitor holds voltage until discharged. Discharge and verify with a meter before working on motor-start, VFD and power-supply circuits.","r":"Stored energy"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A sine wave peaks at 200 V. What is its average value over one half-cycle, to the nearest volt?","o":["283 V","127 V","141 V","100 V"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: Vavg = 0.637 x Vpeak = 0.637 x 200 = 127.4 V. Recognise it: three factors to remember - RMS 0.707, average 0.637, peak 1.414 (RMS to peak). Why the other choices are wrong: 141 V - used the RMS factor; 283 V - used the peak factor; 100 V - halved the peak.","r":"Sine wave values"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"Resistors of 30, 60 and 20 ohms are connected in parallel. What is the total resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["110.0 ohms","36.7 ohms","12.0 ohms","10.0 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: 1/Rt = 1/30 + 1/60 + 1/20 = 0.1000. Rt = 1 / 0.1000 = 10.00 ohms. Recognise it: three branches across one source; answer must be below the smallest branch (20 ohms) - use that as a check. Why the other choices are wrong: 110.0 ohms - added the values (series); 36.7 ohms - took the average; 12.0 ohms - paralleled only the two smallest resistors.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A conductor has a 90 C ampacity of 30 A. It is bundled with 6 current-carrying conductors in one conduit. Using the adjustment factor (80 percent for 6 conductors), what is the adjusted ampacity, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["6.0 A","37.5 A","24.0 A","30.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"Adjusted ampacity = 30 x 0.8 = 24.0 A. Recognise it: 4-6 conductors gets 80 percent, 7-9 gets 70 percent, 10-20 gets 50 percent; multiply the table ampacity by that factor. Why the other choices are wrong: 30.0 A - did not apply any adjustment; 37.5 A - divided by the factor instead of multiplying; 6.0 A - multiplied by the percent removed.","r":"Table 310.15(C)(1)"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"How long can a 12 AWG copper (6,530 CM) single-phase circuit carrying 16 A on 120 V run (one way) before the drop exceeds 3 percent? Use K = 12.9 and round to the nearest foot.","o":["114 ft","57 ft","28 ft","66 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Allowed drop = 120 x 0.03 = 3.60 V. Rearranged: L = VD x CM / (2 x K x I) = 3.60 x 6,530 / (2 x 12.9 x 16) = 56.9 ft. Recognise it: a maximum-distance question; solve the voltage-drop formula for L. Why the other choices are wrong: 114 ft - left out the 2 in the formula; 28 ft - applied the round-trip factor twice; 66 ft - used 1.732 for a single-phase run.","r":"L = VD x CM / 2KI"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has R = 20 ohms, XL = 50 ohms and XC = 26 ohms. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["96.0 ohms","78.6 ohms","56.4 ohms","31.2 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"XL and XC are opposite, so they subtract: net X = 50 - 26 = 24 ohms. Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2) = sqrt(400 + 576) = 31.24 ohms. Recognise it: three series elements; combine XL and XC first, then use the right-triangle formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 78.6 ohms - added XL and XC instead of subtracting; 96.0 ohms - added all three values; 56.4 ohms - left out the resistance.","r":"Series RLC"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A delta-connected load draws 86 A in each line. What is the current in each phase winding, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["149.0 A","28.7 A","86.0 A","49.7 A"],"a":3,"e":"Delta: I(phase) = I(line) / 1.732 = 86 / 1.732 = 49.65 A. Recognise it: phase current is the smaller number in a delta; line current is 1.732 times larger. Why the other choices are wrong: 86.0 A - assumed phase current equals line current; 149.0 A - multiplied by 1.732; 28.7 A - divided by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"The NEC table full-load current for a motor is 21 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the branch-circuit conductors for this single continuous-duty motor (125 percent), to the nearest tenth?","o":["52.5 A","24.2 A","26.3 A","21.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"Conductor ampacity = 21 x 1.25 = 26.25 A. Recognise it: a conductor-sizing question for one motor uses 125 percent of table FLC; overload and short-circuit devices use different percentages. Why the other choices are wrong: 21.0 A - used 100 percent of FLC; 24.2 A - used the 115 percent overload factor; 52.5 A - used the 250 percent short-circuit factor.","r":"430.22"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"What is the equivalent resistance of 12 ohm and 4 ohm resistors in parallel, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["8.0 ohms","3.0 ohms","2.0 ohms","16.0 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: Rt = (R1 x R2) / (R1 + R2) = (12 x 4) / (12 + 4) = 48 / 16 = 3.00 ohms. Recognise it: two resistors across the same two points is parallel; the answer must be less than the smaller resistor (4 ohms). Why the other choices are wrong: 16.0 ohms - added them as a series circuit; 8.0 ohms - averaged the two values; 2.0 ohms - halved the smaller resistor.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A load uses 72 kW of true power and 90 kVA of apparent power. What is its power factor, to two decimals?","o":["18.00","0.60","1.25","0.80"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: PF = kW / kVA = 72 / 90 = 0.800, or 0.80. Recognise it: true power over apparent power; the answer can never exceed 1.0. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.25 - inverted the ratio; 18.00 - subtracted instead of dividing; 0.60 - reported the sine of the angle (reactive ratio).","r":"PF = kW / kVA"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"Chapter 9 Table 8 gives 6 AWG uncoated copper as 0.491 ohms per 1,000 ft. What is the resistance of 150 ft of this conductor, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["3.27 ohms","0.74 ohms","73.65 ohms","0.07 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Scale by length: R = 0.491 x 150 / 1,000 = 0.074 ohms. Recognise it: Table 8 resistance is per 1,000 ft, so multiply by (length / 1,000). Why the other choices are wrong: 73.65 ohms - forgot to divide by 1,000; 0.74 ohms - divided by 100; 3.27 ohms - inverted the length ratio.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 8"},{"c":"Power","d":3,"q":"A heater is rated 1,000 W at 120 V. If it is connected to 110 V instead (resistance unchanged), what power does it produce, to the nearest watt?","o":["1,000 W","1,190 W","917 W","840 W"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: R = E1 squared / P1 stays fixed, so P2 = P1 x (E2 / E1) squared. Substitute: P2 = 1,000 x (110 / 120) squared = 1,000 x 0.8403 = 840.3 W. Recognise it: a fixed-resistance load at a different voltage; power follows the square of the voltage ratio. Why the other choices are wrong: 917 W - scaled power linearly with voltage; 1,190 W - inverted the voltage ratio; 1,000 W - assumed nameplate watts never change.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 8-pole, 60 Hz induction motor has 5 percent slip at full load. What is the rotor speed, to the nearest RPM?","o":["895 RPM","855 RPM","900 RPM","945 RPM"],"a":1,"e":"Ns = 120 x 60 / 8 = 900 RPM. Rotor speed = Ns x (1 - slip) = 900 x 0.950 = 855.0 RPM. Recognise it: an induction rotor always turns slower than the field, so reduce Ns by the slip fraction. Why the other choices are wrong: 945 RPM - added slip to synchronous speed; 895 RPM - subtracted the percent as RPM; 900 RPM - gave synchronous speed.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"A heater is rated 2,000 W at 240 V. What is its hot resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["8.3 ohms","57.6 ohms","28.8 ohms","14.4 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: R = E squared / P. Substitute: R = 240 x 240 / 2,000 = 28.80 ohms, about 28.8 ohms. Recognise it: nameplate watts and volts are given and resistance is wanted, so use R = E squared / P. Why the other choices are wrong: 14.4 ohms - halved the result; 8.3 ohms - used P / E, which is amps, not ohms; 57.6 ohms - doubled the result.","r":"R = E^2 / P"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A transformer has 500 primary turns and 250 secondary turns. With 240 V applied to the primary, what is the secondary voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["120 V","480 V","80 V","208 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: Es = Ep x (Ns / Np) = 240 x (250 / 500) = 120.0 V. Recognise it: voltage follows turns; the winding with more turns has the higher voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 480 V - inverted the turns ratio; 80 V - divided by the sum of the turns; 208 V - added a 1.732 factor to a single-phase turns calculation.","r":"Turns ratio"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"In a balanced three-phase wye system with linear loads, the neutral conductor current is approximately","o":["zero","equal to the phase current","1.732 times the phase current","three times the phase current"],"a":0,"e":"The three phase currents are 120 degrees apart and add vectorially to zero when balanced. With unbalanced loads the neutral carries the difference.","r":"Neutral current"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A 4-pole motor on 60 Hz turns at 1,725 RPM under load. What is the percent slip, to the nearest tenth?","o":["4.3 percent","4.2 percent","75.0 percent","95.8 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Synchronous speed Ns = 120 x 60 / 4 = 1,800 RPM. Slip = (Ns - Nr) / Ns x 100 = (1,800 - 1,725) / 1,800 x 100 = 4.17 percent. Recognise it: slip is always based on synchronous speed; find Ns first. Why the other choices are wrong: 4.3 percent - divided by rotor speed instead of synchronous speed; 75.0 percent - reported the RPM difference as a percent; 95.8 percent - reported rotor speed as a percent of synchronous.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"What is the full-load current on the 208 V side of a 225 kVA three-phase transformer, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["1,081.7 A","360.6 A","624.6 A","1,873.6 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / (1.732 x E) = 225,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 624.56 A. Recognise it: a three-phase kVA rating to amps always includes 1.732 and the voltage of the winding you are asking about. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,081.7 A - forgot 1.732; 360.6 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 1,873.6 A - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has L = 0.5 H and C = 20 microfarads. What is its resonant frequency, to the nearest hertz?","o":["316 Hz","15,915 Hz","50 Hz","25 Hz"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: f = 1 / (2 pi sqrt(LC)). LC = 0.5 x 2.00e-05 = 1.000e-05; sqrt = 0.00316; f = 1 / (6.2832 x 0.00316) = 50.3 Hz. Recognise it: at resonance XL = XC, so set the two reactance formulas equal and solve for f. Why the other choices are wrong: 316 Hz - left out the 2 pi (gave radians per second); 15,915 Hz - forgot the square root; 25 Hz - halved the result.","r":"Resonance"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A 2,400 W, 120 V single-phase resistive load is installed. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["11.5 A","20.0 A","10.0 A","25.0 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = P / E (resistive, PF = 1.0). Substitute: I = 2,400 / 120 = 20.00 A, about 20.0 A. Recognise it: watts and volts known, single-phase, so divide watts by volts. Only use 1.732 for three-phase. Why the other choices are wrong: 25.0 A - assumed an 0.8 power factor for a resistive load; 10.0 A - divided by the wrong voltage; 11.5 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase load.","r":"I = P / E"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"In a wye-connected system the line-to-neutral voltage is 277 V. What is the line-to-line voltage, to the nearest volt? (Use 1.732.)","o":["160 V","554 V","831 V","480 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: E(L-L) = 1.732 x E(L-N) = 1.732 x 277 = 479.8 V, about 480 V. Recognise it: moving from phase to line voltage in a wye multiplies by 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 160 V - divided by 1.732 instead of multiplying; 554 V - doubled it (single-phase thinking); 831 V - multiplied by 3.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"Motors","d":1,"q":"What happens to an induction motor's torque if the rotor runs at synchronous speed?","o":["It would double","It would be at maximum","It would be zero, because there would be no slip to induce rotor current","It would be equal to locked-rotor torque"],"a":2,"e":"Rotor current is induced only when the rotor cuts flux lines, i.e. when there is slip. At zero slip, there is no induced current and no torque. That is why an induction motor always turns slower than the field.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a single-phase circuit of 12 AWG copper (6,530 CM), carrying 16 A over 120 ft one way? Use K = 12.9. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["6.6 V","3.8 V","7.6 V","15.2 V"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: VD = 2 x K x I x L / CM = 2 x 12.9 x 16 x 120 / 6,530 = 49,536.0 / 6,530 = 7.59 V. Recognise it: single-phase, K given, one-way length given; the 2 accounts for current going out and back. Why the other choices are wrong: 3.8 V - left out the 2 for the round trip; 6.6 V - used 1.732 (the three-phase multiplier); 15.2 V - doubled the length twice.","r":"VD = 2KIL/CM"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 2 HP, 230 V single-phase motor runs at 85 percent efficiency and 0.8 power factor. What is the running current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["6.5 A","8.1 A","9.5 A","5.5 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = (HP x 746) / (E x eff x PF) = 1,492 / (230 x 0.85 x 0.8) = 1,492 / 156.4 = 9.54 A. Recognise it: single-phase, so no 1.732; efficiency and PF both reduce how much work each amp does. Why the other choices are wrong: 5.5 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase motor; 6.5 A - ignored efficiency and PF; 8.1 A - left out efficiency.","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A series circuit has 20 ohms resistance and 15 ohms inductive reactance. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["5.0 ohms","25.0 ohms","35.0 ohms","8.6 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Resistance and reactance are 90 degrees apart, so they add as a right triangle: Z = square root of (R squared + X squared) = sqrt(20^2 + 15^2) = sqrt(625) = 25.00 ohms. Recognise it: R plus XL (or XC) in series means impedance by the Pythagorean theorem. Why the other choices are wrong: 35.0 ohms - added R and X arithmetically; 5.0 ohms - subtracted R and X; 8.6 ohms - used the parallel resistor formula.","r":"Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2)"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A conductor has a 90 C ampacity of 50 A. It is bundled with 4 current-carrying conductors in one conduit. Using the adjustment factor (80 percent for 4 conductors), what is the adjusted ampacity, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["62.5 A","10.0 A","40.0 A","50.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"Adjusted ampacity = 50 x 0.8 = 40.0 A. Recognise it: 4-6 conductors gets 80 percent, 7-9 gets 70 percent, 10-20 gets 50 percent; multiply the table ampacity by that factor. Why the other choices are wrong: 50.0 A - did not apply any adjustment; 62.5 A - divided by the factor instead of multiplying; 10.0 A - multiplied by the percent removed.","r":"Table 310.15(C)(1)"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"In a series RL circuit, the voltage drops across R and L are 30 V and 40 V. What is the source voltage?","o":["10 V","70 V","50 V","35 V"],"a":2,"e":"R and L voltages are 90 degrees apart so they add as a right triangle: sqrt(30 squared + 40 squared) = sqrt(2,500) = 50 V. Adding them directly (70 V) is the common mistake; AC voltage drops in R and X do not add arithmetically.","r":"Phasor addition"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 120 V AC source feeds a series circuit of 9 ohms resistance and 12 ohms inductive reactance. What is the current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["5.7 A","13.3 A","10.0 A","8.0 A"],"a":3,"e":"Impedance Z = sqrt(9^2 + 12^2) = 15.00 ohms. Current I = E / Z = 120 / 15.00 = 8.00 A. Recognise it: Ohm's law for AC uses impedance instead of resistance; find Z first. Why the other choices are wrong: 5.7 A - added R and X directly; 13.3 A - ignored the reactance; 10.0 A - ignored the resistance.","r":"I = E / Z"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"What does RMS value of an AC voltage represent?","o":["The highest instantaneous value of the wave","The average of the positive half-cycle","The voltage measured at zero crossing","The DC voltage that would produce the same heating effect in a resistance"],"a":3,"e":"RMS (root mean square) is the effective value. 120 V AC heats a resistor like 120 V DC would. Peak is 1.414 x RMS and the half-cycle average is 0.637 x peak.","r":"RMS"},{"c":"Transformers","d":3,"q":"A 500 kVA, 480 V three-phase transformer has 5 percent impedance. Assuming an infinite primary source, what is the approximate secondary short-circuit current, to the nearest 10 A? (Use 1.732.)","o":["20,830 A","600 A","12,030 A","3,010 A"],"a":2,"e":"Step 1: FLA = 500,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 601.4 A. Step 2: Isc = FLA / (%Z / 100) = 601.4 / 0.0500 = 12028 A. Recognise it: a short-circuit-current question that gives %Z; the lower the impedance, the higher the fault current. Why the other choices are wrong: 3,010 A - multiplied FLA by the percent instead of dividing; 600 A - gave the full-load current; 20,830 A - forgot 1.732 when finding FLA.","r":"Transformer fault current"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 75 kVA three-phase load operates at 208 V. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["120.2 A","360.6 A","208.2 A","624.5 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / (1.732 x E) = 75,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 75,000 / 360.3 = 208.19 A. Recognise it: kVA given for a three-phase system, find amps, so convert to VA and divide by 1.732 x line voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 360.6 A - forgot 1.732; 624.5 A - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing; 120.2 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"I = kVA x 1000 / (1.732 E)"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"What is the resistance of 500 ft of 12 AWG copper (6,530 CM) using R = K x L / CM with K = 12.9? Round to the nearest hundredth of an ohm.","o":["1.98 ohms","1.01 ohms","0.49 ohms","0.99 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: R = K x L / CM = 12.9 x 500 / 6,530 = 0.988 ohms. Recognise it: resistance of one conductor from its length and area; the question asked for one conductor, so no round-trip doubling. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.98 ohms - doubled it for a round trip; 1.01 ohms - inverted the formula; 0.49 ohms - halved it.","r":"R = KL/CM"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"What is the full-load current on the 208 V side of a 45 kVA three-phase transformer, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["72.1 A","216.3 A","124.9 A","374.7 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / (1.732 x E) = 45,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 124.91 A. Recognise it: a three-phase kVA rating to amps always includes 1.732 and the voltage of the winding you are asking about. Why the other choices are wrong: 216.3 A - forgot 1.732; 72.1 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 374.7 A - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A load is rated 150 kVA at 0.95 power factor. How many kW of real power does it use, to the nearest tenth?","o":["150.0 kW","142.5 kW","46.8 kW","157.9 kW"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: kW = kVA x PF = 150 x 0.95 = 142.50 kW. Recognise it: real power is always the smaller of kW and kVA; multiply kVA by PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 157.9 kW - divided kVA by PF; 150.0 kW - assumed unity power factor; 46.8 kW - calculated kVAR.","r":"kW = kVA x PF"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 90 degrees C to degrees F, to the nearest degree.","o":["162 F","194 F","220 F","32 F"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: F = (C x 1.8) + 32 = (90 x 1.8) + 32 = 162 + 32 = 194 F. Recognise it: multiply by 1.8 first, then add 32. Why the other choices are wrong: 162 F - forgot to add 32; 220 F - added 32 before multiplying; 32 F - used the Fahrenheit-to-Celsius formula.","r":"F = 1.8 C + 32"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A single-phase circuit uses 6 AWG copper (0.491 ohms per 1,000 ft) for 250 ft one way and carries 50 A. How much power is lost in the two conductors, to the nearest watt?","o":["3,069 W","614 W","307 W","12 W"],"a":1,"e":"Total conductor length = 2 x 250 = 500 ft, so R = 0.491 x 500 / 1,000 = 0.245 ohms. Loss = I squared x R = 50 x 50 x 0.245 = 613.8 W. Recognise it: heat in the wiring is I squared R using the round-trip resistance. Why the other choices are wrong: 307 W - counted one conductor only; 12 W - did not square the current; 3,069 W - mis-scaled the length.","r":"P = I^2 R"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A motor nameplate lists full-load current of 18 A. A clamp meter reads 12 A while the motor drives its load. The most likely explanation is","o":["the clamp is on the neutral","the supply voltage is too high by 50 percent","the motor is overloaded","the motor is lightly loaded (below full load)"],"a":3,"e":"Motor current is proportional to the mechanical load. 12 A is two-thirds of the nameplate, so the load is below rated. An overload would read above 18 A.","r":"Motor loading"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a single-phase circuit of 6 AWG copper (26,240 CM), carrying 55 A over 250 ft one way? Use K = 12.9. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["6.8 V","27.0 V","11.7 V","13.5 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: VD = 2 x K x I x L / CM = 2 x 12.9 x 55 x 250 / 26,240 = 354,750.0 / 26,240 = 13.52 V. Recognise it: single-phase, K given, one-way length given; the 2 accounts for current going out and back. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.8 V - left out the 2 for the round trip; 11.7 V - used 1.732 (the three-phase multiplier); 27.0 V - doubled the length twice.","r":"VD = 2KIL/CM"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"A wye system measures 480 V line-to-line. What is the line-to-neutral voltage, to the nearest volt? (Use 1.732.)","o":["480 V","240 V","831 V","277 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: E(L-N) = E(L-L) / 1.732 = 480 / 1.732 = 277.1 V, about 277 V. Recognise it: only a wye has a neutral; the phase voltage is the line voltage divided by 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 831 V - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing; 240 V - halved the line voltage; 480 V - assumed line-to-neutral equals line-to-line.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A motor winding insulation system's life is most directly shortened by","o":["operating above its rated temperature","operating below nameplate current","running at the correct voltage","using a higher power factor"],"a":0,"e":"Insulation life roughly halves for each 10 degrees C above its rated temperature. Heat is the main enemy of motors, which is why overload protection is required.","r":"Motor insulation"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":2,"q":"The best protection against arc flash hazard is to","o":["de-energize the equipment whenever possible","work faster","reduce the voltage to 50 V by hand","wear regular cotton work clothes"],"a":0,"e":"Eliminating the energy source eliminates the hazard. PPE is the last line of defense. NFPA 70E requires an energized work permit for exposed energized work.","r":"Arc flash"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"What is the smallest copper conductor (single-phase, K = 12.9) that limits the voltage drop to 5 percent of 120 V for 12 A over 400 ft one way? Use Chapter 9 Table 8 circular mils.","o":["6 AWG","4 AWG","8 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Allowed drop = 120 x 0.05 = 6.00 V. Required CM = 2 x K x I x L / VD = 2 x 12.9 x 12 x 400 / 6.00 = 20,640 CM. The smallest size with at least that is 6 AWG (26,240 CM). Recognise it: 'what size' for a voltage-drop limit means rearrange to CM = 2KIL / VD and pick the next size up from Table 8. Why the other choices are wrong: 8 AWG - next smaller size is below the required circular mils; 10 AWG - forgot the 2 for the round trip; 4 AWG - meets the limit but is not the smallest size that does.","r":"CM = 2KIL/VD"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A single-phase resistive heater draws 16.0 A at 120 V. How many watts does it use?","o":["1,920 W","8 W","30,720 W","136 W"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: P = E x I. Substitute: P = 120 x 16.0 = 1,920 W. Recognise it: volts and amps given, watts wanted, so multiply (power factor is 1.0 for a resistive heater). Why the other choices are wrong: 8 W - divided E / I (that is ohms); 30,720 W - squared the current as well; 136 W - added volts and amps.","r":"P = E x I"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A bank of lamps totals 2,400 W and burns 10 hours. At $0.12 per kWh, what does the run cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$0.29","$2,880.00","$200.00","$2.88"],"a":3,"e":"Convert watts to kilowatts: 2,400 / 1,000 = 2.4 kW. kWh = 2.4 x 10 = 24. Cost = 24 x $0.12 = $2.88. Recognise it: any watts-and-hours problem needs the /1,000 step before the rate is applied. Why the other choices are wrong: $2,880.00 - forgot to convert watts to kilowatts; $0.29 - left out the hours; $200.00 - divided by the rate.","r":"kWh cost"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"A solid round conductor is 128 mils in diameter. What is its area in circular mils?","o":["256 CM","51,472 CM","16,384 CM","12,868 CM"],"a":2,"e":"One circular mil is the area of a circle 1 mil in diameter, so CM = d squared = 128 x 128 = 16,384 CM. Recognise it: circular mil area is simply the diameter in mils, squared; no pi is needed. Why the other choices are wrong: 256 CM - doubled the diameter; 12,868 CM - used the square-mil area formula (pi d squared / 4); 51,472 CM - multiplied by pi.","r":"Circular mil"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A motor delivers 1 HP at 1,140 RPM. What is the shaft torque, to the nearest tenth of a lb-ft? (Torque = HP x 5,252 / RPM)","o":["0.7 lb-ft","4.6 lb-ft","0.2 lb-ft","2.3 lb-ft"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: T = HP x 5,252 / RPM = 1 x 5,252 / 1,140 = 5,252 / 1,140 = 4.61 lb-ft. Recognise it: horsepower and speed known, torque wanted; for the same HP, a slower motor makes more torque. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.7 lb-ft - used watts per HP in place of 5,252; 0.2 lb-ft - multiplied by RPM instead of dividing; 2.3 lb-ft - halved the result.","r":"Torque"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A 4,500 W, 240 V single-phase resistive load is installed. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["10.8 A","23.4 A","37.5 A","18.8 A"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: I = P / E (resistive, PF = 1.0). Substitute: I = 4,500 / 240 = 18.75 A, about 18.8 A. Recognise it: watts and volts known, single-phase, so divide watts by volts. Only use 1.732 for three-phase. Why the other choices are wrong: 23.4 A - assumed an 0.8 power factor for a resistive load; 37.5 A - divided by the wrong voltage; 10.8 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase load.","r":"I = P / E"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load has a power factor of 0.8. What is the phase angle between voltage and current, to the nearest degree?","o":["72 degrees","37 degrees","39 degrees","53 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"PF = cos(angle), so angle = arccos(0.8) = 36.9 degrees. Recognise it: power factor is the cosine of the phase angle; use the inverse cosine key. Why the other choices are wrong: 53 degrees - used the inverse sine instead of inverse cosine; 39 degrees - used the inverse tangent; 72 degrees - scaled the PF to 90 degrees.","r":"PF = cos(theta)"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A 20.0 A current flows through a 0.6 ohm resistance. What is the voltage drop across it, to the nearest tenth of a volt?","o":["12.0 V","20.6 V","24.0 V","240.0 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: E = I x R. Substitute: E = 20.0 x 0.6 = 12.00 V, about 12.0 V. Recognise it: current and resistance known, find the drop, so multiply. Why the other choices are wrong: 240.0 V - squared the current (that is watts); 24.0 V - doubled it for a round trip when only one resistance was given; 20.6 V - added current and resistance.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"What happens to total resistance when another load is added in parallel?","o":["It doubles","It increases","It stays the same","It decreases"],"a":3,"e":"Each added branch gives current another path, so total resistance falls and total current rises. Example: 20 ohms alone; add another 20 ohms in parallel and Rt = 10 ohms. This is why overloads happen when too many loads are plugged in.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has L = 0.25 H and C = 40 microfarads. What is its resonant frequency, to the nearest hertz?","o":["25 Hz","316 Hz","15,915 Hz","50 Hz"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: f = 1 / (2 pi sqrt(LC)). LC = 0.25 x 4.00e-05 = 1.000e-05; sqrt = 0.00316; f = 1 / (6.2832 x 0.00316) = 50.3 Hz. Recognise it: at resonance XL = XC, so set the two reactance formulas equal and solve for f. Why the other choices are wrong: 316 Hz - left out the 2 pi (gave radians per second); 15,915 Hz - forgot the square root; 25 Hz - halved the result.","r":"Resonance"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a single-phase circuit of 12 AWG copper (6,530 CM), carrying 20 A over 60 ft one way? Use K = 12.9. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["9.5 V","4.7 V","2.4 V","4.1 V"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: VD = 2 x K x I x L / CM = 2 x 12.9 x 20 x 60 / 6,530 = 30,960.0 / 6,530 = 4.74 V. Recognise it: single-phase, K given, one-way length given; the 2 accounts for current going out and back. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.4 V - left out the 2 for the round trip; 4.1 V - used 1.732 (the three-phase multiplier); 9.5 V - doubled the length twice.","r":"VD = 2KIL/CM"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 86 degrees F to degrees C, to the nearest degree.","o":["30 C","54 C","16 C","187 C"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: C = (F - 32) / 1.8 = (86 - 32) / 1.8 = 54 / 1.8 = 30.0 C. Recognise it: subtract 32 first, then divide by 1.8 (multiply by 5/9). Why the other choices are wrong: 54 C - subtracted 32 but did not divide by 1.8; 16 C - divided before subtracting 32; 187 C - used the Celsius-to-Fahrenheit formula.","r":"C = (F - 32) / 1.8"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has L = 0.05 H and C = 200 microfarads. What is its resonant frequency, to the nearest hertz?","o":["50 Hz","15,915 Hz","316 Hz","25 Hz"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: f = 1 / (2 pi sqrt(LC)). LC = 0.05 x 2.00e-04 = 1.000e-05; sqrt = 0.00316; f = 1 / (6.2832 x 0.00316) = 50.3 Hz. Recognise it: at resonance XL = XC, so set the two reactance formulas equal and solve for f. Why the other choices are wrong: 316 Hz - left out the 2 pi (gave radians per second); 15,915 Hz - forgot the square root; 25 Hz - halved the result.","r":"Resonance"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 208 V AC source feeds a series circuit of 24 ohms resistance and 7 ohms inductive reactance. What is the current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["8.3 A","29.7 A","8.7 A","6.7 A"],"a":0,"e":"Impedance Z = sqrt(24^2 + 7^2) = 25.00 ohms. Current I = E / Z = 208 / 25.00 = 8.32 A. Recognise it: Ohm's law for AC uses impedance instead of resistance; find Z first. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.7 A - added R and X directly; 8.7 A - ignored the reactance; 29.7 A - ignored the resistance.","r":"I = E / Z"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"In a parallel circuit, which resistor dissipates the most power?","o":["The one with the lowest resistance","All dissipate the same power","The one with the highest resistance","The one closest to the source"],"a":0,"e":"In parallel each branch sees the same voltage, so P = E squared / R is greatest for the smallest R. Example: on 120 V, 10 ohms uses 1,440 W but 100 ohms uses 144 W. Series circuits are the opposite because current is common.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A copper conductor measures 2.0 ohms at 20 C. Using a temperature coefficient of 0.00393 per C, what is its resistance at 60 C, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["2.31 ohms","1.69 ohms","2.47 ohms","0.31 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: R2 = R1 x [1 + alpha x (T2 - T1)] = 2.0 x [1 + 0.00393 x (60 - 20)] = 2.0 x 1.1572 = 2.314 ohms. Recognise it: copper resistance rises with temperature; use the change in temperature, not the final temperature. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.69 ohms - subtracted the temperature rise (resistance falls); 2.47 ohms - used the final temperature instead of the change; 0.31 ohms - left out the original resistance term.","r":"Resistance vs temperature"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire system with a stated voltage of 480Y/277 V has a line-to-line voltage of","o":["831 V","240 V","480 V","277 V"],"a":2,"e":"The first number in 480Y/277 is the line-to-line voltage and the second the line-to-neutral. 277 x 1.732 = 480.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"The peak of a sine wave is 170 V. What is the RMS value, to the nearest volt?","o":["120 V","108 V","85 V","240 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: Vrms = 0.707 x Vpeak = 0.707 x 170 = 120.2 V. Recognise it: RMS is the DC-equivalent heating value and is always lower than peak. Why the other choices are wrong: 240 V - multiplied by 1.414 instead of 0.707; 108 V - used the average value factor; 85 V - halved the peak.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A transformer has 400 primary turns and 200 secondary turns. With 480 V applied to the primary, what is the secondary voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["240 V","416 V","960 V","160 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: Es = Ep x (Ns / Np) = 480 x (200 / 400) = 240.0 V. Recognise it: voltage follows turns; the winding with more turns has the higher voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 960 V - inverted the turns ratio; 160 V - divided by the sum of the turns; 416 V - added a 1.732 factor to a single-phase turns calculation.","r":"Turns ratio"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A meter reads 208 V RMS on an AC circuit. What is the peak voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["147 V","294 V","132 V","416 V"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: Vpeak = 1.414 x Vrms = 1.414 x 208 = 294.1 V. Recognise it: the sine wave peaks higher than its RMS (heating) value; peak is 1.414 times RMS. Why the other choices are wrong: 147 V - multiplied by 0.707 (that converts peak to RMS); 132 V - used the average factor; 416 V - doubled the RMS value.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 5 HP, 230 V single-phase motor runs at 88 percent efficiency and 0.85 power factor. What is the running current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["12.5 A","19.1 A","16.2 A","21.7 A"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: I = (HP x 746) / (E x eff x PF) = 3,730 / (230 x 0.88 x 0.85) = 3,730 / 172.0 = 21.68 A. Recognise it: single-phase, so no 1.732; efficiency and PF both reduce how much work each amp does. Why the other choices are wrong: 12.5 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase motor; 16.2 A - ignored efficiency and PF; 19.1 A - left out efficiency.","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A diode in an AC circuit","o":["allows current in one direction only","stores charge","allows current in both directions equally","steps up voltage"],"a":0,"e":"A diode conducts when forward-biased and blocks in reverse, so it rectifies AC to pulsing DC. A bridge of four diodes gives full-wave rectification.","r":"Diodes"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A motor delivers 25 HP at 1,180 RPM. What is the shaft torque, to the nearest tenth of a lb-ft? (Torque = HP x 5,252 / RPM)","o":["5.6 lb-ft","55.6 lb-ft","111.3 lb-ft","15.8 lb-ft"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: T = HP x 5,252 / RPM = 25 x 5,252 / 1,180 = 131,300 / 1,180 = 111.27 lb-ft. Recognise it: horsepower and speed known, torque wanted; for the same HP, a slower motor makes more torque. Why the other choices are wrong: 15.8 lb-ft - used watts per HP in place of 5,252; 5.6 lb-ft - multiplied by RPM instead of dividing; 55.6 lb-ft - halved the result.","r":"Torque"},{"c":"Kirchhoff's laws","d":1,"q":"A 120 V source feeds a series circuit with three loads. Two drops measure 30 V and 45 V. What is the drop across the third load?","o":["45 V","195 V","75 V","90 V"],"a":0,"e":"Kirchhoff's voltage law: the drops around a series loop equal the source. Third drop = 120 - 30 - 45 = 45 V. Recognise it: a series loop with one unknown voltage; subtract the known drops from the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 195 V - added everything; 90 V - subtracted only one drop; 75 V - reported the sum of the known drops.","r":"Kirchhoff's voltage law"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":1,"q":"A dwelling calculated load totals 42,000 VA on a 120/240 V single-phase service. What is the calculated service current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["201.9 A","101.0 A","175.0 A","350.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = VA / E = 42,000 / 240 = 175.00 A. Recognise it: a 120/240 V single-phase service is sized on 240 V; divide the total VA by 240. Why the other choices are wrong: 350.0 A - divided by 120 V instead of 240 V; 101.0 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase service; 201.9 A - divided by 208 V.","r":"I = VA / E"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"A three-phase panel supplies 200 A per phase at 480 V. What is the apparent power, to the nearest tenth of a kVA? (Use 1.732.)","o":["166,272 kVA","166.3 kVA","96.0 kVA","288.0 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: kVA = 1.732 x E x I / 1,000 = 1.732 x 480 x 200 / 1,000 = 166.27 kVA. Recognise it: volts and amps, three-phase, no PF mentioned, so apparent power is wanted. Why the other choices are wrong: 96.0 kVA - forgot 1.732; 288.0 kVA - multiplied by 3 instead of 1.732; 166,272 kVA - forgot to divide by 1,000.","r":"kVA = 1.732 E I / 1000"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A load is rated 100 kVA at 0.9 power factor. How many kW of real power does it use, to the nearest tenth?","o":["100.0 kW","90.0 kW","111.1 kW","43.6 kW"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: kW = kVA x PF = 100 x 0.9 = 90.00 kW. Recognise it: real power is always the smaller of kW and kVA; multiply kVA by PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 111.1 kW - divided kVA by PF; 100.0 kW - assumed unity power factor; 43.6 kW - calculated kVAR.","r":"kW = kVA x PF"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":3,"q":"Three 8 ohm resistors are connected in delta on a 208 V three-phase supply. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["15.0 A","26.0 A","45.0 A","78.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"In a delta each resistor sees the full line voltage. Phase current = 208 / 8 = 26.00 A. Line current = 1.732 x 26.00 = 45.03 A. Recognise it: delta elements see line voltage, and the line carries 1.732 times the phase current. Why the other choices are wrong: 26.0 A - reported the phase current as the line current; 15.0 A - used wye voltage across a delta element; 78.0 A - multiplied by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Grounding and bonding theory","d":2,"q":"Bonding of metal parts is done to","o":["increase their resistance to ground","ensure electrical continuity and create a low-impedance path for fault current","block lightning completely","isolate parts from each other"],"a":1,"e":"Bonding keeps metal parts at the same potential and ensures that a fault trips the OCPD. Isolated parts might become energized with no path to clear.","r":"Bonding"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"A solid round conductor is 100 mils in diameter. What is its area in circular mils?","o":["10,000 CM","200 CM","7,854 CM","31,416 CM"],"a":0,"e":"One circular mil is the area of a circle 1 mil in diameter, so CM = d squared = 100 x 100 = 10,000 CM. Recognise it: circular mil area is simply the diameter in mils, squared; no pi is needed. Why the other choices are wrong: 200 CM - doubled the diameter; 7,854 CM - used the square-mil area formula (pi d squared / 4); 31,416 CM - multiplied by pi.","r":"Circular mil"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"What is the result of operating an induction motor at significantly below its rated voltage under full load?","o":["Greater torque","Lower current and cooler operation","Higher current and overheating","Higher speed"],"a":2,"e":"The load needs the same power; with less voltage the motor draws more current, I = P / E. Torque falls with the square of voltage, and slip increases.","r":"Motor undervoltage"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A 120 V source is connected across a 24 ohm resistive load. What is the current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["5.0 A","2.5 A","8.7 A","0.2 A"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: I = E / R. Substitute: I = 120 / 24 = 5.00 A, which rounds to 5.0 A. Recognise it: you know voltage and resistance and want current, so put the unknown on top of the Ohm's law triangle and divide the other two. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.2 A - inverted the formula (R / E); 8.7 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase calculation; 2.5 A - used twice the resistance.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"The peak of a sine wave is 390 V. What is the RMS value, to the nearest volt?","o":["276 V","195 V","248 V","551 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: Vrms = 0.707 x Vpeak = 0.707 x 390 = 275.7 V. Recognise it: RMS is the DC-equivalent heating value and is always lower than peak. Why the other choices are wrong: 551 V - multiplied by 1.414 instead of 0.707; 248 V - used the average value factor; 195 V - halved the peak.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 60 degrees C to degrees F, to the nearest degree.","o":["16 F","166 F","140 F","108 F"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: F = (C x 1.8) + 32 = (60 x 1.8) + 32 = 108 + 32 = 140 F. Recognise it: multiply by 1.8 first, then add 32. Why the other choices are wrong: 108 F - forgot to add 32; 166 F - added 32 before multiplying; 16 F - used the Fahrenheit-to-Celsius formula.","r":"F = 1.8 C + 32"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A transformer works only on","o":["direct current","alternating current (changing magnetic flux)","any steady voltage","frequencies of zero Hz"],"a":1,"e":"Induction needs changing flux. Connect a transformer primary to DC and, after the initial inrush, only the low winding resistance limits current, so it overheats and burns out.","r":"Transformer principle"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"In a purely capacitive AC circuit, the current","o":["is zero at all times","lags the voltage by 90 degrees","leads the voltage by 90 degrees","is in phase with the voltage"],"a":2,"e":"ICE: in a capacitor, current (I) leads voltage (E). A capacitor must charge before its voltage rises, so current flows first. This is why capacitors can offset the lagging current of motors.","r":"ELI the ICE man"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"Resistors of 50, 100 and 25 ohms are connected in parallel. What is the total resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["58.3 ohms","14.3 ohms","16.7 ohms","175.0 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: 1/Rt = 1/50 + 1/100 + 1/25 = 0.0700. Rt = 1 / 0.0700 = 14.29 ohms. Recognise it: three branches across one source; answer must be below the smallest branch (25 ohms) - use that as a check. Why the other choices are wrong: 175.0 ohms - added the values (series); 58.3 ohms - took the average; 16.7 ohms - paralleled only the two smallest resistors.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"What is the equivalent resistance of 8 ohm and 24 ohm resistors in parallel, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["16.0 ohms","6.0 ohms","32.0 ohms","4.0 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: Rt = (R1 x R2) / (R1 + R2) = (8 x 24) / (8 + 24) = 192 / 32 = 6.00 ohms. Recognise it: two resistors across the same two points is parallel; the answer must be less than the smaller resistor (8 ohms). Why the other choices are wrong: 32.0 ohms - added them as a series circuit; 16.0 ohms - averaged the two values; 4.0 ohms - halved the smaller resistor.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"The NEC table full-load current for a motor is 14 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the branch-circuit conductors for this single continuous-duty motor (125 percent), to the nearest tenth?","o":["35.0 A","16.1 A","14.0 A","17.5 A"],"a":3,"e":"Conductor ampacity = 14 x 1.25 = 17.50 A. Recognise it: a conductor-sizing question for one motor uses 125 percent of table FLC; overload and short-circuit devices use different percentages. Why the other choices are wrong: 14.0 A - used 100 percent of FLC; 16.1 A - used the 115 percent overload factor; 35.0 A - used the 250 percent short-circuit factor.","r":"430.22"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"What is the resistance of 300 ft of 8 AWG copper (16,510 CM) using R = K x L / CM with K = 12.9? Round to the nearest hundredth of an ohm.","o":["0.12 ohms","4.27 ohms","0.47 ohms","0.23 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: R = K x L / CM = 12.9 x 300 / 16,510 = 0.234 ohms. Recognise it: resistance of one conductor from its length and area; the question asked for one conductor, so no round-trip doubling. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.47 ohms - doubled it for a round trip; 4.27 ohms - inverted the formula; 0.12 ohms - halved it.","r":"R = KL/CM"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"The peak of a sine wave is 294 V. What is the RMS value, to the nearest volt?","o":["187 V","416 V","147 V","208 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: Vrms = 0.707 x Vpeak = 0.707 x 294 = 207.9 V. Recognise it: RMS is the DC-equivalent heating value and is always lower than peak. Why the other choices are wrong: 416 V - multiplied by 1.414 instead of 0.707; 187 V - used the average value factor; 147 V - halved the peak.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"Three resistors of 30, 30 and 40 ohms are connected in series across 208 V. What is the circuit current, to the nearest hundredth of an amp?","o":["6.24 A","6.93 A","2.08 A","19.07 A"],"a":2,"e":"In series, Rt = R1 + R2 + R3 = 30 + 30 + 40 = 100 ohms. Then I = E / Rt = 208 / 100 = 2.080 A, about 2.08 A. Recognise it: one path for the current, so resistances add and the same current flows everywhere. Why the other choices are wrong: 19.07 A - added the resistors as if they were in parallel; 6.93 A - used only the first resistor; 6.24 A - used the average resistance.","r":"Series circuit"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"In a wye-connected system the line-to-neutral voltage is 347 V. What is the line-to-line voltage, to the nearest volt? (Use 1.732.)","o":["601 V","1,041 V","200 V","694 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: E(L-L) = 1.732 x E(L-N) = 1.732 x 347 = 601.0 V, about 601 V. Recognise it: moving from phase to line voltage in a wye multiplies by 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 200 V - divided by 1.732 instead of multiplying; 694 V - doubled it (single-phase thinking); 1,041 V - multiplied by 3.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":3,"q":"A 60 V source feeds a 5 ohm resistor in series with a parallel pair of 15 ohm and 10 ohm. What current flows in the 15 ohm resistor, to the nearest hundredth of an amp?","o":["4.00 A","2.18 A","2.73 A","5.45 A"],"a":1,"e":"Rt = 5 + (15 x 10) / (15 + 10) = 11.00 ohms. Total I = 60 / 11.00 = 5.455 A. Drop in series resistor = 5.455 x 5 = 27.27 V, so the parallel pair sees 60 - 27.27 = 32.73 V. Branch current = 32.73 / 15 = 2.182 A. Recognise it: find Rt, then total current, then voltage across the parallel section, then the branch current. Why the other choices are wrong: 5.45 A - used the total current instead of the branch current; 4.00 A - put the full source voltage across the branch; 2.73 A - split the current equally between unequal branches.","r":"Combination circuit"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":1,"q":"A dwelling calculated load totals 36,000 VA on a 120/240 V single-phase service. What is the calculated service current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["300.0 A","150.0 A","173.1 A","86.6 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = VA / E = 36,000 / 240 = 150.00 A. Recognise it: a 120/240 V single-phase service is sized on 240 V; divide the total VA by 240. Why the other choices are wrong: 300.0 A - divided by 120 V instead of 240 V; 86.6 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase service; 173.1 A - divided by 208 V.","r":"I = VA / E"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"What is the resistance of 750 ft of 10 AWG copper (10,380 CM) using R = K x L / CM with K = 12.9? Round to the nearest hundredth of an ohm.","o":["1.07 ohms","0.93 ohms","0.47 ohms","1.86 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: R = K x L / CM = 12.9 x 750 / 10,380 = 0.932 ohms. Recognise it: resistance of one conductor from its length and area; the question asked for one conductor, so no round-trip doubling. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.86 ohms - doubled it for a round trip; 1.07 ohms - inverted the formula; 0.47 ohms - halved it.","r":"R = KL/CM"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"A conductor has 0.6 ohms of resistance. A new conductor of the same material is 5 times as long and has 2 times the cross-sectional area. What is the new resistance, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["0.24 ohms","6.00 ohms","1.50 ohms","3.00 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Resistance is proportional to length and inversely proportional to area: R2 = R1 x (5 / 2) = 0.6 x 2.500 = 1.500 ohms. Recognise it: more length raises resistance; more area lowers it. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.00 ohms - multiplied by the area factor instead of dividing; 0.24 ohms - divided by length and multiplied by area; 3.00 ohms - ignored the change in area.","r":"R = KL/A"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A circuit draws 7.5 A from a 120 V supply. What is the load resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["16.0 ohms","112.5 ohms","900.0 ohms","8.0 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: R = E / I. Substitute: R = 120 / 7.5 = 16.00 ohms, or 16.0 ohms rounded. Recognise it: voltage and current known, resistance wanted, so divide E by I. Why the other choices are wrong: 8.0 ohms - used twice the current; 900.0 ohms - multiplied E x I, which is watts, not ohms; 112.5 ohms - subtracted current from voltage.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 3 HP, 230 V single-phase motor runs at 84 percent efficiency and 0.82 power factor. What is the running current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["11.9 A","14.1 A","9.7 A","8.2 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = (HP x 746) / (E x eff x PF) = 2,238 / (230 x 0.84 x 0.82) = 2,238 / 158.4 = 14.13 A. Recognise it: single-phase, so no 1.732; efficiency and PF both reduce how much work each amp does. Why the other choices are wrong: 8.2 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase motor; 9.7 A - ignored efficiency and PF; 11.9 A - left out efficiency.","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"How long can a 6 AWG copper (26,240 CM) single-phase circuit carrying 50 A on 240 V run (one way) before the drop exceeds 5 percent? Use K = 12.9 and round to the nearest foot.","o":["282 ft","488 ft","244 ft","122 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Allowed drop = 240 x 0.05 = 12.00 V. Rearranged: L = VD x CM / (2 x K x I) = 12.00 x 26,240 / (2 x 12.9 x 50) = 244.1 ft. Recognise it: a maximum-distance question; solve the voltage-drop formula for L. Why the other choices are wrong: 488 ft - left out the 2 in the formula; 122 ft - applied the round-trip factor twice; 282 ft - used 1.732 for a single-phase run.","r":"L = VD x CM / 2KI"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"Wattage is a measure of","o":["the force pushing electrons through a conductor","the opposition to current flow","the total quantity of charge","the rate at which work is done or energy is used"],"a":3,"e":"A watt is one joule per second - power. Voltage is the push, resistance is the opposition, and charge is in coulombs. P = E x I ties them together.","r":"Electrical theory"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"A 120/208 V three-phase, 4-wire system is a","o":["delta system","single-phase system","wye system","high-leg delta system"],"a":2,"e":"120 V line-to-neutral and 208 V line-to-line (120 x 1.732 = 208) come from a wye. A 4-wire delta with a center tap has 120/240 V and a 208 V high leg.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A 1 HP motor is 70 percent efficient. How many watts does it draw at full load, to the nearest watt?","o":["746 W","522 W","1,066 W","1,429 W"],"a":2,"e":"Output watts = 1 x 746 = 746 W. Input = output / efficiency = 746 / 0.70 = 1,065.7 W. Recognise it: a motor always draws more than it delivers; divide output by efficiency. Why the other choices are wrong: 746 W - ignored efficiency (that is output watts); 522 W - multiplied by efficiency instead of dividing; 1,429 W - used 1,000 W per HP.","r":"HP = 746 W; efficiency"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"If a round conductor's diameter is increased by a factor of 3, by what factor does its circular mil area (and the cross-sectional area) increase?","o":["6 times","3 times","9 times","27 times"],"a":2,"e":"Area = d squared, so multiplying the diameter by 3 multiplies the area by 3 x 3 = 9. Recognise it: area scales with the square of the diameter, so resistance falls to 1/9 for the same length. Why the other choices are wrong: 3 times - assumed area is proportional to diameter; 6 times - doubled the diameter factor; 27 times - cubed the diameter factor.","r":"Circular mil"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"If a round conductor's diameter is increased by a factor of 4, by what factor does its circular mil area (and the cross-sectional area) increase?","o":["16 times","64 times","4 times","8 times"],"a":0,"e":"Area = d squared, so multiplying the diameter by 4 multiplies the area by 4 x 4 = 16. Recognise it: area scales with the square of the diameter, so resistance falls to 1/16 for the same length. Why the other choices are wrong: 4 times - assumed area is proportional to diameter; 8 times - doubled the diameter factor; 64 times - cubed the diameter factor.","r":"Circular mil"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 10 HP motor at 90 percent efficiency runs 8 hours at full load. Electricity costs $0.12 per kWh. What is the cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$7.96","$6.45","$7.16","$9.60"],"a":0,"e":"Input kW = 10 x 0.746 / 0.90 = 8.289 kW. kWh = 8.289 x 8 = 66.31. Cost = 66.31 x 0.12 = $7.96. Recognise it: convert HP to kW, divide by efficiency to get input, then multiply by hours and rate. Why the other choices are wrong: $7.16 - ignored efficiency; $6.45 - multiplied by efficiency instead of dividing; $9.60 - used HP as if it were kW.","r":"HP and kWh"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A 75 kVA transformer is supplying 60 kVA of load. What percent of its rating is in use, to the nearest percent?","o":["0.8 percent","20 percent","80 percent","125 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Percent loading = load / rating x 100 = 60 / 75 x 100 = 80.0 percent. Recognise it: compare actual load to nameplate; a result over 100 percent means overload. Why the other choices are wrong: 125 percent - inverted the ratio; 20 percent - reported the spare capacity; 0.8 percent - forgot to multiply by 100.","r":"Transformer loading"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"Why is the 2 used in the single-phase voltage drop formula?","o":["There are two grounded conductors","It accounts for 80 percent loading","The voltage is two times the line current","Current travels the length of the circuit out and back"],"a":3,"e":"A single-phase circuit has an outgoing conductor and a return conductor, so the effective length is 2 x L. Three-phase balanced uses 1.732 because of the vector sum.","r":"VD = 2KIL/CM"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"Which material has the lowest resistivity of those listed?","o":["Aluminum","Copper","Nichrome","Iron"],"a":1,"e":"Silver is best but expensive; copper is the common lowest-resistivity conductor in wiring. Aluminum is a bit higher, and iron and nichrome (heating element) are far higher.","r":"Resistivity"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A 2.4 kW load runs 10 hours per day for 5 days. At $0.16 per kWh, what is the cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$3.84","$19.20","$19,200.00","$750.00"],"a":1,"e":"Energy = kW x hours = 2.4 x 10 x 5 = 120 kWh. Cost = kWh x rate = 120 x 0.16 = $19.20. Recognise it: kilowatt-hours are power times time; multiply by the rate last. Why the other choices are wrong: $3.84 - calculated one day only; $19,200.00 - used watts instead of kilowatts; $750.00 - divided by the rate instead of multiplying.","r":"kWh cost"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A storage-type water heater is rated 3,000 W at 240 V. Treating it as a continuous load (125 percent), what minimum branch-circuit ampacity is required, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["9.0 A","12.5 A","10.0 A","15.6 A"],"a":3,"e":"Current = 3,000 / 240 = 12.50 A. Branch circuit = 125 percent x 12.50 = 15.62 A. Recognise it: a continuous-load multiplier of 1.25 applies after you convert watts to amps. Why the other choices are wrong: 12.5 A - left out the 125 percent; 10.0 A - multiplied by 80 percent instead of 125 percent; 9.0 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase heater.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":1,"q":"A circuit breaker differs from a fuse in that it","o":["can be reset after tripping","must be replaced after every trip","never opens on short circuits","works only on DC"],"a":0,"e":"A breaker uses a thermal-magnetic or electronic trip mechanism that can be reset after the fault is cleared. Fuses are one-time devices (renewable-link fuses aside).","r":"Overcurrent protection"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"The purpose of a start capacitor on a single-phase motor is to","o":["convert AC to DC","reduce the motor's horsepower","create a phase shift in the starting winding to produce starting torque","increase the motor's synchronous speed"],"a":2,"e":"The capacitor shifts current in the start winding about 90 degrees from the main winding, creating a rotating field. A run capacitor stays in circuit to improve running PF.","r":"Capacitors"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A load uses 27 kW of true power and 30 kVA of apparent power. What is its power factor, to two decimals?","o":["3.00","1.11","0.90","0.44"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: PF = kW / kVA = 27 / 30 = 0.900, or 0.90. Recognise it: true power over apparent power; the answer can never exceed 1.0. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.11 - inverted the ratio; 3.00 - subtracted instead of dividing; 0.44 - reported the sine of the angle (reactive ratio).","r":"PF = kW / kVA"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"If resistance stays the same and voltage is doubled, what happens to current?","o":["It is cut in half","It doubles","It quadruples","It stays the same"],"a":1,"e":"I = E / R. With R fixed, current is directly proportional to voltage. 120 V across 10 ohms = 12 A; 240 V across 10 ohms = 24 A. Quadrupling is what happens to POWER, not current.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A single-phase motor draws 16 A at 115 V with a power factor of 0.75. How many watts of real power is it using, to the nearest watt?","o":["2,453 W","1,380 W","1,840 W","2,390 W"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: P = E x I x PF = 115 x 16 x 0.75 = 1,380 W. Recognise it: a motor or any inductive load has PF below 1, and real watts equal volt-amps times PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,840 W - left out power factor (that is VA); 2,453 W - divided by power factor; 2,390 W - applied 1.732 to a single-phase load.","r":"P = E I PF"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"3 identical 72 ohm lamps are connected in parallel. What is the combined resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["72.0 ohms","24.0 ohms","216.0 ohms","108.0 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"For n equal resistors in parallel, Rt = R / n = 72 / 3 = 24.00 ohms. Recognise it: several identical loads on the same two conductors; each added branch lowers total resistance and raises total current. Why the other choices are wrong: 216.0 ohms - multiplied (series connection); 72.0 ohms - assumed parallel does not change resistance; 108.0 ohms - halved the series total.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"A conductor has 1.2 ohms of resistance. A new conductor of the same material is 4 times as long and has 2 times the cross-sectional area. What is the new resistance, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["2.40 ohms","4.80 ohms","0.60 ohms","9.60 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Resistance is proportional to length and inversely proportional to area: R2 = R1 x (4 / 2) = 1.2 x 2.000 = 2.400 ohms. Recognise it: more length raises resistance; more area lowers it. Why the other choices are wrong: 9.60 ohms - multiplied by the area factor instead of dividing; 0.60 ohms - divided by length and multiplied by area; 4.80 ohms - ignored the change in area.","r":"R = KL/A"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"The resistance of a conductor increases when","o":["its length decreases","its cross-section increases","its length increases or its cross-section decreases","its voltage is reduced"],"a":2,"e":"R = K x L / CM: directly proportional to length, inversely proportional to area. Voltage has no effect on a conductor's resistance.","r":"R = KL/CM"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"A single-phase 120 V circuit uses 12 AWG copper (6,530 CM, K = 12.9) to carry 16 A for 100 ft one way. What is the percent voltage drop, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","o":["5.3 percent","5.6 percent","2.6 percent","6.3 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Step 1: VD = 2 x 12.9 x 16 x 100 / 6,530 = 6.32 V. Step 2: % = VD / E x 100 = 6.32 / 120 x 100 = 5.27 percent. Recognise it: 'percent' means calculate volts of drop first, then divide by the source voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.6 percent - left out the 2 in 2KIL/CM; 6.3 percent - reported volts as a percent; 5.6 percent - divided by the load-end voltage instead of the source.","r":"Percent voltage drop"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"In a wye connection, the line current is","o":["0.577 times the phase current","3 times the phase current","equal to the phase current","1.732 times the phase current"],"a":2,"e":"In a wye the line conductor is in series with its phase winding, so the currents are the same. The 1.732 applies to line voltage.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A series circuit has 30 ohms resistance and 40 ohms inductive reactance. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["10.0 ohms","17.1 ohms","70.0 ohms","50.0 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Resistance and reactance are 90 degrees apart, so they add as a right triangle: Z = square root of (R squared + X squared) = sqrt(30^2 + 40^2) = sqrt(2500) = 50.00 ohms. Recognise it: R plus XL (or XC) in series means impedance by the Pythagorean theorem. Why the other choices are wrong: 70.0 ohms - added R and X arithmetically; 10.0 ohms - subtracted R and X; 17.1 ohms - used the parallel resistor formula.","r":"Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2)"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"When frequency increases, inductive reactance","o":["decreases","stays the same","becomes zero","increases"],"a":3,"e":"XL = 2 pi f L, directly proportional to frequency. At DC (0 Hz) an inductor has no reactance; at higher frequency it opposes current more. Capacitive reactance moves the opposite way.","r":"XL = 2 pi f L"},{"c":"Kirchhoff's laws","d":1,"q":"At a junction, 20 A and 8 A flow in and 18 A flows out on one conductor. A fourth conductor also leaves the junction. What current flows in the fourth conductor?","o":["6 A","12 A","10 A","46 A"],"a":2,"e":"Kirchhoff's current law: current in = current out. In = 20 + 8 = 28 A. Out = 18 + I4, so I4 = 28 - 18 = 10 A. Recognise it: any problem about currents meeting at a point; add everything entering and everything leaving and set them equal. Why the other choices are wrong: 46 A - added all three currents; 6 A - mixed up which currents enter and leave; 12 A - ignored the third current.","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"5 identical 40 ohm lamps are connected in parallel. What is the combined resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["8.0 ohms","40.0 ohms","200.0 ohms","100.0 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"For n equal resistors in parallel, Rt = R / n = 40 / 5 = 8.00 ohms. Recognise it: several identical loads on the same two conductors; each added branch lowers total resistance and raises total current. Why the other choices are wrong: 200.0 ohms - multiplied (series connection); 40.0 ohms - assumed parallel does not change resistance; 100.0 ohms - halved the series total.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Kirchhoff's laws","d":1,"q":"A 24 V source feeds a series circuit with three loads. Two drops measure 6 V and 9 V. What is the drop across the third load?","o":["9 V","39 V","15 V","18 V"],"a":0,"e":"Kirchhoff's voltage law: the drops around a series loop equal the source. Third drop = 24 - 6 - 9 = 9 V. Recognise it: a series loop with one unknown voltage; subtract the known drops from the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 39 V - added everything; 18 V - subtracted only one drop; 15 V - reported the sum of the known drops.","r":"Kirchhoff's voltage law"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"What is the full-load secondary current of a 10 kVA single-phase transformer with a 120 V secondary, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["83.3 A","48.1 A","104.2 A","1.2 A"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / E = 10,000 / 120 = 83.33 A. Recognise it: single-phase transformer rating to amps is VA divided by volts, no 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 48.1 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase transformer; 104.2 A - assumed an 0.8 power factor; 1.2 A - multiplied instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A single-phase resistive heater draws 12.5 A at 120 V. How many watts does it use?","o":["18,750 W","1,500 W","10 W","132 W"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: P = E x I. Substitute: P = 120 x 12.5 = 1,500 W. Recognise it: volts and amps given, watts wanted, so multiply (power factor is 1.0 for a resistive heater). Why the other choices are wrong: 10 W - divided E / I (that is ohms); 18,750 W - squared the current as well; 132 W - added volts and amps.","r":"P = E x I"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A resistor with bands of red, violet, brown, gold has a value of","o":["27 ohms","270 ohms, plus or minus 5 percent","2,700 ohms","720 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Red = 2, violet = 7, brown multiplier = x10, so 27 x 10 = 270 ohms. Gold tolerance is 5 percent. Misreading the multiplier gives 27 or 2,700.","r":"Resistor color code"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A bank of lamps totals 1,500 W and burns 8 hours. At $0.10 per kWh, what does the run cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$0.15","$120.00","$1.20","$1,200.00"],"a":2,"e":"Convert watts to kilowatts: 1,500 / 1,000 = 1.5 kW. kWh = 1.5 x 8 = 12. Cost = 12 x $0.10 = $1.20. Recognise it: any watts-and-hours problem needs the /1,000 step before the rate is applied. Why the other choices are wrong: $1,200.00 - forgot to convert watts to kilowatts; $0.15 - left out the hours; $120.00 - divided by the rate.","r":"kWh cost"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A single-phase resistive heater draws 12.0 A at 240 V. How many watts does it use?","o":["20 W","252 W","34,560 W","2,880 W"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: P = E x I. Substitute: P = 240 x 12.0 = 2,880 W. Recognise it: volts and amps given, watts wanted, so multiply (power factor is 1.0 for a resistive heater). Why the other choices are wrong: 20 W - divided E / I (that is ohms); 34,560 W - squared the current as well; 252 W - added volts and amps.","r":"P = E x I"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A single-phase circuit uses 12 AWG copper (1.98 ohms per 1,000 ft) for 125 ft one way and carries 20 A. How much power is lost in the two conductors, to the nearest watt?","o":["10 W","99 W","198 W","990 W"],"a":2,"e":"Total conductor length = 2 x 125 = 250 ft, so R = 1.98 x 250 / 1,000 = 0.495 ohms. Loss = I squared x R = 20 x 20 x 0.495 = 198.0 W. Recognise it: heat in the wiring is I squared R using the round-trip resistance. Why the other choices are wrong: 99 W - counted one conductor only; 10 W - did not square the current; 990 W - mis-scaled the length.","r":"P = I^2 R"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"A single-phase 240 V circuit uses 10 AWG copper (10,380 CM, K = 12.9) to carry 24 A for 120 ft one way. What is the percent voltage drop, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","o":["3.1 percent","7.2 percent","1.5 percent","3.0 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Step 1: VD = 2 x 12.9 x 24 x 120 / 10,380 = 7.16 V. Step 2: % = VD / E x 100 = 7.16 / 240 x 100 = 2.98 percent. Recognise it: 'percent' means calculate volts of drop first, then divide by the source voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.5 percent - left out the 2 in 2KIL/CM; 7.2 percent - reported volts as a percent; 3.1 percent - divided by the load-end voltage instead of the source.","r":"Percent voltage drop"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"In a balanced delta system, the line current is","o":["equal to the phase current","1.732 times the phase current","one-third the phase current","half the phase current"],"a":1,"e":"Each line current is the vector sum of two phase currents, giving 1.732 x I(phase). In a wye, line current equals phase current.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A single-phase circuit uses 10 AWG copper (1.24 ohms per 1,000 ft) for 150 ft one way and carries 24 A. How much power is lost in the two conductors, to the nearest watt?","o":["1,071 W","9 W","214 W","107 W"],"a":2,"e":"Total conductor length = 2 x 150 = 300 ft, so R = 1.24 x 300 / 1,000 = 0.372 ohms. Loss = I squared x R = 24 x 24 x 0.372 = 214.3 W. Recognise it: heat in the wiring is I squared R using the round-trip resistance. Why the other choices are wrong: 107 W - counted one conductor only; 9 W - did not square the current; 1,071 W - mis-scaled the length.","r":"P = I^2 R"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":1,"q":"A fuse protects a circuit by","o":["reducing the voltage","increasing resistance in the circuit","storing excess current","melting when current exceeds its rating for a time"],"a":3,"e":"The fusible link heats by I squared R and melts, opening the circuit. Time-delay (dual-element) fuses tolerate short motor starting surges.","r":"Overcurrent protection"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A 6 microfarad and a 12 microfarad capacitor are connected in series. What is the total capacitance, to the nearest tenth of a microfarad?","o":["6.0 microfarads","9.0 microfarads","18.0 microfarads","4.0 microfarads"],"a":3,"e":"Capacitors in series combine like resistors in parallel: Ct = (C1 x C2) / (C1 + C2) = (6 x 12) / (6 + 12) = 4.00 microfarads. Recognise it: capacitors are the opposite of resistors - parallel adds, series is the reciprocal formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 18.0 microfarads - added them (that is the parallel result); 9.0 microfarads - averaged the values; 6.0 microfarads - assumed the smaller capacitor alone governs.","r":"Capacitors in series"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A balanced three-phase load draws 120 A at 208 V line-to-line with a power factor of 0.85. How many kilowatts is it using, to the nearest tenth? (Use 1.732.)","o":["21.2 kW","63.6 kW","36.7 kW","43.2 kW"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: P = 1.732 x E x I x PF = 1.732 x 208 x 120 x 0.85 = 36,746 W = 36.75 kW. Recognise it: a three-phase load with a stated PF asks for true power; include 1.732 and PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 21.2 kW - forgot 1.732; 43.2 kW - left out power factor (that is kVA); 63.6 kW - used 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"P = 1.732 E I PF"},{"c":"Grounding and bonding theory","d":2,"q":"A 120 V ground fault through the earth only, with a ground resistance of 25 ohms, would produce a current of about","o":["120 A","25 A","4.8 A, which will not trip a 20 A breaker","48 A"],"a":2,"e":"I = 120 / 25 = 4.8 A. Below the breaker rating, so the fault would persist with dangerous voltage on the metal. A metallic EGC path has a very low impedance and makes the breaker trip.","r":"Fault current path"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A delta-connected load draws 52 A in each line. What is the current in each phase winding, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["30.0 A","90.1 A","52.0 A","17.3 A"],"a":0,"e":"Delta: I(phase) = I(line) / 1.732 = 52 / 1.732 = 30.02 A. Recognise it: phase current is the smaller number in a delta; line current is 1.732 times larger. Why the other choices are wrong: 52.0 A - assumed phase current equals line current; 90.1 A - multiplied by 1.732; 17.3 A - divided by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A copper conductor measures 0.5 ohms at 20 C. Using a temperature coefficient of 0.00393 per C, what is its resistance at 90 C, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["0.68 ohms","0.14 ohms","0.36 ohms","0.64 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: R2 = R1 x [1 + alpha x (T2 - T1)] = 0.5 x [1 + 0.00393 x (90 - 20)] = 0.5 x 1.2751 = 0.638 ohms. Recognise it: copper resistance rises with temperature; use the change in temperature, not the final temperature. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.36 ohms - subtracted the temperature rise (resistance falls); 0.68 ohms - used the final temperature instead of the change; 0.14 ohms - left out the original resistance term.","r":"Resistance vs temperature"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":1,"q":"Which change will reduce voltage drop on a circuit?","o":["Using a larger conductor","Increasing the length of the run","Using a smaller conductor","Increasing the load current"],"a":0,"e":"VD = 2KIL/CM. More circular mils lowers VD. Longer length or higher current raises it.","r":"VD = 2KIL/CM"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"What is the time for one complete cycle of a 25 Hz supply, in milliseconds to the nearest hundredth?","o":["6.37 ms","80.00 ms","20.00 ms","40.00 ms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: T = 1 / f = 1 / 25 = 0.04000 s = 40.00 ms. Recognise it: period is the inverse of frequency; at 60 Hz there are 60 cycles per second. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.37 ms - used 1/(2 pi f), the angular relationship; 80.00 ms - doubled the period; 20.00 ms - reported a half-cycle.","r":"T = 1/f"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load has a power factor of 0.5. What is the phase angle between voltage and current, to the nearest degree?","o":["30 degrees","27 degrees","45 degrees","60 degrees"],"a":3,"e":"PF = cos(angle), so angle = arccos(0.5) = 60.0 degrees. Recognise it: power factor is the cosine of the phase angle; use the inverse cosine key. Why the other choices are wrong: 30 degrees - used the inverse sine instead of inverse cosine; 27 degrees - used the inverse tangent; 45 degrees - scaled the PF to 90 degrees.","r":"PF = cos(theta)"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A delta-connected load draws 30 A in each line. What is the current in each phase winding, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["10.0 A","30.0 A","52.0 A","17.3 A"],"a":3,"e":"Delta: I(phase) = I(line) / 1.732 = 30 / 1.732 = 17.32 A. Recognise it: phase current is the smaller number in a delta; line current is 1.732 times larger. Why the other choices are wrong: 30.0 A - assumed phase current equals line current; 52.0 A - multiplied by 1.732; 10.0 A - divided by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the inductive reactance of a 1.0 H coil at 60 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["377.0 ohms","188.5 ohms","754.0 ohms","60.0 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: XL = 2 x pi x f x L = 6.2832 x 60 x 1.0 = 376.99 ohms. Recognise it: an inductor opposes current more as frequency rises; multiply 2 pi f by henries. Why the other choices are wrong: 754.0 ohms - doubled the 2 pi f L result; 188.5 ohms - left out the 2; 60.0 ohms - multiplied only f x L (left out 2 pi).","r":"XL = 2 pi f L"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 200 kW load operates at 0.75 PF. How many kVAR of capacitors are needed to raise the power factor to 0.95, to the nearest kVAR?","o":["176 kVAR","50 kVAR","111 kVAR","40 kVAR"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: kVAR(cap) = kW x (tan of old angle - tan of new angle). Old angle = arccos(0.75) = 41.4 deg, tan = 0.882. New angle = arccos(0.95) = 18.2 deg, tan = 0.329. kVAR = 200 x (0.882 - 0.329) = 110.6. Recognise it: capacitors supply reactive power; subtract the reactive demand before and after correction. Why the other choices are wrong: 50 kVAR - used kW x (1 - PF); 40 kVAR - multiplied by the PF difference; 176 kVAR - removed all reactive power regardless of the target.","r":"PF correction"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 6-pole, 60 Hz induction motor has 4 percent slip at full load. What is the rotor speed, to the nearest RPM?","o":["1,200 RPM","1,196 RPM","1,248 RPM","1,152 RPM"],"a":3,"e":"Ns = 120 x 60 / 6 = 1,200 RPM. Rotor speed = Ns x (1 - slip) = 1,200 x 0.960 = 1152.0 RPM. Recognise it: an induction rotor always turns slower than the field, so reduce Ns by the slip fraction. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,248 RPM - added slip to synchronous speed; 1,196 RPM - subtracted the percent as RPM; 1,200 RPM - gave synchronous speed.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A 30 kVA transformer is supplying 22.5 kVA of load. What percent of its rating is in use, to the nearest percent?","o":["133 percent","75 percent","25 percent","0.75 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Percent loading = load / rating x 100 = 22.5 / 30 x 100 = 75.0 percent. Recognise it: compare actual load to nameplate; a result over 100 percent means overload. Why the other choices are wrong: 133 percent - inverted the ratio; 25 percent - reported the spare capacity; 0.75 percent - forgot to multiply by 100.","r":"Transformer loading"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the capacitive reactance of a 200 microfarad capacitor at 50 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["31.8 ohms","15.9 ohms","100.0 ohms","0.016 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: XC = 1 / (2 x pi x f x C), with C in farads (200 microfarads = 2.00e-04 F). XC = 1 / (6.2832 x 50 x 2.00e-04) = 15.92 ohms. Recognise it: capacitors oppose current less as frequency rises; convert microfarads to farads first. Why the other choices are wrong: 100.0 ohms - left the 2 pi out of the denominator; 0.016 ohms - treated microfarads as millifarads; 31.8 ohms - used pi instead of 2 pi.","r":"XC = 1/(2 pi f C)"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":1,"q":"A continuity test is performed to determine","o":["if there is a complete, unbroken conductive path","the power factor","the voltage of the circuit","whether current is AC or DC"],"a":0,"e":"Continuity checks (with the circuit de-energized) show if a conductor, fuse or switch is open. A low-resistance reading means continuity.","r":"Continuity"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"A wye system measures 240 V line-to-line. What is the line-to-neutral voltage, to the nearest volt? (Use 1.732.)","o":["416 V","120 V","240 V","139 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: E(L-N) = E(L-L) / 1.732 = 240 / 1.732 = 138.6 V, about 139 V. Recognise it: only a wye has a neutral; the phase voltage is the line voltage divided by 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 416 V - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing; 120 V - halved the line voltage; 240 V - assumed line-to-neutral equals line-to-line.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":3,"q":"A 240 V source feeds a 20 ohm resistor in series with a parallel pair of 30 ohm and 60 ohm. What current flows in the 30 ohm resistor, to the nearest hundredth of an amp?","o":["6.00 A","8.00 A","3.00 A","4.00 A"],"a":3,"e":"Rt = 20 + (30 x 60) / (30 + 60) = 40.00 ohms. Total I = 240 / 40.00 = 6.000 A. Drop in series resistor = 6.000 x 20 = 120.00 V, so the parallel pair sees 240 - 120.00 = 120.00 V. Branch current = 120.00 / 30 = 4.000 A. Recognise it: find Rt, then total current, then voltage across the parallel section, then the branch current. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.00 A - used the total current instead of the branch current; 8.00 A - put the full source voltage across the branch; 3.00 A - split the current equally between unequal branches.","r":"Combination circuit"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":3,"q":"A 48 V source feeds a 6 ohm resistor in series with a parallel pair of 12 ohm and 4 ohm. What current flows in the 12 ohm resistor, to the nearest hundredth of an amp?","o":["1.33 A","4.00 A","2.67 A","5.33 A"],"a":0,"e":"Rt = 6 + (12 x 4) / (12 + 4) = 9.00 ohms. Total I = 48 / 9.00 = 5.333 A. Drop in series resistor = 5.333 x 6 = 32.00 V, so the parallel pair sees 48 - 32.00 = 16.00 V. Branch current = 16.00 / 12 = 1.333 A. Recognise it: find Rt, then total current, then voltage across the parallel section, then the branch current. Why the other choices are wrong: 5.33 A - used the total current instead of the branch current; 4.00 A - put the full source voltage across the branch; 2.67 A - split the current equally between unequal branches.","r":"Combination circuit"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"A feeder conductor has 0.18 ohms of total resistance and carries 35 A. How much power is lost as heat in the conductor, to the nearest watt?","o":["221 W","6,806 W","6 W","13 W"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: P = I squared x R. Substitute: P = 35 x 35 x 0.18 = 220.5 W, rounded 221 W. Recognise it: current and conductor resistance known, wanted is heat loss, so use I squared R. Doubling the current quadruples the loss. Why the other choices are wrong: 6 W - did not square the current (I x R is volts); 6,806 W - divided I squared by R; 13 W - doubled I x R instead of squaring I.","r":"P = I^2 x R"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"The NEC table full-load current for a motor is 65 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the branch-circuit conductors for this single continuous-duty motor (125 percent), to the nearest tenth?","o":["81.3 A","162.5 A","65.0 A","74.8 A"],"a":0,"e":"Conductor ampacity = 65 x 1.25 = 81.25 A. Recognise it: a conductor-sizing question for one motor uses 125 percent of table FLC; overload and short-circuit devices use different percentages. Why the other choices are wrong: 65.0 A - used 100 percent of FLC; 74.8 A - used the 115 percent overload factor; 162.5 A - used the 250 percent short-circuit factor.","r":"430.22"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A circuit serves 40 A of continuous load and 20 A of noncontinuous load. What minimum overcurrent device ampacity is needed before rounding to a standard size? (Continuous load at 125 percent.)","o":["75 A","65 A","60 A","70 A"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: 125 percent of continuous + 100 percent of noncontinuous = 40 x 1.25 + 20 = 50 + 20 = 70 A. Recognise it: lights, signs and anything on 3 hours or more is continuous; only that part gets the 125 percent. Why the other choices are wrong: 60 A - added the loads without the 125 percent factor; 75 A - applied 125 percent to the noncontinuous load too; 65 A - applied 125 percent to the wrong load.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"If the voltage across a fixed resistance is doubled, what happens to the power?","o":["It doubles","It increases four times","It is cut in half","It stays the same"],"a":1,"e":"P = E squared / R. Doubling E squares to 4, so power quadruples. Example: 120 V across 14.4 ohms = 1,000 W; 240 V across 14.4 ohms = 4,000 W. Recognise it: any time voltage or current is squared in the formula, a change of x gives x squared.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"What is the full-load secondary current of a 5 kVA single-phase transformer with a 240 V secondary, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["1.2 A","20.8 A","12.0 A","26.0 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / E = 5,000 / 240 = 20.83 A. Recognise it: single-phase transformer rating to amps is VA divided by volts, no 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 12.0 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase transformer; 26.0 A - assumed an 0.8 power factor; 1.2 A - multiplied instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"A wye system measures 416 V line-to-line. What is the line-to-neutral voltage, to the nearest volt? (Use 1.732.)","o":["416 V","240 V","721 V","208 V"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: E(L-N) = E(L-L) / 1.732 = 416 / 1.732 = 240.2 V, about 240 V. Recognise it: only a wye has a neutral; the phase voltage is the line voltage divided by 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 721 V - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing; 208 V - halved the line voltage; 416 V - assumed line-to-neutral equals line-to-line.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"An ideal transformer steps 480 V down to 208 V. If the primary current is 8 A, what is the secondary current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["10.7 A","3.5 A","8.0 A","18.5 A"],"a":3,"e":"Ideal transformer: VA in = VA out, so Ep x Ip = Es x Is. Is = Ip x Ep / Es = 8 x 480 / 208 = 18.46 A. Recognise it: step-down means more current on the low-voltage side, in exact inverse proportion. Why the other choices are wrong: 3.5 A - used the voltage ratio the wrong way round; 8.0 A - assumed the currents are equal; 10.7 A - divided by 1.732 on a single-phase unit.","r":"Transformer current ratio"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies three motors with table full-load currents of 14 A, 11 A and 7.6 A. What is the minimum feeder conductor ampacity (125 percent of the largest plus the others), to the nearest tenth?","o":["32.6 A","37.3 A","40.8 A","36.1 A"],"a":3,"e":"Largest FLC is 14 A: 14 x 1.25 = 17.5. Add the others: 11 + 7.6. Total = 36.10 A. Recognise it: several motors on one feeder; only the largest motor gets the extra 25 percent. Why the other choices are wrong: 40.8 A - applied 125 percent to all motors; 32.6 A - no 125 percent on the largest; 37.3 A - applied 125 percent to the wrong motors.","r":"430.24"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"A 3 percent voltage drop on a 120 V branch circuit is how many volts?","o":["4.0 V","3.6 V","0.36 V","36 V"],"a":1,"e":"120 x 0.03 = 3.6 V. The load then sees about 116.4 V. The informational note recommends no more than 3 percent on a branch circuit.","r":"Percent voltage drop"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 59 degrees F to degrees C, to the nearest degree.","o":["138 C","15 C","1 C","27 C"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: C = (F - 32) / 1.8 = (59 - 32) / 1.8 = 27 / 1.8 = 15.0 C. Recognise it: subtract 32 first, then divide by 1.8 (multiply by 5/9). Why the other choices are wrong: 27 C - subtracted 32 but did not divide by 1.8; 1 C - divided before subtracting 32; 138 C - used the Celsius-to-Fahrenheit formula.","r":"C = (F - 32) / 1.8"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the capacitive reactance of a 10 microfarad capacitor at 60 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["0.265 ohms","1,666.7 ohms","530.5 ohms","265.3 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: XC = 1 / (2 x pi x f x C), with C in farads (10 microfarads = 1.00e-05 F). XC = 1 / (6.2832 x 60 x 1.00e-05) = 265.26 ohms. Recognise it: capacitors oppose current less as frequency rises; convert microfarads to farads first. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,666.7 ohms - left the 2 pi out of the denominator; 0.265 ohms - treated microfarads as millifarads; 530.5 ohms - used pi instead of 2 pi.","r":"XC = 1/(2 pi f C)"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"Loose connections cause heat mainly because","o":["added contact resistance converts current into heat (I squared R)","the current increases","the voltage rises at the connection","the frequency changes"],"a":0,"e":"A high-resistance joint carries the full circuit current, so P = I squared R is dissipated at that spot. Even a fraction of an ohm at 20 A is tens of watts concentrated in a tiny area.","r":"I^2R heating"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":2,"q":"Which meter category rating is intended for measurements on the service entrance and feeders?","o":["No category is required","CAT II only","CAT III or CAT IV","CAT I"],"a":2,"e":"CAT ratings indicate the transient overvoltage a meter can withstand. CAT IV is at the service origin, CAT III is distribution (panels, feeders), and CAT II is plug-in loads.","r":"Meter categories"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A meter reads 120 V RMS on an AC circuit. What is the peak voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["170 V","85 V","240 V","76 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: Vpeak = 1.414 x Vrms = 1.414 x 120 = 169.7 V. Recognise it: the sine wave peaks higher than its RMS (heating) value; peak is 1.414 times RMS. Why the other choices are wrong: 85 V - multiplied by 0.707 (that converts peak to RMS); 76 V - used the average factor; 240 V - doubled the RMS value.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 150 kVA three-phase load operates at 480 V. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["180.4 A","104.2 A","312.5 A","541.3 A"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / (1.732 x E) = 150,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 150,000 / 831.4 = 180.43 A. Recognise it: kVA given for a three-phase system, find amps, so convert to VA and divide by 1.732 x line voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 312.5 A - forgot 1.732; 541.3 A - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing; 104.2 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"I = kVA x 1000 / (1.732 E)"},{"c":"Power","d":3,"q":"A heater is rated 2,000 W at 120 V. If it is connected to 125 V instead (resistance unchanged), what power does it produce, to the nearest watt?","o":["2,170 W","2,083 W","2,000 W","1,843 W"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: R = E1 squared / P1 stays fixed, so P2 = P1 x (E2 / E1) squared. Substitute: P2 = 2,000 x (125 / 120) squared = 2,000 x 1.0851 = 2,170.1 W. Recognise it: a fixed-resistance load at a different voltage; power follows the square of the voltage ratio. Why the other choices are wrong: 2,083 W - scaled power linearly with voltage; 1,843 W - inverted the voltage ratio; 2,000 W - assumed nameplate watts never change.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A conductor has a 90 C ampacity of 75 A. It is bundled with 9 current-carrying conductors in one conduit. Using the adjustment factor (70 percent for 9 conductors), what is the adjusted ampacity, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["75.0 A","107.1 A","52.5 A","22.5 A"],"a":2,"e":"Adjusted ampacity = 75 x 0.7 = 52.5 A. Recognise it: 4-6 conductors gets 80 percent, 7-9 gets 70 percent, 10-20 gets 50 percent; multiply the table ampacity by that factor. Why the other choices are wrong: 75.0 A - did not apply any adjustment; 107.1 A - divided by the factor instead of multiplying; 22.5 A - multiplied by the percent removed.","r":"Table 310.15(C)(1)"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a balanced three-phase circuit of 12 AWG copper (6,530 CM) carrying 20 A over 80 ft one way? Use K = 12.9 and 1.732. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["10.9 V","5.5 V","3.2 V","6.3 V"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: VD = 1.732 x K x I x L / CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 20 x 80 / 6,530 = 5.47 V. Recognise it: three-phase circuits replace the 2 with 1.732 because the phase currents partially cancel in the return. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.3 V - used 2 (single-phase) instead of 1.732; 3.2 V - used neither 2 nor 1.732; 10.9 V - doubled the length as well as using 1.732.","r":"VD = 1.732KIL/CM"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"Skin effect in AC conductors causes","o":["current to concentrate near the surface, increasing effective resistance in large conductors","resistance to decrease at high currents","current to flow only in the insulation","voltage to double at the surface"],"a":0,"e":"At 60 Hz the effect is small except in very large conductors, but it raises effective AC resistance. Chapter 9 Table 9 lists AC resistance and reactance.","r":"Skin effect"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A load is rated 30 kVA at 0.7 power factor. How many kW of real power does it use, to the nearest tenth?","o":["21.0 kW","42.9 kW","30.0 kW","21.4 kW"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: kW = kVA x PF = 30 x 0.7 = 21.00 kW. Recognise it: real power is always the smaller of kW and kVA; multiply kVA by PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 42.9 kW - divided kVA by PF; 30.0 kW - assumed unity power factor; 21.4 kW - calculated kVAR.","r":"kW = kVA x PF"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load draws 12 kW at 15 kVA. What is its reactive power, to the nearest tenth of a kVAR?","o":["27.0 kVAR","19.2 kVAR","9.0 kVAR","3.0 kVAR"],"a":2,"e":"The power triangle: kVA squared = kW squared + kVAR squared. kVAR = sqrt(15^2 - 12^2) = sqrt(81) = 9.00 kVAR. Recognise it: kVA is the hypotenuse, so the missing side comes from the difference of squares. Why the other choices are wrong: 3.0 kVAR - subtracted kW from kVA directly; 27.0 kVAR - added kW and kVA; 19.2 kVAR - added the squares instead of subtracting.","r":"Power triangle"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"A heater is rated 1,000 W at 120 V. What is its hot resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["28.8 ohms","7.2 ohms","14.4 ohms","8.3 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: R = E squared / P. Substitute: R = 120 x 120 / 1,000 = 14.40 ohms, about 14.4 ohms. Recognise it: nameplate watts and volts are given and resistance is wanted, so use R = E squared / P. Why the other choices are wrong: 7.2 ohms - halved the result; 8.3 ohms - used P / E, which is amps, not ohms; 28.8 ohms - doubled the result.","r":"R = E^2 / P"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":1,"q":"A voltmeter is connected","o":["in parallel with the circuit or component being measured","in series with the neutral only","only after the circuit is de-energized","in series with the load"],"a":0,"e":"Voltmeters have very high internal resistance and measure the potential difference across two points. Ammeters (in-line) are connected in series.","r":"Meter use"},{"c":"Transformers","d":3,"q":"A 150 kVA, 208 V three-phase transformer has 2 percent impedance. Assuming an infinite primary source, what is the approximate secondary short-circuit current, to the nearest 10 A? (Use 1.732.)","o":["36,060 A","830 A","420 A","20,820 A"],"a":3,"e":"Step 1: FLA = 150,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 416.4 A. Step 2: Isc = FLA / (%Z / 100) = 416.4 / 0.0200 = 20819 A. Recognise it: a short-circuit-current question that gives %Z; the lower the impedance, the higher the fault current. Why the other choices are wrong: 830 A - multiplied FLA by the percent instead of dividing; 420 A - gave the full-load current; 36,060 A - forgot 1.732 when finding FLA.","r":"Transformer fault current"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":2,"q":"A megohmmeter (megger) is used to","o":["measure energized voltage","check frequency","measure live current","measure insulation resistance on de-energized equipment"],"a":3,"e":"It applies a high DC voltage (such as 500 or 1,000 V) and measures leakage in megohms. The conductors must be de-energized and disconnected from electronics before testing.","r":"Insulation resistance"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A balanced three-phase load draws 30 A at 240 V line-to-line with a power factor of 0.95. How many kilowatts is it using, to the nearest tenth? (Use 1.732.)","o":["6.8 kW","20.5 kW","11.8 kW","12.5 kW"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: P = 1.732 x E x I x PF = 1.732 x 240 x 30 x 0.95 = 11,847 W = 11.85 kW. Recognise it: a three-phase load with a stated PF asks for true power; include 1.732 and PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.8 kW - forgot 1.732; 12.5 kW - left out power factor (that is kVA); 20.5 kW - used 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"P = 1.732 E I PF"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"What is the resistance of 200 ft of 14 AWG copper (4,110 CM) using R = K x L / CM with K = 12.9? Round to the nearest hundredth of an ohm.","o":["1.59 ohms","0.31 ohms","1.26 ohms","0.63 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: R = K x L / CM = 12.9 x 200 / 4,110 = 0.628 ohms. Recognise it: resistance of one conductor from its length and area; the question asked for one conductor, so no round-trip doubling. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.26 ohms - doubled it for a round trip; 1.59 ohms - inverted the formula; 0.31 ohms - halved it.","r":"R = KL/CM"},{"c":"Transformers","d":3,"q":"A 300 kVA, 480 V three-phase transformer has 4 percent impedance. Assuming an infinite primary source, what is the approximate secondary short-circuit current, to the nearest 10 A? (Use 1.732.)","o":["9,020 A","15,630 A","1,440 A","360 A"],"a":0,"e":"Step 1: FLA = 300,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 360.9 A. Step 2: Isc = FLA / (%Z / 100) = 360.9 / 0.0400 = 9021 A. Recognise it: a short-circuit-current question that gives %Z; the lower the impedance, the higher the fault current. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,440 A - multiplied FLA by the percent instead of dividing; 360 A - gave the full-load current; 15,630 A - forgot 1.732 when finding FLA.","r":"Transformer fault current"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load draws 48 kW at 60 kVA. What is its reactive power, to the nearest tenth of a kVAR?","o":["108.0 kVAR","12.0 kVAR","76.8 kVAR","36.0 kVAR"],"a":3,"e":"The power triangle: kVA squared = kW squared + kVAR squared. kVAR = sqrt(60^2 - 48^2) = sqrt(1296) = 36.00 kVAR. Recognise it: kVA is the hypotenuse, so the missing side comes from the difference of squares. Why the other choices are wrong: 12.0 kVAR - subtracted kW from kVA directly; 108.0 kVAR - added kW and kVA; 76.8 kVAR - added the squares instead of subtracting.","r":"Power triangle"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"In a series circuit of 10, 20 and 30 ohms across 120 V, what is the voltage drop across the 10 ohm resistor, to the nearest tenth of a volt?","o":["40.0 V","20.0 V","24.0 V","120.0 V"],"a":1,"e":"Rt = 10 + 20 + 30 = 60 ohms; I = 120 / 60 = 2.0000 A; drop = I x R = 2.0000 x 10 = 20.00 V. Shortcut: E x R / Rt. Recognise it: drops in a series circuit are proportional to resistance and add up to the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 120.0 V - assumed the full source voltage appears across one resistor; 40.0 V - split the voltage equally; 24.0 V - divided by the other resistors only instead of Rt.","r":"Voltage divider"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"A conductor has 1.0 ohms of resistance. A new conductor of the same material is 3 times as long and has 2 times the cross-sectional area. What is the new resistance, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["6.00 ohms","1.50 ohms","0.67 ohms","3.00 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Resistance is proportional to length and inversely proportional to area: R2 = R1 x (3 / 2) = 1.0 x 1.500 = 1.500 ohms. Recognise it: more length raises resistance; more area lowers it. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.00 ohms - multiplied by the area factor instead of dividing; 0.67 ohms - divided by length and multiplied by area; 3.00 ohms - ignored the change in area.","r":"R = KL/A"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"What is the main difference between an isolation transformer and an autotransformer?","o":["An isolation transformer has separate primary and secondary windings; an autotransformer shares one winding","An autotransformer has no core","An autotransformer always changes the frequency","An isolation transformer cannot change voltage"],"a":0,"e":"Separate windings provide galvanic isolation. In an autotransformer the primary and secondary are electrically connected (tapped from one winding), so there is no isolation.","r":"Transformer types"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 50 degrees C to degrees F, to the nearest degree.","o":["90 F","10 F","122 F","148 F"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: F = (C x 1.8) + 32 = (50 x 1.8) + 32 = 90 + 32 = 122 F. Recognise it: multiply by 1.8 first, then add 32. Why the other choices are wrong: 90 F - forgot to add 32; 148 F - added 32 before multiplying; 10 F - used the Fahrenheit-to-Celsius formula.","r":"F = 1.8 C + 32"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 22.5 kW three-phase resistive heater is connected to 240 V. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["54.1 A","162.4 A","93.8 A","31.3 A"],"a":0,"e":"Resistive, so PF = 1. I = W / (1.732 x E) = 22,500 / (1.732 x 240) = 54.13 A. Recognise it: kW and three-phase voltage, find amps, so divide by 1.732 x E. Why the other choices are wrong: 93.8 A - treated it as single-phase; 31.3 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 162.4 A - multiplied by 1.732.","r":"I = P / (1.732 E)"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A load uses 15 kW of true power and 20 kVA of apparent power. What is its power factor, to two decimals?","o":["0.75","5.00","1.33","0.66"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: PF = kW / kVA = 15 / 20 = 0.750, or 0.75. Recognise it: true power over apparent power; the answer can never exceed 1.0. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.33 - inverted the ratio; 5.00 - subtracted instead of dividing; 0.66 - reported the sine of the angle (reactive ratio).","r":"PF = kW / kVA"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"On a 120/240 V, 3-phase, 4-wire delta system, the high-leg conductor measures what to neutral?","o":["240 V","208 V","277 V","120 V"],"a":1,"e":"The high leg is 1.732 x 120 = 208 V to the neutral. It is identified by orange. 120 V is the other two phases to neutral and 240 V is the line-to-line voltage.","r":"High-leg delta"},{"c":"Power","d":3,"q":"A heater is rated 3,000 W at 240 V. If it is connected to 208 V instead (resistance unchanged), what power does it produce, to the nearest watt?","o":["2,253 W","3,000 W","2,600 W","3,994 W"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: R = E1 squared / P1 stays fixed, so P2 = P1 x (E2 / E1) squared. Substitute: P2 = 3,000 x (208 / 240) squared = 3,000 x 0.7511 = 2,253.3 W. Recognise it: a fixed-resistance load at a different voltage; power follows the square of the voltage ratio. Why the other choices are wrong: 2,600 W - scaled power linearly with voltage; 3,994 W - inverted the voltage ratio; 3,000 W - assumed nameplate watts never change.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A motor delivers 5 HP at 1,725 RPM. What is the shaft torque, to the nearest tenth of a lb-ft? (Torque = HP x 5,252 / RPM)","o":["15.2 lb-ft","1.6 lb-ft","2.2 lb-ft","7.6 lb-ft"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: T = HP x 5,252 / RPM = 5 x 5,252 / 1,725 = 26,260 / 1,725 = 15.22 lb-ft. Recognise it: horsepower and speed known, torque wanted; for the same HP, a slower motor makes more torque. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.2 lb-ft - used watts per HP in place of 5,252; 1.6 lb-ft - multiplied by RPM instead of dividing; 7.6 lb-ft - halved the result.","r":"Torque"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"The NEC table full-load current for a motor is 4.8 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the branch-circuit conductors for this single continuous-duty motor (125 percent), to the nearest tenth?","o":["6.0 A","4.8 A","5.5 A","12.0 A"],"a":0,"e":"Conductor ampacity = 4.8 x 1.25 = 6.00 A. Recognise it: a conductor-sizing question for one motor uses 125 percent of table FLC; overload and short-circuit devices use different percentages. Why the other choices are wrong: 4.8 A - used 100 percent of FLC; 5.5 A - used the 115 percent overload factor; 12.0 A - used the 250 percent short-circuit factor.","r":"430.22"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies three motors with table full-load currents of 28 A, 14 A and 10 A. What is the minimum feeder conductor ampacity (125 percent of the largest plus the others), to the nearest tenth?","o":["52.0 A","59.0 A","65.0 A","58.0 A"],"a":1,"e":"Largest FLC is 28 A: 28 x 1.25 = 35. Add the others: 14 + 10. Total = 59.00 A. Recognise it: several motors on one feeder; only the largest motor gets the extra 25 percent. Why the other choices are wrong: 65.0 A - applied 125 percent to all motors; 52.0 A - no 125 percent on the largest; 58.0 A - applied 125 percent to the wrong motors.","r":"430.24"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":3,"q":"Three 30 ohm resistors are connected in delta on a 480 V three-phase supply. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["48.0 A","16.0 A","9.2 A","27.7 A"],"a":3,"e":"In a delta each resistor sees the full line voltage. Phase current = 480 / 30 = 16.00 A. Line current = 1.732 x 16.00 = 27.71 A. Recognise it: delta elements see line voltage, and the line carries 1.732 times the phase current. Why the other choices are wrong: 16.0 A - reported the phase current as the line current; 9.2 A - used wye voltage across a delta element; 48.0 A - multiplied by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"In a series circuit of 30, 45 and 25 ohms across 200 V, what is the voltage drop across the 45 ohm resistor, to the nearest tenth of a volt?","o":["163.6 V","200.0 V","66.7 V","90.0 V"],"a":3,"e":"Rt = 30 + 45 + 25 = 100 ohms; I = 200 / 100 = 2.0000 A; drop = I x R = 2.0000 x 45 = 90.00 V. Shortcut: E x R / Rt. Recognise it: drops in a series circuit are proportional to resistance and add up to the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 200.0 V - assumed the full source voltage appears across one resistor; 66.7 V - split the voltage equally; 163.6 V - divided by the other resistors only instead of Rt.","r":"Voltage divider"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 80 kW load operates at 0.85 PF. How many kVAR of capacitors are needed to raise the power factor to 0.95, to the nearest kVAR?","o":["8 kVAR","23 kVAR","50 kVAR","12 kVAR"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: kVAR(cap) = kW x (tan of old angle - tan of new angle). Old angle = arccos(0.85) = 31.8 deg, tan = 0.620. New angle = arccos(0.95) = 18.2 deg, tan = 0.329. kVAR = 80 x (0.620 - 0.329) = 23.3. Recognise it: capacitors supply reactive power; subtract the reactive demand before and after correction. Why the other choices are wrong: 12 kVAR - used kW x (1 - PF); 8 kVAR - multiplied by the PF difference; 50 kVAR - removed all reactive power regardless of the target.","r":"PF correction"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A motor delivers 20 HP at 1,760 RPM. What is the shaft torque, to the nearest tenth of a lb-ft? (Torque = HP x 5,252 / RPM)","o":["29.8 lb-ft","8.5 lb-ft","6.7 lb-ft","59.7 lb-ft"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: T = HP x 5,252 / RPM = 20 x 5,252 / 1,760 = 105,040 / 1,760 = 59.68 lb-ft. Recognise it: horsepower and speed known, torque wanted; for the same HP, a slower motor makes more torque. Why the other choices are wrong: 8.5 lb-ft - used watts per HP in place of 5,252; 6.7 lb-ft - multiplied by RPM instead of dividing; 29.8 lb-ft - halved the result.","r":"Torque"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load has a power factor of 0.6. What is the phase angle between voltage and current, to the nearest degree?","o":["37 degrees","54 degrees","53 degrees","31 degrees"],"a":2,"e":"PF = cos(angle), so angle = arccos(0.6) = 53.1 degrees. Recognise it: power factor is the cosine of the phase angle; use the inverse cosine key. Why the other choices are wrong: 37 degrees - used the inverse sine instead of inverse cosine; 31 degrees - used the inverse tangent; 54 degrees - scaled the PF to 90 degrees.","r":"PF = cos(theta)"},{"c":"Grounding and bonding theory","d":3,"q":"If the neutral and equipment ground are improperly connected downstream of the service, the result is","o":["GFCI protection becomes more effective","the circuit operates more safely","normal neutral current flows on metal parts and the ground path, creating shock hazards","lower voltage drop on the conductor"],"a":2,"e":"Neutral current is diverted onto conduit and equipment frames, which will carry load current and can energize metal. GFCI devices may also nuisance trip.","r":"Neutral-ground"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A storage-type water heater is rated 5,500 W at 240 V. Treating it as a continuous load (125 percent), what minimum branch-circuit ampacity is required, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["16.5 A","18.3 A","22.9 A","28.6 A"],"a":3,"e":"Current = 5,500 / 240 = 22.92 A. Branch circuit = 125 percent x 22.92 = 28.65 A. Recognise it: a continuous-load multiplier of 1.25 applies after you convert watts to amps. Why the other choices are wrong: 22.9 A - left out the 125 percent; 18.3 A - multiplied by 80 percent instead of 125 percent; 16.5 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase heater.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"The starting (locked-rotor) current of a typical induction motor is about","o":["six times full-load current","one-half of full-load current","equal to full-load current","two times full-load current"],"a":0,"e":"At standstill the rotor has no counter-EMF, so current is very high, typically 5 to 8 times FLA. Overcurrent devices for motors must tolerate this inrush.","r":"Motor starting current"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"A voltmeter connected across an open switch in a closed series circuit would read approximately","o":["zero volts","the full source voltage","the voltage drop of the load","half the source voltage"],"a":1,"e":"With the switch open, no current flows, so there is no drop across the load. All the voltage is across the open contacts. Zero would be the reading across a closed switch. Use this to troubleshoot: voltage across an open device means that is the open point.","r":"Voltage divider"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A 240 V source is connected across a 16 ohm resistive load. What is the current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["15.0 A","7.5 A","0.1 A","26.0 A"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: I = E / R. Substitute: I = 240 / 16 = 15.00 A, which rounds to 15.0 A. Recognise it: you know voltage and resistance and want current, so put the unknown on top of the Ohm's law triangle and divide the other two. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.1 A - inverted the formula (R / E); 26.0 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase calculation; 7.5 A - used twice the resistance.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A 2,400 sq ft dwelling has general lighting at 3 VA per sq ft, two 1,500 VA small-appliance circuits and one 1,500 VA laundry circuit. Applying the standard demand factors (first 3,000 VA at 100 percent, the rest at 35 percent), what is the demand load, to the nearest VA?","o":["4,095 VA","11,700 VA","6,045 VA","4,470 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Connected load = 2,400 x 3 = 7,200 + 3,000 + 1,500 = 11,700 VA. Demand = 3,000 + 0.35 x (11,700 - 3,000) = 3,000 + 3,045.0 = 6,045.0 VA. Recognise it: a dwelling lighting-and-receptacle demand problem; add the three loads, then take 100 percent of the first 3,000 VA and 35 percent of the remainder. Why the other choices are wrong: 11,700 VA - skipped the demand factors; 4,095 VA - applied 35 percent to the whole load; 4,470 VA - left out the small-appliance and laundry circuits.","r":"Dwelling demand factors"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A series circuit has 9 ohms resistance and 12 ohms inductive reactance. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["3.0 ohms","5.1 ohms","15.0 ohms","21.0 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Resistance and reactance are 90 degrees apart, so they add as a right triangle: Z = square root of (R squared + X squared) = sqrt(9^2 + 12^2) = sqrt(225) = 15.00 ohms. Recognise it: R plus XL (or XC) in series means impedance by the Pythagorean theorem. Why the other choices are wrong: 21.0 ohms - added R and X arithmetically; 3.0 ohms - subtracted R and X; 5.1 ohms - used the parallel resistor formula.","r":"Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2)"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"What is the approximate line current of a 7.5 HP, 230 V three-phase motor at 87 percent efficiency and 0.82 power factor? Use 1.732 and round to the nearest tenth of an amp.","o":["19.7 A","14.0 A","17.1 A","34.1 A"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: I = (HP x 746) / (1.732 x E x eff x PF). Numerator = 7.5 x 746 = 5,595. Denominator = 1.732 x 230 x 0.87 x 0.82 = 284.2. I = 19.69 A. Recognise it: horsepower to amps always needs 746, the 1.732 for three-phase, and both efficiency and PF in the denominator. Why the other choices are wrong: 34.1 A - forgot 1.732; 14.0 A - left out efficiency and PF; 17.1 A - left out efficiency.","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"A three-phase panel supplies 42 A per phase at 208 V. What is the apparent power, to the nearest tenth of a kVA? (Use 1.732.)","o":["8.7 kVA","15.1 kVA","15,131 kVA","26.2 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: kVA = 1.732 x E x I / 1,000 = 1.732 x 208 x 42 / 1,000 = 15.13 kVA. Recognise it: volts and amps, three-phase, no PF mentioned, so apparent power is wanted. Why the other choices are wrong: 8.7 kVA - forgot 1.732; 26.2 kVA - multiplied by 3 instead of 1.732; 15,131 kVA - forgot to divide by 1,000.","r":"kVA = 1.732 E I / 1000"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A 4,000 W, 208 V single-phase resistive load is installed. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["33.3 A","24.0 A","11.1 A","19.2 A"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: I = P / E (resistive, PF = 1.0). Substitute: I = 4,000 / 208 = 19.23 A, about 19.2 A. Recognise it: watts and volts known, single-phase, so divide watts by volts. Only use 1.732 for three-phase. Why the other choices are wrong: 24.0 A - assumed an 0.8 power factor for a resistive load; 33.3 A - divided by the wrong voltage; 11.1 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase load.","r":"I = P / E"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"Chapter 9 Table 8 gives 14 AWG uncoated copper as 3.07 ohms per 1,000 ft. What is the resistance of 100 ft of this conductor, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["0.31 ohms","307.00 ohms","3.07 ohms","30.70 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Scale by length: R = 3.07 x 100 / 1,000 = 0.307 ohms. Recognise it: Table 8 resistance is per 1,000 ft, so multiply by (length / 1,000). Why the other choices are wrong: 307.00 ohms - forgot to divide by 1,000; 3.07 ohms - divided by 100; 30.70 ohms - inverted the length ratio.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 8"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"What is the approximate line current of a 50 HP, 460 V three-phase motor at 94 percent efficiency and 0.9 power factor? Use 1.732 and round to the nearest tenth of an amp.","o":["95.8 A","55.3 A","46.8 A","52.0 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = (HP x 746) / (1.732 x E x eff x PF). Numerator = 50 x 746 = 37,300. Denominator = 1.732 x 460 x 0.94 x 0.9 = 674.0. I = 55.34 A. Recognise it: horsepower to amps always needs 746, the 1.732 for three-phase, and both efficiency and PF in the denominator. Why the other choices are wrong: 95.8 A - forgot 1.732; 46.8 A - left out efficiency and PF; 52.0 A - left out efficiency.","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"What is the resistance of 250 ft of 4 AWG copper (41,740 CM) using R = K x L / CM with K = 12.9? Round to the nearest hundredth of an ohm.","o":["12.94 ohms","0.04 ohms","0.15 ohms","0.08 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: R = K x L / CM = 12.9 x 250 / 41,740 = 0.077 ohms. Recognise it: resistance of one conductor from its length and area; the question asked for one conductor, so no round-trip doubling. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.15 ohms - doubled it for a round trip; 12.94 ohms - inverted the formula; 0.04 ohms - halved it.","r":"R = KL/CM"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A 4-pole motor on 60 Hz turns at 1,750 RPM under load. What is the percent slip, to the nearest tenth?","o":["2.9 percent","97.2 percent","2.8 percent","50.0 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Synchronous speed Ns = 120 x 60 / 4 = 1,800 RPM. Slip = (Ns - Nr) / Ns x 100 = (1,800 - 1,750) / 1,800 x 100 = 2.78 percent. Recognise it: slip is always based on synchronous speed; find Ns first. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.9 percent - divided by rotor speed instead of synchronous speed; 50.0 percent - reported the RPM difference as a percent; 97.2 percent - reported rotor speed as a percent of synchronous.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A single-phase resistive heater draws 10.0 A at 277 V. How many watts does it use?","o":["28 W","287 W","27,700 W","2,770 W"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: P = E x I. Substitute: P = 277 x 10.0 = 2,770 W. Recognise it: volts and amps given, watts wanted, so multiply (power factor is 1.0 for a resistive heater). Why the other choices are wrong: 28 W - divided E / I (that is ohms); 27,700 W - squared the current as well; 287 W - added volts and amps.","r":"P = E x I"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has R = 10 ohms, XL = 30 ohms and XC = 18 ohms. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["58.0 ohms","49.0 ohms","35.0 ohms","15.6 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"XL and XC are opposite, so they subtract: net X = 30 - 18 = 12 ohms. Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2) = sqrt(100 + 144) = 15.62 ohms. Recognise it: three series elements; combine XL and XC first, then use the right-triangle formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 49.0 ohms - added XL and XC instead of subtracting; 58.0 ohms - added all three values; 35.0 ohms - left out the resistance.","r":"Series RLC"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"3 identical 30 ohm lamps are connected in parallel. What is the combined resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["10.0 ohms","90.0 ohms","45.0 ohms","30.0 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"For n equal resistors in parallel, Rt = R / n = 30 / 3 = 10.00 ohms. Recognise it: several identical loads on the same two conductors; each added branch lowers total resistance and raises total current. Why the other choices are wrong: 90.0 ohms - multiplied (series connection); 30.0 ohms - assumed parallel does not change resistance; 45.0 ohms - halved the series total.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":2,"q":"A reading of 'OL' on an ohmmeter between two points means","o":["an open circuit or resistance beyond the meter's range","the meter battery is charged","a short circuit","zero resistance"],"a":0,"e":"OL is overload or out of limit - essentially infinite resistance on that range. A short would read near 0 ohms.","r":"Ohmmeter"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":2,"q":"A motor with an efficiency of 80 percent and an output of 1 HP draws about","o":["597 W","932 W","1,000 W","746 W"],"a":1,"e":"Input = output / efficiency = 746 / 0.80 = 932.5 W. Multiplying (597 W) would make the input smaller than the output, which is impossible. Recognise it: input is always greater than output, so divide by efficiency.","r":"Efficiency"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A balanced three-phase load draws 40 A at 480 V line-to-line with a power factor of 0.85. How many kilowatts is it using, to the nearest tenth? (Use 1.732.)","o":["33.3 kW","28.3 kW","16.3 kW","49.0 kW"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: P = 1.732 x E x I x PF = 1.732 x 480 x 40 x 0.85 = 28,266 W = 28.27 kW. Recognise it: a three-phase load with a stated PF asks for true power; include 1.732 and PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 16.3 kW - forgot 1.732; 33.3 kW - left out power factor (that is kVA); 49.0 kW - used 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"P = 1.732 E I PF"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 2-pole, 60 Hz induction motor has 3 percent slip at full load. What is the rotor speed, to the nearest RPM?","o":["3,597 RPM","3,600 RPM","3,492 RPM","3,708 RPM"],"a":2,"e":"Ns = 120 x 60 / 2 = 3,600 RPM. Rotor speed = Ns x (1 - slip) = 3,600 x 0.970 = 3492.0 RPM. Recognise it: an induction rotor always turns slower than the field, so reduce Ns by the slip fraction. Why the other choices are wrong: 3,708 RPM - added slip to synchronous speed; 3,597 RPM - subtracted the percent as RPM; 3,600 RPM - gave synchronous speed.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 40 degrees C to degrees F, to the nearest degree.","o":["4 F","104 F","130 F","72 F"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: F = (C x 1.8) + 32 = (40 x 1.8) + 32 = 72 + 32 = 104 F. Recognise it: multiply by 1.8 first, then add 32. Why the other choices are wrong: 72 F - forgot to add 32; 130 F - added 32 before multiplying; 4 F - used the Fahrenheit-to-Celsius formula.","r":"F = 1.8 C + 32"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the inductive reactance of a 0.02 H coil at 60 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["15.1 ohms","7.5 ohms","3.8 ohms","1.2 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: XL = 2 x pi x f x L = 6.2832 x 60 x 0.02 = 7.54 ohms. Recognise it: an inductor opposes current more as frequency rises; multiply 2 pi f by henries. Why the other choices are wrong: 15.1 ohms - doubled the 2 pi f L result; 3.8 ohms - left out the 2; 1.2 ohms - multiplied only f x L (left out 2 pi).","r":"XL = 2 pi f L"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A 20 microfarad and a 30 microfarad capacitor are connected in series. What is the total capacitance, to the nearest tenth of a microfarad?","o":["25.0 microfarads","12.0 microfarads","50.0 microfarads","20.0 microfarads"],"a":1,"e":"Capacitors in series combine like resistors in parallel: Ct = (C1 x C2) / (C1 + C2) = (20 x 30) / (20 + 30) = 12.00 microfarads. Recognise it: capacitors are the opposite of resistors - parallel adds, series is the reciprocal formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 50.0 microfarads - added them (that is the parallel result); 25.0 microfarads - averaged the values; 20.0 microfarads - assumed the smaller capacitor alone governs.","r":"Capacitors in series"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A 2 HP motor is 75 percent efficient. How many watts does it draw at full load, to the nearest watt?","o":["1,119 W","2,667 W","1,989 W","1,492 W"],"a":2,"e":"Output watts = 2 x 746 = 1,492 W. Input = output / efficiency = 1,492 / 0.75 = 1,989.3 W. Recognise it: a motor always draws more than it delivers; divide output by efficiency. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,492 W - ignored efficiency (that is output watts); 1,119 W - multiplied by efficiency instead of dividing; 2,667 W - used 1,000 W per HP.","r":"HP = 746 W; efficiency"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A storage-type water heater is rated 3,800 W at 240 V. Treating it as a continuous load (125 percent), what minimum branch-circuit ampacity is required, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["19.8 A","15.8 A","12.7 A","11.4 A"],"a":0,"e":"Current = 3,800 / 240 = 15.83 A. Branch circuit = 125 percent x 15.83 = 19.79 A. Recognise it: a continuous-load multiplier of 1.25 applies after you convert watts to amps. Why the other choices are wrong: 15.8 A - left out the 125 percent; 12.7 A - multiplied by 80 percent instead of 125 percent; 11.4 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase heater.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A conductor has a 90 C ampacity of 40 A. It is bundled with 8 current-carrying conductors in one conduit. Using the adjustment factor (70 percent for 8 conductors), what is the adjusted ampacity, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["57.1 A","12.0 A","40.0 A","28.0 A"],"a":3,"e":"Adjusted ampacity = 40 x 0.7 = 28.0 A. Recognise it: 4-6 conductors gets 80 percent, 7-9 gets 70 percent, 10-20 gets 50 percent; multiply the table ampacity by that factor. Why the other choices are wrong: 40.0 A - did not apply any adjustment; 57.1 A - divided by the factor instead of multiplying; 12.0 A - multiplied by the percent removed.","r":"Table 310.15(C)(1)"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A motor produces 20 lb-ft of torque at 1,750 RPM. What horsepower is it delivering? (HP = torque x RPM / 5,252)","o":["About 6.7 HP","About 35 HP","About 46.9 HP","About 60 HP"],"a":0,"e":"HP = 20 x 1,750 / 5,252 = 35,000 / 5,252 = 6.66 HP. Wrong choices: 60 HP comes from the torque formula (HP x 5,252 / RPM) used backwards; 35 HP leaves out the 5,252; 46.9 HP divides by 746 instead. Recognise it: HP rises with both torque and speed.","r":"Torque and HP"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"Why does a stranded conductor have a bit more resistance than a solid conductor of the same AWG?","o":["Strands carry current only on the surface","Stranded wire is always aluminum","The lay of the strands makes the conductor longer, and the gaps reduce the metal area slightly","The insulation adds resistance"],"a":2,"e":"Strands spiral along the conductor, so each wire is slightly longer than the cable, and air gaps reduce area. The effect appears in Chapter 9 Table 8, where stranded resistance is a little higher.","r":"Stranded conductors"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":2,"q":"A ladder used near electrical equipment should be","o":["any type if dry","aluminum for strength","non-conductive (fiberglass) rated for the use","metal to dissipate charge"],"a":2,"e":"Metal ladders can conduct fault current. Use fiberglass or other nonconductive ladders around energized conductors.","r":"Electrical safety"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A 8.0 A current flows through a 3.5 ohm resistance. What is the voltage drop across it, to the nearest tenth of a volt?","o":["224.0 V","28.0 V","11.5 V","56.0 V"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: E = I x R. Substitute: E = 8.0 x 3.5 = 28.00 V, about 28.0 V. Recognise it: current and resistance known, find the drop, so multiply. Why the other choices are wrong: 224.0 V - squared the current (that is watts); 56.0 V - doubled it for a round trip when only one resistance was given; 11.5 V - added current and resistance.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A 12 kW load runs 1.5 hours per day for 20 days. At $0.15 per kWh, what is the cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$2.70","$2,400.00","$54.00","$54,000.00"],"a":2,"e":"Energy = kW x hours = 12 x 1.5 x 20 = 360 kWh. Cost = kWh x rate = 360 x 0.15 = $54.00. Recognise it: kilowatt-hours are power times time; multiply by the rate last. Why the other choices are wrong: $2.70 - calculated one day only; $54,000.00 - used watts instead of kilowatts; $2,400.00 - divided by the rate instead of multiplying.","r":"kWh cost"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a balanced three-phase circuit of 4 AWG copper (41,740 CM) carrying 65 A over 175 ft one way? Use K = 12.9 and 1.732. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["3.5 V","12.2 V","7.0 V","6.1 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: VD = 1.732 x K x I x L / CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 65 x 175 / 41,740 = 6.09 V. Recognise it: three-phase circuits replace the 2 with 1.732 because the phase currents partially cancel in the return. Why the other choices are wrong: 7.0 V - used 2 (single-phase) instead of 1.732; 3.5 V - used neither 2 nor 1.732; 12.2 V - doubled the length as well as using 1.732.","r":"VD = 1.732KIL/CM"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 5 HP motor at 85 percent efficiency runs 10 hours at full load. Electricity costs $0.14 per kWh. What is the cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$6.14","$7.00","$5.22","$4.44"],"a":0,"e":"Input kW = 5 x 0.746 / 0.85 = 4.388 kW. kWh = 4.388 x 10 = 43.88. Cost = 43.88 x 0.14 = $6.14. Recognise it: convert HP to kW, divide by efficiency to get input, then multiply by hours and rate. Why the other choices are wrong: $5.22 - ignored efficiency; $4.44 - multiplied by efficiency instead of dividing; $7.00 - used HP as if it were kW.","r":"HP and kWh"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"In a wye-connected system the line-to-neutral voltage is 127 V. What is the line-to-line voltage, to the nearest volt? (Use 1.732.)","o":["73 V","254 V","381 V","220 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: E(L-L) = 1.732 x E(L-N) = 1.732 x 127 = 220.0 V, about 220 V. Recognise it: moving from phase to line voltage in a wye multiplies by 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 73 V - divided by 1.732 instead of multiplying; 254 V - doubled it (single-phase thinking); 381 V - multiplied by 3.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"A solid round conductor is 50 mils in diameter. What is its area in circular mils?","o":["1,963 CM","100 CM","2,500 CM","7,854 CM"],"a":2,"e":"One circular mil is the area of a circle 1 mil in diameter, so CM = d squared = 50 x 50 = 2,500 CM. Recognise it: circular mil area is simply the diameter in mils, squared; no pi is needed. Why the other choices are wrong: 100 CM - doubled the diameter; 1,963 CM - used the square-mil area formula (pi d squared / 4); 7,854 CM - multiplied by pi.","r":"Circular mil"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"A feeder conductor has 1.50 ohms of total resistance and carries 12 A. How much power is lost as heat in the conductor, to the nearest watt?","o":["36 W","96 W","18 W","216 W"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: P = I squared x R. Substitute: P = 12 x 12 x 1.50 = 216.0 W, rounded 216 W. Recognise it: current and conductor resistance known, wanted is heat loss, so use I squared R. Doubling the current quadruples the loss. Why the other choices are wrong: 18 W - did not square the current (I x R is volts); 96 W - divided I squared by R; 36 W - doubled I x R instead of squaring I.","r":"P = I^2 x R"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"An ideal transformer steps 4160 V down to 480 V. If the primary current is 3 A, what is the secondary current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["3.0 A","15.0 A","26.0 A","0.3 A"],"a":2,"e":"Ideal transformer: VA in = VA out, so Ep x Ip = Es x Is. Is = Ip x Ep / Es = 3 x 4160 / 480 = 26.00 A. Recognise it: step-down means more current on the low-voltage side, in exact inverse proportion. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.3 A - used the voltage ratio the wrong way round; 3.0 A - assumed the currents are equal; 15.0 A - divided by 1.732 on a single-phase unit.","r":"Transformer current ratio"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"Which is the correct value for the peak of a 240 V RMS sine wave?","o":["About 480 V","About 170 V","About 240 V","About 339 V"],"a":3,"e":"Peak = 1.414 x 240 = 339.4 V. 170 V is the peak of a 120 V circuit, and 480 V would be double. Recognise it: multiply RMS by 1.414 for peak.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A transformer has 1,000 primary turns and 577 secondary turns. With 208 V applied to the primary, what is the secondary voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["76 V","208 V","360 V","120 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: Es = Ep x (Ns / Np) = 208 x (577 / 1,000) = 120.0 V. Recognise it: voltage follows turns; the winding with more turns has the higher voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 360 V - inverted the turns ratio; 76 V - divided by the sum of the turns; 208 V - added a 1.732 factor to a single-phase turns calculation.","r":"Turns ratio"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A 10 kW electric heat strip is running. How many BTU per hour does it produce? (1 kW = 3,412 BTU/hr)","o":["10,000 BTU/hr","7,460 BTU/hr","34,120 BTU/hr","2,931 BTU/hr"],"a":2,"e":"Conversion: BTU/hr = kW x 3,412 = 10 x 3,412 = 34,120 BTU/hr. Recognise it: electric heat is 100 percent efficient, so every kW becomes 3,412 BTU/hr. Why the other choices are wrong: 10,000 BTU/hr - reported watts as BTU; 7,460 BTU/hr - used the 746 horsepower factor; 2,931 BTU/hr - divided by 3.412 instead of multiplying.","r":"1 kW = 3,412 BTU/hr"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A circuit serves 32 A of continuous load and 16 A of noncontinuous load. What minimum overcurrent device ampacity is needed before rounding to a standard size? (Continuous load at 125 percent.)","o":["60 A","52 A","56 A","48 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: 125 percent of continuous + 100 percent of noncontinuous = 32 x 1.25 + 16 = 40 + 16 = 56 A. Recognise it: lights, signs and anything on 3 hours or more is continuous; only that part gets the 125 percent. Why the other choices are wrong: 48 A - added the loads without the 125 percent factor; 60 A - applied 125 percent to the noncontinuous load too; 52 A - applied 125 percent to the wrong load.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A 12.0 ohm heating element is connected to 240 V. What power does it dissipate, to the nearest watt?","o":["2,400 W","20 W","4,800 W","2,880 W"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: P = E squared / R. Substitute: P = 240 x 240 / 12.0 = 4,800.0 W, about 4,800 W. Recognise it: you have voltage and resistance but no current, so use E squared over R. Why the other choices are wrong: 20 W - did not square the voltage (E / R is amps); 2,400 W - used twice the resistance; 2,880 W - multiplied E x R.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"Three resistors of 25, 35 and 40 ohms are connected in series across 200 V. What is the circuit current, to the nearest hundredth of an amp?","o":["8.00 A","18.71 A","2.00 A","6.00 A"],"a":2,"e":"In series, Rt = R1 + R2 + R3 = 25 + 35 + 40 = 100 ohms. Then I = E / Rt = 200 / 100 = 2.000 A, about 2.00 A. Recognise it: one path for the current, so resistances add and the same current flows everywhere. Why the other choices are wrong: 18.71 A - added the resistors as if they were in parallel; 8.00 A - used only the first resistor; 6.00 A - used the average resistance.","r":"Series circuit"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A series circuit has 12 ohms resistance and 16 ohms inductive reactance. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["28.0 ohms","6.9 ohms","4.0 ohms","20.0 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Resistance and reactance are 90 degrees apart, so they add as a right triangle: Z = square root of (R squared + X squared) = sqrt(12^2 + 16^2) = sqrt(400) = 20.00 ohms. Recognise it: R plus XL (or XC) in series means impedance by the Pythagorean theorem. Why the other choices are wrong: 28.0 ohms - added R and X arithmetically; 4.0 ohms - subtracted R and X; 6.9 ohms - used the parallel resistor formula.","r":"Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2)"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"Which meter reading is the 'effective' value of AC voltage on most meters?","o":["Peak","Peak-to-peak","RMS","Instantaneous"],"a":2,"e":"Most meters are calibrated to display RMS for a sine wave. A 120 V reading means 120 V RMS (about 170 V peak).","r":"RMS"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"Two resistive loads of 10 ohms and 40 ohms are connected in parallel across 120 V. What is the total current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["4.8 A","12.0 A","2.4 A","15.0 A"],"a":3,"e":"Each branch sees the full 120 V. I1 = 120 / 10 = 12.00 A and I2 = 120 / 40 = 3.00 A. Total = 15.00 A (Kirchhoff's current law). Recognise it: in parallel, voltage is common and branch currents add. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.4 A - used series resistance; 12.0 A - counted only the first branch; 4.8 A - used the average resistance.","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":3,"q":"Three 20 ohm resistors are connected in delta on a 480 V three-phase supply. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["41.6 A","72.0 A","24.0 A","13.9 A"],"a":0,"e":"In a delta each resistor sees the full line voltage. Phase current = 480 / 20 = 24.00 A. Line current = 1.732 x 24.00 = 41.57 A. Recognise it: delta elements see line voltage, and the line carries 1.732 times the phase current. Why the other choices are wrong: 24.0 A - reported the phase current as the line current; 13.9 A - used wye voltage across a delta element; 72.0 A - multiplied by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A thermal-magnetic circuit breaker responds to overloads with the ___ element and to short circuits with the ___ element.","o":["thermal (bimetal); magnetic (instantaneous)","magnetic; thermal","magnetic; magnetic","thermal; thermal"],"a":0,"e":"The bimetal strip heats and bends for slow overloads (time delay). The electromagnet trips instantly on high fault current.","r":"Breaker operation"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":2,"q":"Approximately how much current through the chest can cause ventricular fibrillation?","o":["About 1 milliamp","About 20 amps","About 0.01 milliamp","About 100 milliamps or more"],"a":3,"e":"Perception is around 1 mA, let-go threshold is about 10-16 mA, and 50-100+ mA across the heart can cause fibrillation. Currents of an amp or more cause burns and cardiac arrest.","r":"Shock effects"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A delta-connected load draws 104 A in each line. What is the current in each phase winding, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["104.0 A","34.7 A","180.1 A","60.0 A"],"a":3,"e":"Delta: I(phase) = I(line) / 1.732 = 104 / 1.732 = 60.05 A. Recognise it: phase current is the smaller number in a delta; line current is 1.732 times larger. Why the other choices are wrong: 104.0 A - assumed phase current equals line current; 180.1 A - multiplied by 1.732; 34.7 A - divided by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A circuit draws 16.0 A from a 208 V supply. What is the load resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["192.0 ohms","6.5 ohms","3,328.0 ohms","13.0 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: R = E / I. Substitute: R = 208 / 16.0 = 13.00 ohms, or 13.0 ohms rounded. Recognise it: voltage and current known, resistance wanted, so divide E by I. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.5 ohms - used twice the current; 3,328.0 ohms - multiplied E x I, which is watts, not ohms; 192.0 ohms - subtracted current from voltage.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Kirchhoff's laws","d":1,"q":"At a junction, 15 A and 10 A flow in and 12 A flows out on one conductor. A fourth conductor also leaves the junction. What current flows in the fourth conductor?","o":["37 A","7 A","13 A","5 A"],"a":2,"e":"Kirchhoff's current law: current in = current out. In = 15 + 10 = 25 A. Out = 12 + I4, so I4 = 25 - 12 = 13 A. Recognise it: any problem about currents meeting at a point; add everything entering and everything leaving and set them equal. Why the other choices are wrong: 37 A - added all three currents; 7 A - mixed up which currents enter and leave; 5 A - ignored the third current.","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"Two incandescent lamps, 100 W and 60 W, both rated 120 V, are wired in series on 120 V. Which lamp glows brighter?","o":["Both are equally bright because the current is the same","The 100 W lamp, because it has the higher wattage","Neither lights because the voltage is divided","The 60 W lamp, because it has the higher resistance"],"a":3,"e":"Resistance: 100 W lamp = 120 x 120 / 100 = 144 ohms; 60 W lamp = 240 ohms. In series both carry the same current, so P = I squared R is greater in the larger resistance, the 60 W lamp. Recognise it: in series, the larger resistance dissipates more power; in parallel, the smaller resistance does.","r":"P = I^2 R"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A metal oxide varistor (MOV) in a surge protector","o":["converts AC to DC","clamps voltage spikes by lowering its resistance at high voltage","increases the line voltage","stores energy indefinitely"],"a":1,"e":"Above its clamping voltage, the MOV's resistance drops sharply and diverts surge current away from the load. It degrades with repeated surges.","r":"Surge protection"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":2,"q":"A megger reading on a motor winding drops steadily over several months. This most likely indicates","o":["insulation deterioration (moisture, contamination, or aging)","the winding is improving","the meter battery is new","the motor's voltage is rising"],"a":0,"e":"Trend testing shows insulation degrading. Low readings suggest moisture or damaged insulation and a risk of ground faults.","r":"Insulation resistance"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"A wire short placed in parallel with a load will","o":["increase the total resistance of the circuit","reduce the voltage to zero without raising current","greatly reduce the total resistance and cause excessive current","have no effect on the circuit"],"a":2,"e":"A near-zero resistance in parallel drops the equivalent resistance toward zero. By I = E / R the current becomes very large until the overcurrent device opens. That is how a short circuit creates an overcurrent.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":1,"q":"A 480 V source feeds a load through conductors with a 14.4 V drop. What voltage is available at the load?","o":["494.4 V","14.4 V","410.9 V","465.6 V"],"a":3,"e":"Load voltage = source - drop = 480 - 14.4 = 465.6 V. Recognise it: conductor resistance always costs voltage; subtract the drop from the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 494.4 V - added the drop; 410.9 V - treated the drop as a percent; 14.4 V - gave the drop itself.","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A 20.0 ohm heating element is connected to 230 V. What power does it dissipate, to the nearest watt?","o":["12 W","4,600 W","1,323 W","2,645 W"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: P = E squared / R. Substitute: P = 230 x 230 / 20.0 = 2,645.0 W, about 2,645 W. Recognise it: you have voltage and resistance but no current, so use E squared over R. Why the other choices are wrong: 12 W - did not square the voltage (E / R is amps); 1,323 W - used twice the resistance; 4,600 W - multiplied E x R.","r":"P = E^2 / R"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":2,"q":"A phase rotation meter is used to","o":["measure harmonic distortion","measure power factor","determine the sequence of the phases (A-B-C or A-C-B)","measure grounding resistance"],"a":2,"e":"Phase sequence determines the direction a three-phase motor will rotate. Check it before connecting motors where direction matters.","r":"Phase rotation"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A transformer has 200 primary turns and 400 secondary turns. With 120 V applied to the primary, what is the secondary voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["416 V","60 V","80 V","240 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: Es = Ep x (Ns / Np) = 120 x (400 / 200) = 240.0 V. Recognise it: voltage follows turns; the winding with more turns has the higher voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 60 V - inverted the turns ratio; 80 V - divided by the sum of the turns; 416 V - added a 1.732 factor to a single-phase turns calculation.","r":"Turns ratio"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"A three-phase panel supplies 60 A per phase at 480 V. What is the apparent power, to the nearest tenth of a kVA? (Use 1.732.)","o":["49.9 kVA","49,882 kVA","28.8 kVA","86.4 kVA"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: kVA = 1.732 x E x I / 1,000 = 1.732 x 480 x 60 / 1,000 = 49.88 kVA. Recognise it: volts and amps, three-phase, no PF mentioned, so apparent power is wanted. Why the other choices are wrong: 28.8 kVA - forgot 1.732; 86.4 kVA - multiplied by 3 instead of 1.732; 49,882 kVA - forgot to divide by 1,000.","r":"kVA = 1.732 E I / 1000"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A meter reads 277 V RMS on an AC circuit. What is the peak voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["392 V","554 V","196 V","176 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: Vpeak = 1.414 x Vrms = 1.414 x 277 = 391.7 V. Recognise it: the sine wave peaks higher than its RMS (heating) value; peak is 1.414 times RMS. Why the other choices are wrong: 196 V - multiplied by 0.707 (that converts peak to RMS); 176 V - used the average factor; 554 V - doubled the RMS value.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A copper conductor measures 3.0 ohms at 20 C. Using a temperature coefficient of 0.00393 per C, what is its resistance at 50 C, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["2.65 ohms","0.35 ohms","3.59 ohms","3.35 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: R2 = R1 x [1 + alpha x (T2 - T1)] = 3.0 x [1 + 0.00393 x (50 - 20)] = 3.0 x 1.1179 = 3.354 ohms. Recognise it: copper resistance rises with temperature; use the change in temperature, not the final temperature. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.65 ohms - subtracted the temperature rise (resistance falls); 3.59 ohms - used the final temperature instead of the change; 0.35 ohms - left out the original resistance term.","r":"Resistance vs temperature"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A circuit serves 80 A of continuous load and 25 A of noncontinuous load. What minimum overcurrent device ampacity is needed before rounding to a standard size? (Continuous load at 125 percent.)","o":["131.25 A","105 A","111.25 A","125 A"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: 125 percent of continuous + 100 percent of noncontinuous = 80 x 1.25 + 25 = 100 + 25 = 125 A. Recognise it: lights, signs and anything on 3 hours or more is continuous; only that part gets the 125 percent. Why the other choices are wrong: 105 A - added the loads without the 125 percent factor; 131.25 A - applied 125 percent to the noncontinuous load too; 111.25 A - applied 125 percent to the wrong load.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":3,"q":"A balanced three-phase load draws 50 A per line. What is the current in each phase of a delta-connected load?","o":["About 28.9 A","About 16.7 A","About 86.6 A","50 A"],"a":0,"e":"Delta phase current = line current / 1.732 = 50 / 1.732 = 28.87 A. Multiplying (86.6 A) is the wrong direction, and 16.7 A divides by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"In a 3-phase transformer bank, the secondary of a delta-wye transformer provides","o":["dc voltage","no ability to supply single-phase loads","a neutral for 120/208 V or 277/480 V loads","only 240 V delta"],"a":2,"e":"The wye secondary has a common point, the neutral, giving line-to-neutral voltages. A delta secondary has no neutral unless center-tapped.","r":"Transformer connections"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"Chapter 9 Table 8 gives 8 AWG uncoated copper as 0.778 ohms per 1,000 ft. What is the resistance of 600 ft of this conductor, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["0.47 ohms","1.30 ohms","466.80 ohms","4.67 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Scale by length: R = 0.778 x 600 / 1,000 = 0.467 ohms. Recognise it: Table 8 resistance is per 1,000 ft, so multiply by (length / 1,000). Why the other choices are wrong: 466.80 ohms - forgot to divide by 1,000; 4.67 ohms - divided by 100; 1.30 ohms - inverted the length ratio.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 8"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":2,"q":"Why is a GFCI recommended where a tool is used outdoors?","o":["It prevents overloading the circuit","It stops arcing in the cord","It cuts power in milliseconds if current leaks through a person or the ground path","It increases the voltage at the tool"],"a":2,"e":"A GFCI detects imbalance of 4-6 mA between hot and neutral and opens quickly, limiting shock exposure. It does not protect from overloads or line-to-line contact.","r":"GFCI"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 45 kVA three-phase load operates at 480 V. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["54.1 A","31.3 A","162.4 A","93.8 A"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / (1.732 x E) = 45,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 45,000 / 831.4 = 54.13 A. Recognise it: kVA given for a three-phase system, find amps, so convert to VA and divide by 1.732 x line voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 93.8 A - forgot 1.732; 162.4 A - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing; 31.3 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"I = kVA x 1000 / (1.732 E)"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"4 identical 24 ohm lamps are connected in parallel. What is the combined resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["24.0 ohms","96.0 ohms","48.0 ohms","6.0 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"For n equal resistors in parallel, Rt = R / n = 24 / 4 = 6.00 ohms. Recognise it: several identical loads on the same two conductors; each added branch lowers total resistance and raises total current. Why the other choices are wrong: 96.0 ohms - multiplied (series connection); 24.0 ohms - assumed parallel does not change resistance; 48.0 ohms - halved the series total.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A meter reads 480 V RMS on an AC circuit. What is the peak voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["679 V","306 V","960 V","339 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: Vpeak = 1.414 x Vrms = 1.414 x 480 = 678.7 V. Recognise it: the sine wave peaks higher than its RMS (heating) value; peak is 1.414 times RMS. Why the other choices are wrong: 339 V - multiplied by 0.707 (that converts peak to RMS); 306 V - used the average factor; 960 V - doubled the RMS value.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A circuit serves 12 A of continuous load and 8 A of noncontinuous load. What minimum overcurrent device ampacity is needed before rounding to a standard size? (Continuous load at 125 percent.)","o":["23 A","22 A","25 A","20 A"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: 125 percent of continuous + 100 percent of noncontinuous = 12 x 1.25 + 8 = 15 + 8 = 23 A. Recognise it: lights, signs and anything on 3 hours or more is continuous; only that part gets the 125 percent. Why the other choices are wrong: 20 A - added the loads without the 125 percent factor; 25 A - applied 125 percent to the noncontinuous load too; 22 A - applied 125 percent to the wrong load.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"Power factor of 1.0 means","o":["current and voltage are in phase and kW equals kVA","no real power is delivered","current lags voltage by 90 degrees","the load is entirely inductive"],"a":0,"e":"A purely resistive load (heater, incandescent lamp) has a 0-degree angle, so cos = 1.0 and true power equals apparent power. A 90-degree lag would give PF = 0.","r":"Power factor"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has L = 1.0 H and C = 10 microfarads. What is its resonant frequency, to the nearest hertz?","o":["316 Hz","50 Hz","15,915 Hz","25 Hz"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: f = 1 / (2 pi sqrt(LC)). LC = 1.0 x 1.00e-05 = 1.000e-05; sqrt = 0.00316; f = 1 / (6.2832 x 0.00316) = 50.3 Hz. Recognise it: at resonance XL = XC, so set the two reactance formulas equal and solve for f. Why the other choices are wrong: 316 Hz - left out the 2 pi (gave radians per second); 15,915 Hz - forgot the square root; 25 Hz - halved the result.","r":"Resonance"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 50 kW load operates at 0.7 PF. How many kVAR of capacitors are needed to raise the power factor to 0.9, to the nearest kVAR?","o":["51 kVAR","10 kVAR","15 kVAR","27 kVAR"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: kVAR(cap) = kW x (tan of old angle - tan of new angle). Old angle = arccos(0.7) = 45.6 deg, tan = 1.020. New angle = arccos(0.9) = 25.8 deg, tan = 0.484. kVAR = 50 x (1.020 - 0.484) = 26.8. Recognise it: capacitors supply reactive power; subtract the reactive demand before and after correction. Why the other choices are wrong: 15 kVAR - used kW x (1 - PF); 10 kVAR - multiplied by the PF difference; 51 kVAR - removed all reactive power regardless of the target.","r":"PF correction"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A 4.5 kW load runs 2 hours per day for 30 days. At $0.14 per kWh, what is the cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$1.26","$37,800.00","$1,928.57","$37.80"],"a":3,"e":"Energy = kW x hours = 4.5 x 2 x 30 = 270 kWh. Cost = kWh x rate = 270 x 0.14 = $37.80. Recognise it: kilowatt-hours are power times time; multiply by the rate last. Why the other choices are wrong: $1.26 - calculated one day only; $37,800.00 - used watts instead of kilowatts; $1,928.57 - divided by the rate instead of multiplying.","r":"kWh cost"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"A 20 ohm resistor is in series with two resistors (30 ohm and 60 ohm) that are in parallel with each other. What is the total circuit resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["110.0 ohms","40.0 ohms","10.0 ohms","20.0 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Step 1, reduce the parallel pair: (30 x 60) / (30 + 60) = 20.00 ohms. Step 2, add the series resistor: 20 + 20.00 = 40.00 ohms. Recognise it: a mix of series and parallel; always collapse the parallel groups first, then add the series parts. Why the other choices are wrong: 110.0 ohms - treated everything as series; 10.0 ohms - treated everything as parallel; 20.0 ohms - forgot to add the series resistor.","r":"Combination circuit"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"The unit used to measure conductor cross-sectional area for large conductors, 250 and above, is","o":["sq in per ft","kcmil (thousands of circular mils)","mils per foot","AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Larger than 4/0 AWG, conductors are sized in kcmil (formerly MCM). 250 kcmil = 250,000 circular mils.","r":"kcmil"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":1,"q":"Resistance must be measured with the circuit","o":["de-energized and isolated","carrying rated current","connected to the neutral only","energized at full load"],"a":0,"e":"An ohmmeter supplies its own small test voltage. External voltage gives false readings and can damage the meter. Isolate the component to avoid parallel paths.","r":"Ohmmeter"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the unit of impedance?","o":["farad","ohm","watt","henry"],"a":1,"e":"Impedance (Z) is the total opposition to AC from resistance and reactance; it is measured in ohms. Henrys measure inductance, farads capacitance, and watts power.","r":"Impedance"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has R = 8 ohms, XL = 20 ohms and XC = 14 ohms. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["10.0 ohms","42.0 ohms","34.9 ohms","24.4 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"XL and XC are opposite, so they subtract: net X = 20 - 14 = 6 ohms. Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2) = sqrt(64 + 36) = 10.00 ohms. Recognise it: three series elements; combine XL and XC first, then use the right-triangle formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 34.9 ohms - added XL and XC instead of subtracting; 42.0 ohms - added all three values; 24.4 ohms - left out the resistance.","r":"Series RLC"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A transformer with more turns on the primary than on the secondary is a","o":["isolation transformer with 1:1 ratio","step-down transformer","step-up transformer","autotransformer"],"a":1,"e":"Voltage follows turns: Es = Ep x Ns / Np. Fewer secondary turns means lower secondary voltage - a step-down transformer, with higher secondary current.","r":"Turns ratio"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"The total resistance of a parallel circuit is always","o":["the sum of the branch resistances","the average of the branch resistances","equal to the largest branch resistance","less than the smallest branch resistance"],"a":3,"e":"Every branch adds another path for current, so the combined resistance must be below even the smallest branch. Example: 10 ohms and 40 ohms in parallel = 8 ohms. Use this as a check on every parallel calculation.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"A heater is rated 1,800 W at 120 V. What is its hot resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["8.0 ohms","4.0 ohms","15.0 ohms","16.0 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: R = E squared / P. Substitute: R = 120 x 120 / 1,800 = 8.00 ohms, about 8.0 ohms. Recognise it: nameplate watts and volts are given and resistance is wanted, so use R = E squared / P. Why the other choices are wrong: 4.0 ohms - halved the result; 15.0 ohms - used P / E, which is amps, not ohms; 16.0 ohms - doubled the result.","r":"R = E^2 / P"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"What is the full-load current on the 208 V side of a 150 kVA three-phase transformer, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["1,249.0 A","416.4 A","240.4 A","721.2 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / (1.732 x E) = 150,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 416.37 A. Recognise it: a three-phase kVA rating to amps always includes 1.732 and the voltage of the winding you are asking about. Why the other choices are wrong: 721.2 A - forgot 1.732; 240.4 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 1,249.0 A - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A bank of lamps totals 3,600 W and burns 5 hours. At $0.11 per kWh, what does the run cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$0.40","$163.64","$1,980.00","$1.98"],"a":3,"e":"Convert watts to kilowatts: 3,600 / 1,000 = 3.6 kW. kWh = 3.6 x 5 = 18. Cost = 18 x $0.11 = $1.98. Recognise it: any watts-and-hours problem needs the /1,000 step before the rate is applied. Why the other choices are wrong: $1,980.00 - forgot to convert watts to kilowatts; $0.40 - left out the hours; $163.64 - divided by the rate.","r":"kWh cost"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A single-phase circuit uses 12 AWG copper (1.98 ohms per 1,000 ft) for 100 ft one way and carries 16 A. How much power is lost in the two conductors, to the nearest watt?","o":["101 W","51 W","507 W","6 W"],"a":0,"e":"Total conductor length = 2 x 100 = 200 ft, so R = 1.98 x 200 / 1,000 = 0.396 ohms. Loss = I squared x R = 16 x 16 x 0.396 = 101.4 W. Recognise it: heat in the wiring is I squared R using the round-trip resistance. Why the other choices are wrong: 51 W - counted one conductor only; 6 W - did not square the current; 507 W - mis-scaled the length.","r":"P = I^2 R"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"Chapter 9 Table 8 gives 12 AWG uncoated copper as 1.98 ohms per 1,000 ft. What is the resistance of 75 ft of this conductor, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["26.40 ohms","148.50 ohms","1.49 ohms","0.15 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Scale by length: R = 1.98 x 75 / 1,000 = 0.148 ohms. Recognise it: Table 8 resistance is per 1,000 ft, so multiply by (length / 1,000). Why the other choices are wrong: 148.50 ohms - forgot to divide by 1,000; 1.49 ohms - divided by 100; 26.40 ohms - inverted the length ratio.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 8"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A single-phase circuit uses 14 AWG copper (3.07 ohms per 1,000 ft) for 75 ft one way and carries 12 A. How much power is lost in the two conductors, to the nearest watt?","o":["66 W","6 W","332 W","33 W"],"a":0,"e":"Total conductor length = 2 x 75 = 150 ft, so R = 3.07 x 150 / 1,000 = 0.461 ohms. Loss = I squared x R = 12 x 12 x 0.461 = 66.3 W. Recognise it: heat in the wiring is I squared R using the round-trip resistance. Why the other choices are wrong: 33 W - counted one conductor only; 6 W - did not square the current; 332 W - mis-scaled the length.","r":"P = I^2 R"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 3 HP motor at 80 percent efficiency runs 6 hours at full load. Electricity costs $0.15 per kWh. What is the cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$2.52","$2.01","$1.61","$2.70"],"a":0,"e":"Input kW = 3 x 0.746 / 0.80 = 2.797 kW. kWh = 2.797 x 6 = 16.79. Cost = 16.79 x 0.15 = $2.52. Recognise it: convert HP to kW, divide by efficiency to get input, then multiply by hours and rate. Why the other choices are wrong: $2.01 - ignored efficiency; $1.61 - multiplied by efficiency instead of dividing; $2.70 - used HP as if it were kW.","r":"HP and kWh"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":1,"q":"A dwelling calculated load totals 31,200 VA on a 120/240 V single-phase service. What is the calculated service current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["260.0 A","75.1 A","130.0 A","150.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = VA / E = 31,200 / 240 = 130.00 A. Recognise it: a 120/240 V single-phase service is sized on 240 V; divide the total VA by 240. Why the other choices are wrong: 260.0 A - divided by 120 V instead of 240 V; 75.1 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase service; 150.0 A - divided by 208 V.","r":"I = VA / E"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A 2 kW electric heat strip is running. How many BTU per hour does it produce? (1 kW = 3,412 BTU/hr)","o":["586 BTU/hr","2,000 BTU/hr","6,824 BTU/hr","1,492 BTU/hr"],"a":2,"e":"Conversion: BTU/hr = kW x 3,412 = 2 x 3,412 = 6,824 BTU/hr. Recognise it: electric heat is 100 percent efficient, so every kW becomes 3,412 BTU/hr. Why the other choices are wrong: 2,000 BTU/hr - reported watts as BTU; 1,492 BTU/hr - used the 746 horsepower factor; 586 BTU/hr - divided by 3.412 instead of multiplying.","r":"1 kW = 3,412 BTU/hr"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"The peak of a sine wave is 340 V. What is the RMS value, to the nearest volt?","o":["240 V","481 V","170 V","217 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: Vrms = 0.707 x Vpeak = 0.707 x 340 = 240.4 V. Recognise it: RMS is the DC-equivalent heating value and is always lower than peak. Why the other choices are wrong: 481 V - multiplied by 1.414 instead of 0.707; 217 V - used the average value factor; 170 V - halved the peak.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"A conductor has 2.0 ohms of resistance. A new conductor of the same material is 1.5 times as long and has 3 times the cross-sectional area. What is the new resistance, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["1.00 ohms","9.00 ohms","3.00 ohms","4.00 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Resistance is proportional to length and inversely proportional to area: R2 = R1 x (1.5 / 3) = 2.0 x 0.500 = 1.000 ohms. Recognise it: more length raises resistance; more area lowers it. Why the other choices are wrong: 9.00 ohms - multiplied by the area factor instead of dividing; 4.00 ohms - divided by length and multiplied by area; 3.00 ohms - ignored the change in area.","r":"R = KL/A"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has L = 0.1 H and C = 100 microfarads. What is its resonant frequency, to the nearest hertz?","o":["50 Hz","15,915 Hz","316 Hz","25 Hz"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: f = 1 / (2 pi sqrt(LC)). LC = 0.1 x 1.00e-04 = 1.000e-05; sqrt = 0.00316; f = 1 / (6.2832 x 0.00316) = 50.3 Hz. Recognise it: at resonance XL = XC, so set the two reactance formulas equal and solve for f. Why the other choices are wrong: 316 Hz - left out the 2 pi (gave radians per second); 15,915 Hz - forgot the square root; 25 Hz - halved the result.","r":"Resonance"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":3,"q":"An NTC thermistor's resistance","o":["decreases as temperature increases","increases as temperature increases","does not change with temperature","becomes infinite when hot"],"a":0,"e":"NTC = negative temperature coefficient. These are used in temperature sensing and inrush limiting. A PTC thermistor does the opposite.","r":"Thermistors"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"What is the equivalent resistance of 15 ohm and 10 ohm resistors in parallel, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["12.5 ohms","6.0 ohms","25.0 ohms","5.0 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: Rt = (R1 x R2) / (R1 + R2) = (15 x 10) / (15 + 10) = 150 / 25 = 6.00 ohms. Recognise it: two resistors across the same two points is parallel; the answer must be less than the smaller resistor (10 ohms). Why the other choices are wrong: 25.0 ohms - added them as a series circuit; 12.5 ohms - averaged the two values; 5.0 ohms - halved the smaller resistor.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a balanced three-phase circuit of 10 AWG copper (10,380 CM) carrying 28 A over 100 ft one way? Use K = 12.9 and 1.732. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["12.1 V","6.0 V","7.0 V","3.5 V"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: VD = 1.732 x K x I x L / CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 28 x 100 / 10,380 = 6.03 V. Recognise it: three-phase circuits replace the 2 with 1.732 because the phase currents partially cancel in the return. Why the other choices are wrong: 7.0 V - used 2 (single-phase) instead of 1.732; 3.5 V - used neither 2 nor 1.732; 12.1 V - doubled the length as well as using 1.732.","r":"VD = 1.732KIL/CM"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A 3 kW load runs 5 hours per day for 7 days. At $0.13 per kWh, what is the cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$807.69","$13.65","$1.95","$13,650.00"],"a":1,"e":"Energy = kW x hours = 3 x 5 x 7 = 105 kWh. Cost = kWh x rate = 105 x 0.13 = $13.65. Recognise it: kilowatt-hours are power times time; multiply by the rate last. Why the other choices are wrong: $1.95 - calculated one day only; $13,650.00 - used watts instead of kilowatts; $807.69 - divided by the rate instead of multiplying.","r":"kWh cost"},{"c":"Kirchhoff's laws","d":1,"q":"At a junction, 25 A and 25 A flow in and 30 A flows out on one conductor. A fourth conductor also leaves the junction. What current flows in the fourth conductor?","o":["0 A","80 A","20 A","30 A"],"a":2,"e":"Kirchhoff's current law: current in = current out. In = 25 + 25 = 50 A. Out = 30 + I4, so I4 = 50 - 30 = 20 A. Recognise it: any problem about currents meeting at a point; add everything entering and everything leaving and set them equal. Why the other choices are wrong: 80 A - added all three currents; 30 A - mixed up which currents enter and leave; 0 A - ignored the third current.","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"Two resistive loads of 26 ohms and 52 ohms are connected in parallel across 208 V. What is the total current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["5.3 A","8.0 A","2.7 A","12.0 A"],"a":3,"e":"Each branch sees the full 208 V. I1 = 208 / 26 = 8.00 A and I2 = 208 / 52 = 4.00 A. Total = 12.00 A (Kirchhoff's current law). Recognise it: in parallel, voltage is common and branch currents add. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.7 A - used series resistance; 8.0 A - counted only the first branch; 5.3 A - used the average resistance.","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":2,"q":"A clamp-on ammeter reading is accurate only when the jaws surround","o":["both the hot and neutral of the same circuit","the equipment grounding conductor and hot","a single conductor (not the whole cable)","all three phase conductors"],"a":2,"e":"The meter senses the magnetic field around a conductor. If both the hot and return are enclosed, the fields cancel and the reading is near zero. Clamp one conductor at a time.","r":"Clamp meter"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A circuit serves 20 A of continuous load and 5 A of noncontinuous load. What minimum overcurrent device ampacity is needed before rounding to a standard size? (Continuous load at 125 percent.)","o":["26.25 A","31.25 A","30 A","25 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: 125 percent of continuous + 100 percent of noncontinuous = 20 x 1.25 + 5 = 25 + 5 = 30 A. Recognise it: lights, signs and anything on 3 hours or more is continuous; only that part gets the 125 percent. Why the other choices are wrong: 25 A - added the loads without the 125 percent factor; 31.25 A - applied 125 percent to the noncontinuous load too; 26.25 A - applied 125 percent to the wrong load.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"In a series circuit of 4, 8 and 12 ohms across 48 V, what is the voltage drop across the 4 ohm resistor, to the nearest tenth of a volt?","o":["9.6 V","8.0 V","16.0 V","48.0 V"],"a":1,"e":"Rt = 4 + 8 + 12 = 24 ohms; I = 48 / 24 = 2.0000 A; drop = I x R = 2.0000 x 4 = 8.00 V. Shortcut: E x R / Rt. Recognise it: drops in a series circuit are proportional to resistance and add up to the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 48.0 V - assumed the full source voltage appears across one resistor; 16.0 V - split the voltage equally; 9.6 V - divided by the other resistors only instead of Rt.","r":"Voltage divider"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a single-phase circuit of 14 AWG copper (4,110 CM), carrying 15 A over 75 ft one way? Use K = 12.9. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["3.5 V","14.1 V","7.1 V","6.1 V"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: VD = 2 x K x I x L / CM = 2 x 12.9 x 15 x 75 / 4,110 = 29,025.0 / 4,110 = 7.06 V. Recognise it: single-phase, K given, one-way length given; the 2 accounts for current going out and back. Why the other choices are wrong: 3.5 V - left out the 2 for the round trip; 6.1 V - used 1.732 (the three-phase multiplier); 14.1 V - doubled the length twice.","r":"VD = 2KIL/CM"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 194 degrees F to degrees C, to the nearest degree.","o":["162 C","381 C","76 C","90 C"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: C = (F - 32) / 1.8 = (194 - 32) / 1.8 = 162 / 1.8 = 90.0 C. Recognise it: subtract 32 first, then divide by 1.8 (multiply by 5/9). Why the other choices are wrong: 162 C - subtracted 32 but did not divide by 1.8; 76 C - divided before subtracting 32; 381 C - used the Celsius-to-Fahrenheit formula.","r":"C = (F - 32) / 1.8"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A 150 kVA transformer is supplying 120 kVA of load. What percent of its rating is in use, to the nearest percent?","o":["80 percent","125 percent","20 percent","0.8 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Percent loading = load / rating x 100 = 120 / 150 x 100 = 80.0 percent. Recognise it: compare actual load to nameplate; a result over 100 percent means overload. Why the other choices are wrong: 125 percent - inverted the ratio; 20 percent - reported the spare capacity; 0.8 percent - forgot to multiply by 100.","r":"Transformer loading"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"What does a motor's service factor of 1.15 indicate?","o":["It can carry up to 115 percent of rated load continuously under specified conditions","It is 115 percent efficient","Its starting current is 1.15 times full-load","It can run at 115 percent of rated voltage"],"a":0,"e":"Service factor is a multiplier for allowable continuous overload. Rated 10 HP with SF 1.15 can deliver 11.5 HP. It does not change voltage or efficiency.","r":"Service factor"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":2,"q":"Increasing the temperature of a copper conductor causes its resistance to","o":["become zero","decrease","stay the same","increase"],"a":3,"e":"Metals have a positive temperature coefficient. R2 = R1[1 + alpha (T2 - T1)] with alpha about 0.0039 per degree C for copper. Carbon and thermistor (NTC) materials behave the other way.","r":"Resistance vs temperature"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"If the number of poles in an induction motor is doubled on the same supply frequency, synchronous speed","o":["stays the same","is cut in half","increases by 1.732","doubles"],"a":1,"e":"Ns = 120 f / P. P in the denominator: 4 poles at 60 Hz = 1,800; 8 poles = 900 RPM.","r":"Ns = 120f/P"},{"c":"Motors","d":1,"q":"What is the synchronous speed of a 2-pole induction motor on a 50 Hz supply?","o":["1,500 RPM","3,000 RPM","12,000 RPM","5 RPM"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: Ns = 120 x f / P = 120 x 50 / 2 = 6,000 / 2 = 3,000 RPM. Recognise it: the question gives poles and frequency and asks for speed; the 120 already accounts for pole pairs. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,500 RPM - used 60 instead of 120; 5 RPM - inverted poles and frequency; 12,000 RPM - divided by pole pairs instead of poles.","r":"Ns = 120f/P"},{"c":"Grounding and bonding theory","d":2,"q":"Which statement is correct about a grounded (neutral) conductor and a grounding conductor?","o":["The neutral carries normal load current; the equipment grounding conductor does not except during faults","They are interchangeable","The grounding conductor carries all return current","Both carry normal load current equally"],"a":0,"e":"The neutral is a current-carrying circuit conductor. The EGC is a safety conductor and should carry current only during a fault or from leakage.","r":"Grounded vs grounding"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the main benefit of correcting a low power factor?","o":["Lower current for the same real power, which reduces conductor loss and voltage drop","More real power is produced by the load","The voltage of the system increases permanently","The conductors can no longer overheat under any load"],"a":0,"e":"At the same kW, a higher PF reduces kVA and current. I = kW / (1.732 x E x PF). Example: 100 kW at 480 V: PF 0.8 = 150 A; PF 1.0 = 120 A. The load does not produce more power.","r":"Power factor correction"},{"c":"Transformers","d":3,"q":"A 225 kVA, 208 V three-phase transformer has 2.5 percent impedance. Assuming an infinite primary source, what is the approximate secondary short-circuit current, to the nearest 10 A? (Use 1.732.)","o":["620 A","24,980 A","43,270 A","1,560 A"],"a":1,"e":"Step 1: FLA = 225,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 624.6 A. Step 2: Isc = FLA / (%Z / 100) = 624.6 / 0.0250 = 24982 A. Recognise it: a short-circuit-current question that gives %Z; the lower the impedance, the higher the fault current. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,560 A - multiplied FLA by the percent instead of dividing; 620 A - gave the full-load current; 43,270 A - forgot 1.732 when finding FLA.","r":"Transformer fault current"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 122 degrees F to degrees C, to the nearest degree.","o":["90 C","50 C","36 C","252 C"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: C = (F - 32) / 1.8 = (122 - 32) / 1.8 = 90 / 1.8 = 50.0 C. Recognise it: subtract 32 first, then divide by 1.8 (multiply by 5/9). Why the other choices are wrong: 90 C - subtracted 32 but did not divide by 1.8; 36 C - divided before subtracting 32; 252 C - used the Celsius-to-Fahrenheit formula.","r":"C = (F - 32) / 1.8"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"In a balanced delta load each phase winding carries 12 A. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["6.9 A","36.0 A","20.8 A","12.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"Delta: I(line) = 1.732 x I(phase) = 1.732 x 12 = 20.78 A. Recognise it: in delta the line voltage equals phase voltage but line current is 1.732 times phase current. Why the other choices are wrong: 12.0 A - assumed line current equals phase current (that is wye); 6.9 A - divided by 1.732 instead of multiplying; 36.0 A - multiplied by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"In a balanced delta load each phase winding carries 10 A. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["5.8 A","30.0 A","10.0 A","17.3 A"],"a":3,"e":"Delta: I(line) = 1.732 x I(phase) = 1.732 x 10 = 17.32 A. Recognise it: in delta the line voltage equals phase voltage but line current is 1.732 times phase current. Why the other choices are wrong: 10.0 A - assumed line current equals phase current (that is wye); 5.8 A - divided by 1.732 instead of multiplying; 30.0 A - multiplied by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A 5 HP motor is 85 percent efficient. How many watts does it draw at full load, to the nearest watt?","o":["5,882 W","4,388 W","3,171 W","3,730 W"],"a":1,"e":"Output watts = 5 x 746 = 3,730 W. Input = output / efficiency = 3,730 / 0.85 = 4,388.2 W. Recognise it: a motor always draws more than it delivers; divide output by efficiency. Why the other choices are wrong: 3,730 W - ignored efficiency (that is output watts); 3,171 W - multiplied by efficiency instead of dividing; 5,882 W - used 1,000 W per HP.","r":"HP = 746 W; efficiency"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load has a power factor of 0.9. What is the phase angle between voltage and current, to the nearest degree?","o":["26 degrees","64 degrees","81 degrees","42 degrees"],"a":0,"e":"PF = cos(angle), so angle = arccos(0.9) = 25.8 degrees. Recognise it: power factor is the cosine of the phase angle; use the inverse cosine key. Why the other choices are wrong: 64 degrees - used the inverse sine instead of inverse cosine; 42 degrees - used the inverse tangent; 81 degrees - scaled the PF to 90 degrees.","r":"PF = cos(theta)"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":3,"q":"A 120 V source feeds a 10 ohm resistor in series with a parallel pair of 20 ohm and 30 ohm. What current flows in the 20 ohm resistor, to the nearest hundredth of an amp?","o":["5.45 A","6.00 A","2.73 A","3.27 A"],"a":3,"e":"Rt = 10 + (20 x 30) / (20 + 30) = 22.00 ohms. Total I = 120 / 22.00 = 5.455 A. Drop in series resistor = 5.455 x 10 = 54.55 V, so the parallel pair sees 120 - 54.55 = 65.45 V. Branch current = 65.45 / 20 = 3.273 A. Recognise it: find Rt, then total current, then voltage across the parallel section, then the branch current. Why the other choices are wrong: 5.45 A - used the total current instead of the branch current; 6.00 A - put the full source voltage across the branch; 2.73 A - split the current equally between unequal branches.","r":"Combination circuit"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 1 HP, 115 V single-phase motor runs at 78 percent efficiency and 0.75 power factor. What is the running current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["6.4 A","11.1 A","6.5 A","8.6 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = (HP x 746) / (E x eff x PF) = 746 / (115 x 0.78 x 0.75) = 746 / 67.3 = 11.09 A. Recognise it: single-phase, so no 1.732; efficiency and PF both reduce how much work each amp does. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.4 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase motor; 6.5 A - ignored efficiency and PF; 8.6 A - left out efficiency.","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"What is true of voltage in a parallel circuit?","o":["The smallest resistance has the highest voltage","Every branch has the same voltage","Voltage adds up across the branches","Voltage divides among the branches"],"a":1,"e":"Each branch is connected directly across the source, so each sees the full source voltage. Branch currents differ, voltage does not. That is why receptacles on a branch circuit all receive about 120 V.","r":"Parallel circuit"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A GFCI trips when the difference between current on the hot and neutral conductors is about","o":["100 amps","15 amps","1 amp","4 to 6 milliamps"],"a":3,"e":"A Class A GFCI trips at 4-6 mA of ground-fault current, well below the level that causes ventricular fibrillation. It senses the imbalance, not the overload.","r":"GFCI"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A 3,500 sq ft dwelling has general lighting at 3 VA per sq ft, two 1,500 VA small-appliance circuits and one 1,500 VA laundry circuit. Applying the standard demand factors (first 3,000 VA at 100 percent, the rest at 35 percent), what is the demand load, to the nearest VA?","o":["7,200 VA","15,000 VA","5,625 VA","5,250 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Connected load = 3,500 x 3 = 10,500 + 3,000 + 1,500 = 15,000 VA. Demand = 3,000 + 0.35 x (15,000 - 3,000) = 3,000 + 4,200.0 = 7,200.0 VA. Recognise it: a dwelling lighting-and-receptacle demand problem; add the three loads, then take 100 percent of the first 3,000 VA and 35 percent of the remainder. Why the other choices are wrong: 15,000 VA - skipped the demand factors; 5,250 VA - applied 35 percent to the whole load; 5,625 VA - left out the small-appliance and laundry circuits.","r":"Dwelling demand factors"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A potentiometer is","o":["a fixed capacitor","a two-wire switch","a variable resistor with three terminals used as a voltage divider","a type of transformer"],"a":2,"e":"Moving the wiper along the resistive element taps off a portion of the supplied voltage. A rheostat uses two terminals for variable resistance.","r":"Variable resistors"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has R = 15 ohms, XL = 35 ohms and XC = 20 ohms. What is the impedance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["70.0 ohms","21.2 ohms","57.0 ohms","40.3 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"XL and XC are opposite, so they subtract: net X = 35 - 20 = 15 ohms. Z = sqrt(R^2 + X^2) = sqrt(225 + 225) = 21.21 ohms. Recognise it: three series elements; combine XL and XC first, then use the right-triangle formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 57.0 ohms - added XL and XC instead of subtracting; 70.0 ohms - added all three values; 40.3 ohms - left out the resistance.","r":"Series RLC"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A bank of lamps totals 9,000 W and burns 6 hours. At $0.13 per kWh, what does the run cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$415.38","$7,020.00","$7.02","$1.17"],"a":2,"e":"Convert watts to kilowatts: 9,000 / 1,000 = 9 kW. kWh = 9 x 6 = 54. Cost = 54 x $0.13 = $7.02. Recognise it: any watts-and-hours problem needs the /1,000 step before the rate is applied. Why the other choices are wrong: $7,020.00 - forgot to convert watts to kilowatts; $1.17 - left out the hours; $415.38 - divided by the rate.","r":"kWh cost"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":3,"q":"Three 12 ohm resistors are connected in delta on a 208 V three-phase supply. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["52.0 A","17.3 A","30.0 A","10.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"In a delta each resistor sees the full line voltage. Phase current = 208 / 12 = 17.33 A. Line current = 1.732 x 17.33 = 30.02 A. Recognise it: delta elements see line voltage, and the line carries 1.732 times the phase current. Why the other choices are wrong: 17.3 A - reported the phase current as the line current; 10.0 A - used wye voltage across a delta element; 52.0 A - multiplied by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"A single-phase 120 V circuit uses 14 AWG copper (4,110 CM, K = 12.9) to carry 12 A for 80 ft one way. What is the percent voltage drop, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","o":["6.0 percent","5.0 percent","5.3 percent","2.5 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Step 1: VD = 2 x 12.9 x 12 x 80 / 4,110 = 6.03 V. Step 2: % = VD / E x 100 = 6.03 / 120 x 100 = 5.02 percent. Recognise it: 'percent' means calculate volts of drop first, then divide by the source voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.5 percent - left out the 2 in 2KIL/CM; 6.0 percent - reported volts as a percent; 5.3 percent - divided by the load-end voltage instead of the source.","r":"Percent voltage drop"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"A 20 HP motor at 92 percent efficiency runs 24 hours at full load. Electricity costs $0.10 per kWh. What is the cost, to the nearest cent?","o":["$48.00","$38.92","$35.81","$32.94"],"a":1,"e":"Input kW = 20 x 0.746 / 0.92 = 16.217 kW. kWh = 16.217 x 24 = 389.22. Cost = 389.22 x 0.10 = $38.92. Recognise it: convert HP to kW, divide by efficiency to get input, then multiply by hours and rate. Why the other choices are wrong: $35.81 - ignored efficiency; $32.94 - multiplied by efficiency instead of dividing; $48.00 - used HP as if it were kW.","r":"HP and kWh"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":2,"q":"When using a voltage tester, the safest technique to check for voltage is","o":["test line-to-ground, line-to-line and line-to-neutral, with a known-source verification","check once and continue","use a non-contact tester only","test only between hot and neutral"],"a":0,"e":"Check all combinations; a missing neutral or reverse polarity could leave voltage elsewhere. A non-contact tester is only a quick indicator, not proof.","r":"Test before touch"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"What is the full-load current on the 480 V side of a 30 kVA three-phase transformer, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["20.8 A","36.1 A","62.5 A","108.3 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / (1.732 x E) = 30,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 36.09 A. Recognise it: a three-phase kVA rating to amps always includes 1.732 and the voltage of the winding you are asking about. Why the other choices are wrong: 62.5 A - forgot 1.732; 20.8 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 108.3 A - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"Kirchhoff's laws","d":1,"q":"Kirchhoff's current law states that","o":["current in a node always splits equally","the sum of voltage drops is zero for any single resistor","current is the same in all parallel branches","the current entering a junction equals the current leaving it"],"a":3,"e":"Charge cannot accumulate at a node, so currents in equal currents out. Used for parallel branch currents. The statement about voltage drops is the voltage law, not the current law.","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"A feeder conductor has 0.20 ohms of total resistance and carries 60 A. How much power is lost as heat in the conductor, to the nearest watt?","o":["18,000 W","12 W","24 W","720 W"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: P = I squared x R. Substitute: P = 60 x 60 x 0.20 = 720.0 W, rounded 720 W. Recognise it: current and conductor resistance known, wanted is heat loss, so use I squared R. Doubling the current quadruples the loss. Why the other choices are wrong: 12 W - did not square the current (I x R is volts); 18,000 W - divided I squared by R; 24 W - doubled I x R instead of squaring I.","r":"P = I^2 x R"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"What is the time for one complete cycle of a 50 Hz supply, in milliseconds to the nearest hundredth?","o":["3.18 ms","40.00 ms","20.00 ms","10.00 ms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: T = 1 / f = 1 / 50 = 0.02000 s = 20.00 ms. Recognise it: period is the inverse of frequency; at 60 Hz there are 60 cycles per second. Why the other choices are wrong: 3.18 ms - used 1/(2 pi f), the angular relationship; 40.00 ms - doubled the period; 10.00 ms - reported a half-cycle.","r":"T = 1/f"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"A heater is rated 1,500 W at 120 V. What is its hot resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["4.8 ohms","12.5 ohms","19.2 ohms","9.6 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: R = E squared / P. Substitute: R = 120 x 120 / 1,500 = 9.60 ohms, about 9.6 ohms. Recognise it: nameplate watts and volts are given and resistance is wanted, so use R = E squared / P. Why the other choices are wrong: 4.8 ohms - halved the result; 12.5 ohms - used P / E, which is amps, not ohms; 19.2 ohms - doubled the result.","r":"R = E^2 / P"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A capacitor in a DC circuit, after it is fully charged, behaves like","o":["a resistor equal to its reactance","a short circuit","a transformer","an open circuit"],"a":3,"e":"Once charged to the source voltage, current stops. Capacitors block DC and pass AC (with XC dependent on frequency).","r":"Capacitors"},{"c":"Kirchhoff's laws","d":1,"q":"At a junction, 9 A and 6 A flow in and 10 A flows out on one conductor. A fourth conductor also leaves the junction. What current flows in the fourth conductor?","o":["25 A","7 A","3 A","5 A"],"a":3,"e":"Kirchhoff's current law: current in = current out. In = 9 + 6 = 15 A. Out = 10 + I4, so I4 = 15 - 10 = 5 A. Recognise it: any problem about currents meeting at a point; add everything entering and everything leaving and set them equal. Why the other choices are wrong: 25 A - added all three currents; 7 A - mixed up which currents enter and leave; 3 A - ignored the third current.","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A 7.5 kW electric heat strip is running. How many BTU per hour does it produce? (1 kW = 3,412 BTU/hr)","o":["5,595 BTU/hr","7,500 BTU/hr","25,590 BTU/hr","2,198 BTU/hr"],"a":2,"e":"Conversion: BTU/hr = kW x 3,412 = 7.5 x 3,412 = 25,590 BTU/hr. Recognise it: electric heat is 100 percent efficient, so every kW becomes 3,412 BTU/hr. Why the other choices are wrong: 7,500 BTU/hr - reported watts as BTU; 5,595 BTU/hr - used the 746 horsepower factor; 2,198 BTU/hr - divided by 3.412 instead of multiplying.","r":"1 kW = 3,412 BTU/hr"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"A wye system measures 600 V line-to-line. What is the line-to-neutral voltage, to the nearest volt? (Use 1.732.)","o":["346 V","600 V","300 V","1,039 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: E(L-N) = E(L-L) / 1.732 = 600 / 1.732 = 346.4 V, about 346 V. Recognise it: only a wye has a neutral; the phase voltage is the line voltage divided by 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,039 V - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing; 300 V - halved the line voltage; 600 V - assumed line-to-neutral equals line-to-line.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the inductive reactance of a 0.05 H coil at 400 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["62.8 ohms","251.3 ohms","20.0 ohms","125.7 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: XL = 2 x pi x f x L = 6.2832 x 400 x 0.05 = 125.66 ohms. Recognise it: an inductor opposes current more as frequency rises; multiply 2 pi f by henries. Why the other choices are wrong: 251.3 ohms - doubled the 2 pi f L result; 62.8 ohms - left out the 2; 20.0 ohms - multiplied only f x L (left out 2 pi).","r":"XL = 2 pi f L"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"An ideal transformer steps 480 V down to 240 V. If the primary current is 10 A, what is the secondary current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["11.5 A","5.0 A","20.0 A","10.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"Ideal transformer: VA in = VA out, so Ep x Ip = Es x Is. Is = Ip x Ep / Es = 10 x 480 / 240 = 20.00 A. Recognise it: step-down means more current on the low-voltage side, in exact inverse proportion. Why the other choices are wrong: 5.0 A - used the voltage ratio the wrong way round; 10.0 A - assumed the currents are equal; 11.5 A - divided by 1.732 on a single-phase unit.","r":"Transformer current ratio"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":1,"q":"A 120 V source feeds a load through conductors with a 3.6 V drop. What voltage is available at the load?","o":["116.4 V","123.6 V","3.6 V","115.7 V"],"a":0,"e":"Load voltage = source - drop = 120 - 3.6 = 116.4 V. Recognise it: conductor resistance always costs voltage; subtract the drop from the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 123.6 V - added the drop; 115.7 V - treated the drop as a percent; 3.6 V - gave the drop itself.","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A circuit draws 15.0 A from a 277 V supply. What is the load resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["4,155.0 ohms","9.2 ohms","18.5 ohms","262.0 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: R = E / I. Substitute: R = 277 / 15.0 = 18.47 ohms, or 18.5 ohms rounded. Recognise it: voltage and current known, resistance wanted, so divide E by I. Why the other choices are wrong: 9.2 ohms - used twice the current; 4,155.0 ohms - multiplied E x I, which is watts, not ohms; 262.0 ohms - subtracted current from voltage.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"A solid round conductor is 102 mils in diameter. What is its area in circular mils?","o":["32,685 CM","10,404 CM","204 CM","8,171 CM"],"a":1,"e":"One circular mil is the area of a circle 1 mil in diameter, so CM = d squared = 102 x 102 = 10,404 CM. Recognise it: circular mil area is simply the diameter in mils, squared; no pi is needed. Why the other choices are wrong: 204 CM - doubled the diameter; 8,171 CM - used the square-mil area formula (pi d squared / 4); 32,685 CM - multiplied by pi.","r":"Circular mil"},{"c":"Motors","d":1,"q":"A standard 4-pole induction motor on 60 Hz operates at a full-load speed of about","o":["1,800 RPM exactly","1,750 RPM","900 RPM","3,600 RPM"],"a":1,"e":"Synchronous speed is 1,800 RPM. The rotor must lag a little (slip) to induce current, giving 1,725 to 1,780 RPM. 3,600 is a 2-pole motor and 900 an 8-pole.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A three-phase induction motor turns the wrong way. How can the direction be reversed?","o":["Reverse the neutral and ground conductors","Swap any two of the three line conductors","Replace the fuses with breakers","Increase the supply voltage"],"a":1,"e":"Direction depends on the phase sequence. Swapping any two line leads reverses the rotating field. The neutral and ground do not affect rotation, and voltage or fuse changes will not reverse it.","r":"Phase rotation"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A conductor has a 90 C ampacity of 30 A. It is bundled with 5 current-carrying conductors in one conduit. Using the adjustment factor (80 percent for 5 conductors), what is the adjusted ampacity, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["24.0 A","37.5 A","6.0 A","30.0 A"],"a":0,"e":"Adjusted ampacity = 30 x 0.8 = 24.0 A. Recognise it: 4-6 conductors gets 80 percent, 7-9 gets 70 percent, 10-20 gets 50 percent; multiply the table ampacity by that factor. Why the other choices are wrong: 30.0 A - did not apply any adjustment; 37.5 A - divided by the factor instead of multiplying; 6.0 A - multiplied by the percent removed.","r":"Table 310.15(C)(1)"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A 277 V source is connected across a 40 ohm resistive load. What is the current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["3.5 A","6.9 A","12.0 A","0.1 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = E / R. Substitute: I = 277 / 40 = 6.92 A, which rounds to 6.9 A. Recognise it: you know voltage and resistance and want current, so put the unknown on top of the Ohm's law triangle and divide the other two. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.1 A - inverted the formula (R / E); 12.0 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase calculation; 3.5 A - used twice the resistance.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 167 degrees F to degrees C, to the nearest degree.","o":["333 C","61 C","135 C","75 C"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: C = (F - 32) / 1.8 = (167 - 32) / 1.8 = 135 / 1.8 = 75.0 C. Recognise it: subtract 32 first, then divide by 1.8 (multiply by 5/9). Why the other choices are wrong: 135 C - subtracted 32 but did not divide by 1.8; 61 C - divided before subtracting 32; 333 C - used the Celsius-to-Fahrenheit formula.","r":"C = (F - 32) / 1.8"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"What is the full-load secondary current of a 75 kVA single-phase transformer with a 240 V secondary, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["18.0 A","390.6 A","180.4 A","312.5 A"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / E = 75,000 / 240 = 312.50 A. Recognise it: single-phase transformer rating to amps is VA divided by volts, no 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 180.4 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase transformer; 390.6 A - assumed an 0.8 power factor; 18.0 A - multiplied instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"Three resistors of 10, 20 and 30 ohms are connected in series across 120 V. What is the circuit current, to the nearest hundredth of an amp?","o":["12.00 A","6.00 A","22.00 A","2.00 A"],"a":3,"e":"In series, Rt = R1 + R2 + R3 = 10 + 20 + 30 = 60 ohms. Then I = E / Rt = 120 / 60 = 2.000 A, about 2.00 A. Recognise it: one path for the current, so resistances add and the same current flows everywhere. Why the other choices are wrong: 22.00 A - added the resistors as if they were in parallel; 12.00 A - used only the first resistor; 6.00 A - used the average resistance.","r":"Series circuit"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 100 kW load operates at 0.8 PF. How many kVAR of capacitors are needed to raise the power factor to 0.95, to the nearest kVAR?","o":["15 kVAR","20 kVAR","75 kVAR","42 kVAR"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: kVAR(cap) = kW x (tan of old angle - tan of new angle). Old angle = arccos(0.8) = 36.9 deg, tan = 0.750. New angle = arccos(0.95) = 18.2 deg, tan = 0.329. kVAR = 100 x (0.750 - 0.329) = 42.1. Recognise it: capacitors supply reactive power; subtract the reactive demand before and after correction. Why the other choices are wrong: 20 kVAR - used kW x (1 - PF); 15 kVAR - multiplied by the PF difference; 75 kVAR - removed all reactive power regardless of the target.","r":"PF correction"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"Which devices commonly cause a lagging power factor in electrical systems?","o":["Induction motors and transformers","Incandescent lamps","Resistance heaters","Capacitor banks"],"a":0,"e":"Inductive loads need magnetizing current that lags voltage. Resistive loads have a PF near 1.0, and capacitors produce a leading current that is used to correct lagging PF.","r":"Power factor"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"Which load is most affected by low voltage caused by voltage drop?","o":["An incandescent lamp, which becomes brighter","A motor, which draws more current as voltage falls","A resistance heater, which draws more power","A fuse, which increases its rating"],"a":1,"e":"Motors draw increased current when voltage is low, causing overheating. Resistive loads simply produce less power (P = E squared / R), and lamps dim.","r":"Voltage drop effects"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"A 4 ohm resistor is in series with two resistors (18 ohm and 9 ohm) that are in parallel with each other. What is the total circuit resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["2.4 ohms","6.0 ohms","31.0 ohms","10.0 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Step 1, reduce the parallel pair: (18 x 9) / (18 + 9) = 6.00 ohms. Step 2, add the series resistor: 4 + 6.00 = 10.00 ohms. Recognise it: a mix of series and parallel; always collapse the parallel groups first, then add the series parts. Why the other choices are wrong: 31.0 ohms - treated everything as series; 2.4 ohms - treated everything as parallel; 6.0 ohms - forgot to add the series resistor.","r":"Combination circuit"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"A circuit draws 2.5 A from a 24 V supply. What is the load resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["4.8 ohms","9.6 ohms","21.5 ohms","60.0 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: R = E / I. Substitute: R = 24 / 2.5 = 9.60 ohms, or 9.6 ohms rounded. Recognise it: voltage and current known, resistance wanted, so divide E by I. Why the other choices are wrong: 4.8 ohms - used twice the current; 60.0 ohms - multiplied E x I, which is watts, not ohms; 21.5 ohms - subtracted current from voltage.","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A 10 microfarad and a 40 microfarad capacitor are connected in series. What is the total capacitance, to the nearest tenth of a microfarad?","o":["10.0 microfarads","8.0 microfarads","50.0 microfarads","25.0 microfarads"],"a":1,"e":"Capacitors in series combine like resistors in parallel: Ct = (C1 x C2) / (C1 + C2) = (10 x 40) / (10 + 40) = 8.00 microfarads. Recognise it: capacitors are the opposite of resistors - parallel adds, series is the reciprocal formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 50.0 microfarads - added them (that is the parallel result); 25.0 microfarads - averaged the values; 10.0 microfarads - assumed the smaller capacitor alone governs.","r":"Capacitors in series"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"As the AWG number of a conductor gets larger, the conductor","o":["gets larger and has more resistance","gets larger and has less resistance","gets smaller in diameter and has more resistance","gets smaller and has less resistance"],"a":2,"e":"AWG numbers run opposite to size: 14 AWG is smaller than 10 AWG. Smaller area means higher resistance. 4/0 is the large end before kcmil sizes.","r":"AWG"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 500 kVA three-phase load operates at 480 V. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["1,804.2 A","347.2 A","601.4 A","1,041.7 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / (1.732 x E) = 500,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 500,000 / 831.4 = 601.42 A. Recognise it: kVA given for a three-phase system, find amps, so convert to VA and divide by 1.732 x line voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,041.7 A - forgot 1.732; 1,804.2 A - multiplied by 1.732 instead of dividing; 347.2 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"I = kVA x 1000 / (1.732 E)"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":2,"q":"What is the voltage drop on a single-phase circuit of 10 AWG copper (10,380 CM), carrying 30 A over 150 ft one way? Use K = 12.9. Round to the nearest tenth of a volt.","o":["11.2 V","5.6 V","9.7 V","22.4 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: VD = 2 x K x I x L / CM = 2 x 12.9 x 30 x 150 / 10,380 = 116,100.0 / 10,380 = 11.18 V. Recognise it: single-phase, K given, one-way length given; the 2 accounts for current going out and back. Why the other choices are wrong: 5.6 V - left out the 2 for the round trip; 9.7 V - used 1.732 (the three-phase multiplier); 22.4 V - doubled the length twice.","r":"VD = 2KIL/CM"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":1,"q":"An in-line ammeter is connected","o":["in parallel with the source","across the load","across the supply conductors","in series with the circuit"],"a":3,"e":"All the current must pass through the meter, so it is placed in series. Connecting it across a source would cause a short circuit. Clamp meters avoid breaking the circuit.","r":"Meter use"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"A 20 kW electric heat strip is running. How many BTU per hour does it produce? (1 kW = 3,412 BTU/hr)","o":["68,240 BTU/hr","14,920 BTU/hr","5,862 BTU/hr","20,000 BTU/hr"],"a":0,"e":"Conversion: BTU/hr = kW x 3,412 = 20 x 3,412 = 68,240 BTU/hr. Recognise it: electric heat is 100 percent efficient, so every kW becomes 3,412 BTU/hr. Why the other choices are wrong: 20,000 BTU/hr - reported watts as BTU; 14,920 BTU/hr - used the 746 horsepower factor; 5,862 BTU/hr - divided by 3.412 instead of multiplying.","r":"1 kW = 3,412 BTU/hr"},{"c":"Temperature","d":1,"q":"Convert 75 degrees C to degrees F, to the nearest degree.","o":["135 F","24 F","167 F","193 F"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: F = (C x 1.8) + 32 = (75 x 1.8) + 32 = 135 + 32 = 167 F. Recognise it: multiply by 1.8 first, then add 32. Why the other choices are wrong: 135 F - forgot to add 32; 193 F - added 32 before multiplying; 24 F - used the Fahrenheit-to-Celsius formula.","r":"F = 1.8 C + 32"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load draws 80 kW at 100 kVA. What is its reactive power, to the nearest tenth of a kVAR?","o":["180.0 kVAR","20.0 kVAR","128.1 kVAR","60.0 kVAR"],"a":3,"e":"The power triangle: kVA squared = kW squared + kVAR squared. kVAR = sqrt(100^2 - 80^2) = sqrt(3600) = 60.00 kVAR. Recognise it: kVA is the hypotenuse, so the missing side comes from the difference of squares. Why the other choices are wrong: 20.0 kVAR - subtracted kW from kVA directly; 180.0 kVAR - added kW and kVA; 128.1 kVAR - added the squares instead of subtracting.","r":"Power triangle"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":1,"q":"A relay is","o":["a form of fuse","a type of capacitor","an electrically operated switch controlled by a coil","a transformer that changes frequency"],"a":2,"e":"A small coil current closes contacts that switch a separate load circuit. A contactor is a heavy-duty relay for power loads such as motors.","r":"Relays"},{"c":"Transformers","d":3,"q":"A 480 V to 208Y/120 V transformer rated 75 kVA is loaded to 60 kVA. What percent loaded is it?","o":["125 percent","75 percent","80 percent","20 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Loading = 60 / 75 x 100 = 80 percent. 125 percent inverts the ratio (75/60), and 20 percent is the spare capacity. Recognise it: actual load over rating.","r":"Transformer loading"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A load draws 36 kW at 45 kVA. What is its reactive power, to the nearest tenth of a kVAR?","o":["9.0 kVAR","81.0 kVAR","27.0 kVAR","57.6 kVAR"],"a":2,"e":"The power triangle: kVA squared = kW squared + kVAR squared. kVAR = sqrt(45^2 - 36^2) = sqrt(729) = 27.00 kVAR. Recognise it: kVA is the hypotenuse, so the missing side comes from the difference of squares. Why the other choices are wrong: 9.0 kVAR - subtracted kW from kVA directly; 81.0 kVAR - added kW and kVA; 57.6 kVAR - added the squares instead of subtracting.","r":"Power triangle"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"A step-up transformer has","o":["the same voltage but isolated","lower secondary voltage and lower current","higher secondary voltage and lower secondary current than the primary","higher secondary voltage and higher secondary current"],"a":2,"e":"VA in equals VA out (ideal), so raising voltage lowers current in the same proportion. Power is not increased. This is why high-voltage transmission uses small conductors.","r":"Transformer current ratio"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"What is the approximate line current of a 20 HP, 460 V three-phase motor at 91 percent efficiency and 0.87 power factor? Use 1.732 and round to the nearest tenth of an amp.","o":["18.7 A","41.0 A","23.7 A","21.5 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = (HP x 746) / (1.732 x E x eff x PF). Numerator = 20 x 746 = 14,920. Denominator = 1.732 x 460 x 0.91 x 0.87 = 630.8. I = 23.65 A. Recognise it: horsepower to amps always needs 746, the 1.732 for three-phase, and both efficiency and PF in the denominator. Why the other choices are wrong: 41.0 A - forgot 1.732; 18.7 A - left out efficiency and PF; 21.5 A - left out efficiency.","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"The peak of a sine wave is 679 V. What is the RMS value, to the nearest volt?","o":["960 V","433 V","340 V","480 V"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: Vrms = 0.707 x Vpeak = 0.707 x 679 = 480.1 V. Recognise it: RMS is the DC-equivalent heating value and is always lower than peak. Why the other choices are wrong: 960 V - multiplied by 1.414 instead of 0.707; 433 V - used the average value factor; 340 V - halved the peak.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":1,"q":"A dwelling calculated load totals 24,000 VA on a 120/240 V single-phase service. What is the calculated service current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["200.0 A","100.0 A","115.4 A","57.7 A"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: I = VA / E = 24,000 / 240 = 100.00 A. Recognise it: a 120/240 V single-phase service is sized on 240 V; divide the total VA by 240. Why the other choices are wrong: 200.0 A - divided by 120 V instead of 240 V; 57.7 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase service; 115.4 A - divided by 208 V.","r":"I = VA / E"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"How long can a 10 AWG copper (10,380 CM) single-phase circuit carrying 24 A on 240 V run (one way) before the drop exceeds 3 percent? Use K = 12.9 and round to the nearest foot.","o":["60 ft","121 ft","139 ft","241 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Allowed drop = 240 x 0.03 = 7.20 V. Rearranged: L = VD x CM / (2 x K x I) = 7.20 x 10,380 / (2 x 12.9 x 24) = 120.7 ft. Recognise it: a maximum-distance question; solve the voltage-drop formula for L. Why the other choices are wrong: 241 ft - left out the 2 in the formula; 60 ft - applied the round-trip factor twice; 139 ft - used 1.732 for a single-phase run.","r":"L = VD x CM / 2KI"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A load is rated 50 kVA at 0.8 power factor. How many kW of real power does it use, to the nearest tenth?","o":["40.0 kW","30.0 kW","50.0 kW","62.5 kW"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: kW = kVA x PF = 50 x 0.8 = 40.00 kW. Recognise it: real power is always the smaller of kW and kVA; multiply kVA by PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 62.5 kW - divided kVA by PF; 50.0 kW - assumed unity power factor; 30.0 kW - calculated kVAR.","r":"kW = kVA x PF"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"In a balanced delta load each phase winding carries 50 A. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["86.6 A","50.0 A","150.0 A","28.9 A"],"a":0,"e":"Delta: I(line) = 1.732 x I(phase) = 1.732 x 50 = 86.60 A. Recognise it: in delta the line voltage equals phase voltage but line current is 1.732 times phase current. Why the other choices are wrong: 50.0 A - assumed line current equals phase current (that is wye); 28.9 A - divided by 1.732 instead of multiplying; 150.0 A - multiplied by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A 8-pole motor on 60 Hz turns at 855 RPM under load. What is the percent slip, to the nearest tenth?","o":["45.0 percent","5.3 percent","5.0 percent","95.0 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Synchronous speed Ns = 120 x 60 / 8 = 900 RPM. Slip = (Ns - Nr) / Ns x 100 = (900 - 855) / 900 x 100 = 5.00 percent. Recognise it: slip is always based on synchronous speed; find Ns first. Why the other choices are wrong: 5.3 percent - divided by rotor speed instead of synchronous speed; 45.0 percent - reported the RPM difference as a percent; 95.0 percent - reported rotor speed as a percent of synchronous.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"A three-phase panel supplies 225 A per phase at 208 V. What is the apparent power, to the nearest tenth of a kVA? (Use 1.732.)","o":["81,058 kVA","81.1 kVA","140.4 kVA","46.8 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: kVA = 1.732 x E x I / 1,000 = 1.732 x 208 x 225 / 1,000 = 81.06 kVA. Recognise it: volts and amps, three-phase, no PF mentioned, so apparent power is wanted. Why the other choices are wrong: 46.8 kVA - forgot 1.732; 140.4 kVA - multiplied by 3 instead of 1.732; 81,058 kVA - forgot to divide by 1,000.","r":"kVA = 1.732 E I / 1000"},{"c":"Motors","d":3,"q":"What is the approximate line current of a 10 HP, 460 V three-phase motor at 90 percent efficiency and 0.85 power factor? Use 1.732 and round to the nearest tenth of an amp.","o":["11.0 A","9.4 A","21.2 A","12.2 A"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: I = (HP x 746) / (1.732 x E x eff x PF). Numerator = 10 x 746 = 7,460. Denominator = 1.732 x 460 x 0.90 x 0.85 = 609.5. I = 12.24 A. Recognise it: horsepower to amps always needs 746, the 1.732 for three-phase, and both efficiency and PF in the denominator. Why the other choices are wrong: 21.2 A - forgot 1.732; 9.4 A - left out efficiency and PF; 11.0 A - left out efficiency.","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A storage-type water heater is rated 2,000 W at 240 V. Treating it as a continuous load (125 percent), what minimum branch-circuit ampacity is required, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["6.7 A","6.0 A","8.3 A","10.4 A"],"a":3,"e":"Current = 2,000 / 240 = 8.33 A. Branch circuit = 125 percent x 8.33 = 10.42 A. Recognise it: a continuous-load multiplier of 1.25 applies after you convert watts to amps. Why the other choices are wrong: 8.3 A - left out the 125 percent; 6.7 A - multiplied by 80 percent instead of 125 percent; 6.0 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase heater.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the capacitive reactance of a 20 microfarad capacitor at 60 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["833.3 ohms","0.133 ohms","265.3 ohms","132.6 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: XC = 1 / (2 x pi x f x C), with C in farads (20 microfarads = 2.00e-05 F). XC = 1 / (6.2832 x 60 x 2.00e-05) = 132.63 ohms. Recognise it: capacitors oppose current less as frequency rises; convert microfarads to farads first. Why the other choices are wrong: 833.3 ohms - left the 2 pi out of the denominator; 0.133 ohms - treated microfarads as millifarads; 265.3 ohms - used pi instead of 2 pi.","r":"XC = 1/(2 pi f C)"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"A sine wave peaks at 100 V. What is its average value over one half-cycle, to the nearest volt?","o":["64 V","71 V","141 V","50 V"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: Vavg = 0.637 x Vpeak = 0.637 x 100 = 63.7 V. Recognise it: three factors to remember - RMS 0.707, average 0.637, peak 1.414 (RMS to peak). Why the other choices are wrong: 71 V - used the RMS factor; 141 V - used the peak factor; 50 V - halved the peak.","r":"Sine wave values"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":1,"q":"A dwelling calculated load totals 28,800 VA on a 120/240 V single-phase service. What is the calculated service current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["120.0 A","138.5 A","69.3 A","240.0 A"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: I = VA / E = 28,800 / 240 = 120.00 A. Recognise it: a 120/240 V single-phase service is sized on 240 V; divide the total VA by 240. Why the other choices are wrong: 240.0 A - divided by 120 V instead of 240 V; 69.3 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase service; 138.5 A - divided by 208 V.","r":"I = VA / E"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":3,"q":"A 2,000 sq ft dwelling has general lighting at 3 VA per sq ft, two 1,500 VA small-appliance circuits and one 1,500 VA laundry circuit. Applying the standard demand factors (first 3,000 VA at 100 percent, the rest at 35 percent), what is the demand load, to the nearest VA?","o":["3,675 VA","5,625 VA","4,050 VA","10,500 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Connected load = 2,000 x 3 = 6,000 + 3,000 + 1,500 = 10,500 VA. Demand = 3,000 + 0.35 x (10,500 - 3,000) = 3,000 + 2,625.0 = 5,625.0 VA. Recognise it: a dwelling lighting-and-receptacle demand problem; add the three loads, then take 100 percent of the first 3,000 VA and 35 percent of the remainder. Why the other choices are wrong: 10,500 VA - skipped the demand factors; 3,675 VA - applied 35 percent to the whole load; 4,050 VA - left out the small-appliance and laundry circuits.","r":"Dwelling demand factors"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":1,"q":"What is the first rule when working on electrical equipment?","o":["Rely on the panel directory","Treat all conductors as energized until you have proven them de-energized","Test with the back of the hand","Assume the circuit is off if the breaker is off"],"a":1,"e":"A mislabeled panel, backfed circuit or wrong breaker can leave conductors live. Verify with a properly rated voltage tester.","r":"Electrical safety"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 120 V AC source feeds a series circuit of 6 ohms resistance and 8 ohms inductive reactance. What is the current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["15.0 A","20.0 A","8.6 A","12.0 A"],"a":3,"e":"Impedance Z = sqrt(6^2 + 8^2) = 10.00 ohms. Current I = E / Z = 120 / 10.00 = 12.00 A. Recognise it: Ohm's law for AC uses impedance instead of resistance; find Z first. Why the other choices are wrong: 8.6 A - added R and X directly; 20.0 A - ignored the reactance; 15.0 A - ignored the resistance.","r":"I = E / Z"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"Two resistive loads of 15 ohms and 60 ohms are connected in parallel across 120 V. What is the total current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["3.2 A","10.0 A","8.0 A","1.6 A"],"a":1,"e":"Each branch sees the full 120 V. I1 = 120 / 15 = 8.00 A and I2 = 120 / 60 = 2.00 A. Total = 10.00 A (Kirchhoff's current law). Recognise it: in parallel, voltage is common and branch currents add. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.6 A - used series resistance; 8.0 A - counted only the first branch; 3.2 A - used the average resistance.","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"Two resistive loads of 6 ohms and 12 ohms are connected in parallel across 24 V. What is the total current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["4.0 A","1.3 A","6.0 A","2.7 A"],"a":2,"e":"Each branch sees the full 24 V. I1 = 24 / 6 = 4.00 A and I2 = 24 / 12 = 2.00 A. Total = 6.00 A (Kirchhoff's current law). Recognise it: in parallel, voltage is common and branch currents add. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.3 A - used series resistance; 4.0 A - counted only the first branch; 2.7 A - used the average resistance.","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Transformers","d":1,"q":"What is the full-load secondary current of a 25 kVA single-phase transformer with a 240 V secondary, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["104.2 A","60.1 A","6.0 A","130.2 A"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: I = (kVA x 1,000) / E = 25,000 / 240 = 104.17 A. Recognise it: single-phase transformer rating to amps is VA divided by volts, no 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 60.1 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase transformer; 130.2 A - assumed an 0.8 power factor; 6.0 A - multiplied instead of dividing.","r":"Transformer FLA"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A load uses 45 kW of true power and 60 kVA of apparent power. What is its power factor, to two decimals?","o":["0.66","15.00","0.75","1.33"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: PF = kW / kVA = 45 / 60 = 0.750, or 0.75. Recognise it: true power over apparent power; the answer can never exceed 1.0. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.33 - inverted the ratio; 15.00 - subtracted instead of dividing; 0.66 - reported the sine of the angle (reactive ratio).","r":"PF = kW / kVA"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":2,"q":"Why can 120 V be lethal?","o":["Because dry skin has zero resistance","Current through the body depends on voltage and body resistance, and even a small current can disrupt the heart","Because voltage alone is harmless","Because 120 V always produces 20 A"],"a":1,"e":"I = E / R: with wet skin and low body resistance of 1,000 ohms, 120 V could push 120 mA - in the fibrillation range. Dry skin resistance is much higher, but contact conditions vary.","r":"Shock effects"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A 7.5 HP motor is 88 percent efficient. How many watts does it draw at full load, to the nearest watt?","o":["4,924 W","5,595 W","8,523 W","6,358 W"],"a":3,"e":"Output watts = 7.5 x 746 = 5,595 W. Input = output / efficiency = 5,595 / 0.88 = 6,358.0 W. Recognise it: a motor always draws more than it delivers; divide output by efficiency. Why the other choices are wrong: 5,595 W - ignored efficiency (that is output watts); 4,924 W - multiplied by efficiency instead of dividing; 8,523 W - used 1,000 W per HP.","r":"HP = 746 W; efficiency"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"When frequency increases, capacitive reactance","o":["decreases","doubles","increases","stays the same"],"a":0,"e":"XC = 1 / (2 pi f C), inversely proportional to frequency. A capacitor blocks DC (infinite reactance) and passes higher frequencies more easily.","r":"XC = 1 / 2 pi f C"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"What is the time for one complete cycle of a 400 Hz supply, in milliseconds to the nearest hundredth?","o":["5.00 ms","0.40 ms","2.50 ms","1.25 ms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: T = 1 / f = 1 / 400 = 0.00250 s = 2.50 ms. Recognise it: period is the inverse of frequency; at 60 Hz there are 60 cycles per second. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.40 ms - used 1/(2 pi f), the angular relationship; 5.00 ms - doubled the period; 1.25 ms - reported a half-cycle.","r":"T = 1/f"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"What is the time for one complete cycle of a 60 Hz supply, in milliseconds to the nearest hundredth?","o":["2.65 ms","8.33 ms","16.67 ms","33.33 ms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: T = 1 / f = 1 / 60 = 0.01667 s = 16.67 ms. Recognise it: period is the inverse of frequency; at 60 Hz there are 60 cycles per second. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.65 ms - used 1/(2 pi f), the angular relationship; 33.33 ms - doubled the period; 8.33 ms - reported a half-cycle.","r":"T = 1/f"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"At the resonant frequency of a series RLC circuit","o":["XL equals XC and the impedance is at its minimum (equal to R)","the impedance is at its maximum","XC is infinite","XL is zero"],"a":0,"e":"At resonance the opposite reactances cancel, leaving only R. Current is therefore maximum in a series circuit. A parallel resonant circuit is the one with maximum impedance.","r":"Resonance"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":1,"q":"If the length of a circuit is doubled and everything else stays the same, the voltage drop","o":["is cut in half","stays the same","doubles","quadruples"],"a":2,"e":"VD is directly proportional to length, VD = 2KIL/CM. Doubling L doubles VD. Doubling current also doubles it; doubling circular mils halves it.","r":"VD = 2KIL/CM"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"Resistors of 10, 20 and 20 ohms are connected in parallel. What is the total resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["16.7 ohms","6.7 ohms","50.0 ohms","5.0 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: 1/Rt = 1/10 + 1/20 + 1/20 = 0.2000. Rt = 1 / 0.2000 = 5.00 ohms. Recognise it: three branches across one source; answer must be below the smallest branch (10 ohms) - use that as a check. Why the other choices are wrong: 50.0 ohms - added the values (series); 16.7 ohms - took the average; 6.7 ohms - paralleled only the two smallest resistors.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"A single open (burned-out) element in a series string of lights causes","o":["the remaining lights to get brighter","the voltage to drop to zero at the source","all of the lights to go out","only that light to go out"],"a":2,"e":"A series circuit is one path; an open stops current everywhere. The full source voltage appears across the open. In a parallel circuit only the open branch goes dark.","r":"Series circuit"},{"c":"Motors","d":1,"q":"What is the synchronous speed of a 2-pole induction motor on a 60 Hz supply?","o":["1,800 RPM","4 RPM","3,600 RPM","14,400 RPM"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: Ns = 120 x f / P = 120 x 60 / 2 = 7,200 / 2 = 3,600 RPM. Recognise it: the question gives poles and frequency and asks for speed; the 120 already accounts for pole pairs. Why the other choices are wrong: 1,800 RPM - used 60 instead of 120; 4 RPM - inverted poles and frequency; 14,400 RPM - divided by pole pairs instead of poles.","r":"Ns = 120f/P"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":3,"q":"Three 15 ohm resistors are connected in delta on a 240 V three-phase supply. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["27.7 A","16.0 A","48.0 A","9.2 A"],"a":0,"e":"In a delta each resistor sees the full line voltage. Phase current = 240 / 15 = 16.00 A. Line current = 1.732 x 16.00 = 27.71 A. Recognise it: delta elements see line voltage, and the line carries 1.732 times the phase current. Why the other choices are wrong: 16.0 A - reported the phase current as the line current; 9.2 A - used wye voltage across a delta element; 48.0 A - multiplied by 3 instead of 1.732.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"How long can a 14 AWG copper (4,110 CM) single-phase circuit carrying 12 A on 120 V run (one way) before the drop exceeds 3 percent? Use K = 12.9 and round to the nearest foot.","o":["24 ft","55 ft","96 ft","48 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Allowed drop = 120 x 0.03 = 3.60 V. Rearranged: L = VD x CM / (2 x K x I) = 3.60 x 4,110 / (2 x 12.9 x 12) = 47.8 ft. Recognise it: a maximum-distance question; solve the voltage-drop formula for L. Why the other choices are wrong: 96 ft - left out the 2 in the formula; 24 ft - applied the round-trip factor twice; 55 ft - used 1.732 for a single-phase run.","r":"L = VD x CM / 2KI"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":1,"q":"A 240 V source feeds a load through conductors with a 9.8 V drop. What voltage is available at the load?","o":["249.8 V","230.2 V","216.5 V","9.8 V"],"a":1,"e":"Load voltage = source - drop = 240 - 9.8 = 230.2 V. Recognise it: conductor resistance always costs voltage; subtract the drop from the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 249.8 V - added the drop; 216.5 V - treated the drop as a percent; 9.8 V - gave the drop itself.","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Load and ampacity math","d":2,"q":"A storage-type water heater is rated 4,500 W at 240 V. Treating it as a continuous load (125 percent), what minimum branch-circuit ampacity is required, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["15.0 A","23.4 A","18.8 A","13.5 A"],"a":1,"e":"Current = 4,500 / 240 = 18.75 A. Branch circuit = 125 percent x 18.75 = 23.44 A. Recognise it: a continuous-load multiplier of 1.25 applies after you convert watts to amps. Why the other choices are wrong: 18.8 A - left out the 125 percent; 15.0 A - multiplied by 80 percent instead of 125 percent; 13.5 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase heater.","r":"125% continuous load"},{"c":"Grounding and bonding theory","d":2,"q":"Grounding the electrical system to earth primarily helps to","o":["increase the efficiency of loads","limit voltage from lightning, line surges or contact with higher-voltage lines","clear faults without overcurrent devices","eliminate neutral current"],"a":1,"e":"Earth connection stabilizes voltage relative to ground. It does not serve as the path to clear faults; that is the job of the bonded metallic path.","r":"System grounding"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"A single-phase 240 V circuit uses 8 AWG copper (16,510 CM, K = 12.9) to carry 40 A for 150 ft one way. What is the percent voltage drop, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","o":["4.1 percent","2.0 percent","9.4 percent","3.9 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Step 1: VD = 2 x 12.9 x 40 x 150 / 16,510 = 9.38 V. Step 2: % = VD / E x 100 = 9.38 / 240 x 100 = 3.91 percent. Recognise it: 'percent' means calculate volts of drop first, then divide by the source voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.0 percent - left out the 2 in 2KIL/CM; 9.4 percent - reported volts as a percent; 4.1 percent - divided by the load-end voltage instead of the source.","r":"Percent voltage drop"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A meter reads 240 V RMS on an AC circuit. What is the peak voltage, to the nearest volt?","o":["153 V","170 V","339 V","480 V"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: Vpeak = 1.414 x Vrms = 1.414 x 240 = 339.4 V. Recognise it: the sine wave peaks higher than its RMS (heating) value; peak is 1.414 times RMS. Why the other choices are wrong: 170 V - multiplied by 0.707 (that converts peak to RMS); 153 V - used the average factor; 480 V - doubled the RMS value.","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 15 kW three-phase resistive heater is connected to 208 V. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["41.6 A","124.9 A","72.1 A","24.0 A"],"a":0,"e":"Resistive, so PF = 1. I = W / (1.732 x E) = 15,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 41.64 A. Recognise it: kW and three-phase voltage, find amps, so divide by 1.732 x E. Why the other choices are wrong: 72.1 A - treated it as single-phase; 24.0 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 124.9 A - multiplied by 1.732.","r":"I = P / (1.732 E)"},{"c":"Grounding and bonding theory","d":2,"q":"Touch voltage is","o":["voltage inside a transformer","voltage drop on the neutral only","the voltage a person can contact between a grounded object and another point","the voltage of the battery"],"a":2,"e":"If a fault energizes metal, the potential difference between that metal and the earth where you stand is the touch voltage. Bonding keeps this low and the OCPD clears the fault quickly.","r":"Touch voltage"},{"c":"Power","d":1,"q":"A 1,500 W, 120 V single-phase resistive load is installed. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["6.3 A","7.2 A","12.5 A","15.6 A"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: I = P / E (resistive, PF = 1.0). Substitute: I = 1,500 / 120 = 12.50 A, about 12.5 A. Recognise it: watts and volts known, single-phase, so divide watts by volts. Only use 1.732 for three-phase. Why the other choices are wrong: 15.6 A - assumed an 0.8 power factor for a resistive load; 6.3 A - divided by the wrong voltage; 7.2 A - applied 1.732 to a single-phase load.","r":"I = P / E"},{"c":"Power","d":2,"q":"A feeder conductor has 0.40 ohms of total resistance and carries 20 A. How much power is lost as heat in the conductor, to the nearest watt?","o":["16 W","160 W","1,000 W","8 W"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: P = I squared x R. Substitute: P = 20 x 20 x 0.40 = 160.0 W, rounded 160 W. Recognise it: current and conductor resistance known, wanted is heat loss, so use I squared R. Doubling the current quadruples the loss. Why the other choices are wrong: 8 W - did not square the current (I x R is volts); 1,000 W - divided I squared by R; 16 W - doubled I x R instead of squaring I.","r":"P = I^2 x R"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":1,"q":"A 240 V source feeds a load through conductors with a 7.2 V drop. What voltage is available at the load?","o":["7.2 V","232.8 V","222.7 V","247.2 V"],"a":1,"e":"Load voltage = source - drop = 240 - 7.2 = 232.8 V. Recognise it: conductor resistance always costs voltage; subtract the drop from the source. Why the other choices are wrong: 247.2 V - added the drop; 222.7 V - treated the drop as a percent; 7.2 V - gave the drop itself.","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"Three resistors of 6, 14 and 20 ohms are connected in series across 24 V. What is the circuit current, to the nearest hundredth of an amp?","o":["0.60 A","1.80 A","4.00 A","6.91 A"],"a":0,"e":"In series, Rt = R1 + R2 + R3 = 6 + 14 + 20 = 40 ohms. Then I = E / Rt = 24 / 40 = 0.600 A, about 0.60 A. Recognise it: one path for the current, so resistances add and the same current flows everywhere. Why the other choices are wrong: 6.91 A - added the resistors as if they were in parallel; 4.00 A - used only the first resistor; 1.80 A - used the average resistance.","r":"Series circuit"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A 60 kW three-phase resistive heater is connected to 208 V. What is the line current, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["288.5 A","166.5 A","499.6 A","96.2 A"],"a":1,"e":"Resistive, so PF = 1. I = W / (1.732 x E) = 60,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 166.55 A. Recognise it: kW and three-phase voltage, find amps, so divide by 1.732 x E. Why the other choices are wrong: 288.5 A - treated it as single-phase; 96.2 A - divided by 3 instead of 1.732; 499.6 A - multiplied by 1.732.","r":"I = P / (1.732 E)"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":2,"q":"Which fire extinguisher class is suitable for energized electrical equipment?","o":["Class B only","Water extinguisher","Class C (or ABC multipurpose)","Class A only"],"a":2,"e":"Class C is for energized electrical fires - non-conductive agents. Water is a shock hazard. Once power is cut, other classes may apply.","r":"Fire safety"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":2,"q":"Resistors of 12, 24 and 8 ohms are connected in parallel. What is the total resistance, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["4.8 ohms","14.7 ohms","4.0 ohms","44.0 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: 1/Rt = 1/12 + 1/24 + 1/8 = 0.2500. Rt = 1 / 0.2500 = 4.00 ohms. Recognise it: three branches across one source; answer must be below the smallest branch (8 ohms) - use that as a check. Why the other choices are wrong: 44.0 ohms - added the values (series); 14.7 ohms - took the average; 4.8 ohms - paralleled only the two smallest resistors.","r":"Parallel resistance"},{"c":"Safety basics","d":1,"q":"Which sequence correctly describes the first steps of lockout/tagout?","o":["Identify sources, notify affected people, shut off and lock out, then verify de-energization","Remove the fuse and begin work immediately","Tag the switch and test with a neon light only","Lock out only after starting the job"],"a":0,"e":"LOTO: plan and notify, shut down, isolate, apply locks and tags, release stored energy, and verify zero energy with a tester before touching. A tag alone is not enough.","r":"Lockout/tagout"},{"c":"Motors","d":1,"q":"What is the synchronous speed of a 4-pole induction motor on a 400 Hz supply?","o":["6,000 RPM","24,000 RPM","1 RPM","12,000 RPM"],"a":3,"e":"Formula: Ns = 120 x f / P = 120 x 400 / 4 = 48,000 / 4 = 12,000 RPM. Recognise it: the question gives poles and frequency and asks for speed; the 120 already accounts for pole pairs. Why the other choices are wrong: 6,000 RPM - used 60 instead of 120; 1 RPM - inverted poles and frequency; 24,000 RPM - divided by pole pairs instead of poles.","r":"Ns = 120f/P"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":2,"q":"A delta-connected load draws 17.32 A in each line. What is the current in each phase winding, to the nearest tenth of an amp? (Use 1.732.)","o":["5.8 A","30.0 A","10.0 A","17.3 A"],"a":2,"e":"Delta: I(phase) = I(line) / 1.732 = 17.32 / 1.732 = 10.00 A. Recognise it: phase current is the smaller number in a delta; line current is 1.732 times larger. Why the other choices are wrong: 17.3 A - assumed phase current equals line current; 30.0 A - multiplied by 1.732; 5.8 A - divided by 3.","r":"Delta relationships"},{"c":"Grounding and bonding theory","d":1,"q":"The main purpose of the equipment grounding conductor is to","o":["reduce the line frequency","lower the voltage drop","carry normal load current","provide a low-impedance fault path so the overcurrent device opens quickly"],"a":3,"e":"In a fault, current flows through the EGC back to the source and trips the breaker. The earth is not a good fault path - the resistance is too high to trip a 20 A breaker.","r":"Fault current path"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"Chapter 9 Table 8 gives 4 AWG uncoated copper as 0.308 ohms per 1,000 ft. What is the resistance of 800 ft of this conductor, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["0.25 ohms","246.40 ohms","0.39 ohms","2.46 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Scale by length: R = 0.308 x 800 / 1,000 = 0.246 ohms. Recognise it: Table 8 resistance is per 1,000 ft, so multiply by (length / 1,000). Why the other choices are wrong: 246.40 ohms - forgot to divide by 1,000; 2.46 ohms - divided by 100; 0.39 ohms - inverted the length ratio.","r":"NEC Chapter 9 Table 8"},{"c":"Transformers","d":2,"q":"The kVA rating of a transformer is used instead of kW because","o":["kW is harder to calculate for a transformer","the transformer heat depends on current, regardless of power factor","transformer loads are always resistive","kVA is a metric unit and kW is not"],"a":1,"e":"Winding heating depends on current (I squared R) and voltage-limited insulation, so the limit is volt-amps. A load with low PF still draws full current from the transformer.","r":"Transformer rating"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":1,"q":"A solid round conductor is 64 mils in diameter. What is its area in circular mils?","o":["3,217 CM","12,868 CM","4,096 CM","128 CM"],"a":2,"e":"One circular mil is the area of a circle 1 mil in diameter, so CM = d squared = 64 x 64 = 4,096 CM. Recognise it: circular mil area is simply the diameter in mils, squared; no pi is needed. Why the other choices are wrong: 128 CM - doubled the diameter; 3,217 CM - used the square-mil area formula (pi d squared / 4); 12,868 CM - multiplied by pi.","r":"Circular mil"},{"c":"Energy and cost","d":1,"q":"One horsepower is equal to approximately","o":["746 watts","550 watts","3,412 watts","1,000 watts"],"a":0,"e":"1 HP = 746 W (by definition about 0.746 kW). 550 is foot-pounds per second, and 3,412 is BTU/hr per kW. Example: a 10 HP motor delivers 7,460 W at the shaft.","r":"1 HP = 746 W"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A copper conductor measures 1.0 ohms at 20 C. Using a temperature coefficient of 0.00393 per C, what is its resistance at 75 C, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["0.78 ohms","0.22 ohms","1.22 ohms","1.29 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Formula: R2 = R1 x [1 + alpha x (T2 - T1)] = 1.0 x [1 + 0.00393 x (75 - 20)] = 1.0 x 1.2162 = 1.216 ohms. Recognise it: copper resistance rises with temperature; use the change in temperature, not the final temperature. Why the other choices are wrong: 0.78 ohms - subtracted the temperature rise (resistance falls); 1.29 ohms - used the final temperature instead of the change; 0.22 ohms - left out the original resistance term.","r":"Resistance vs temperature"},{"c":"Conductors and resistance","d":3,"q":"A copper conductor measures 1.5 ohms at 20 C. Using a temperature coefficient of 0.00393 per C, what is its resistance at 90 C, to the nearest hundredth of an ohm?","o":["1.91 ohms","1.09 ohms","2.03 ohms","0.41 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: R2 = R1 x [1 + alpha x (T2 - T1)] = 1.5 x [1 + 0.00393 x (90 - 20)] = 1.5 x 1.2751 = 1.913 ohms. Recognise it: copper resistance rises with temperature; use the change in temperature, not the final temperature. Why the other choices are wrong: 1.09 ohms - subtracted the temperature rise (resistance falls); 2.03 ohms - used the final temperature instead of the change; 0.41 ohms - left out the original resistance term.","r":"Resistance vs temperature"},{"c":"Circuit components","d":2,"q":"A 15 microfarad and a 10 microfarad capacitor are connected in series. What is the total capacitance, to the nearest tenth of a microfarad?","o":["6.0 microfarads","10.0 microfarads","12.5 microfarads","25.0 microfarads"],"a":0,"e":"Capacitors in series combine like resistors in parallel: Ct = (C1 x C2) / (C1 + C2) = (15 x 10) / (15 + 10) = 6.00 microfarads. Recognise it: capacitors are the opposite of resistors - parallel adds, series is the reciprocal formula. Why the other choices are wrong: 25.0 microfarads - added them (that is the parallel result); 12.5 microfarads - averaged the values; 10.0 microfarads - assumed the smaller capacitor alone governs.","r":"Capacitors in series"},{"c":"Voltage drop","d":3,"q":"A single-phase 240 V circuit uses 6 AWG copper (26,240 CM, K = 12.9) to carry 50 A for 200 ft one way. What is the percent voltage drop, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","o":["4.1 percent","9.8 percent","4.3 percent","2.0 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Step 1: VD = 2 x 12.9 x 50 x 200 / 26,240 = 9.83 V. Step 2: % = VD / E x 100 = 9.83 / 240 x 100 = 4.10 percent. Recognise it: 'percent' means calculate volts of drop first, then divide by the source voltage. Why the other choices are wrong: 2.0 percent - left out the 2 in 2KIL/CM; 9.8 percent - reported volts as a percent; 4.3 percent - divided by the load-end voltage instead of the source.","r":"Percent voltage drop"},{"c":"AC theory","d":2,"q":"What is the capacitive reactance of a 50 microfarad capacitor at 60 Hz, to the nearest tenth of an ohm?","o":["53.1 ohms","0.053 ohms","106.1 ohms","333.3 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: XC = 1 / (2 x pi x f x C), with C in farads (50 microfarads = 5.00e-05 F). XC = 1 / (6.2832 x 60 x 5.00e-05) = 53.05 ohms. Recognise it: capacitors oppose current less as frequency rises; convert microfarads to farads first. Why the other choices are wrong: 333.3 ohms - left the 2 pi out of the denominator; 0.053 ohms - treated microfarads as millifarads; 106.1 ohms - used pi instead of 2 pi.","r":"XC = 1/(2 pi f C)"},{"c":"Ohm's law","d":1,"q":"If voltage stays the same and resistance doubles, what happens to current?","o":["It quadruples","It is cut in half","It stays the same","It doubles"],"a":1,"e":"I = E / R. With E fixed, current is inversely proportional to R, so doubling R halves I. Example: 120 V across 10 ohms is 12 A; across 20 ohms is 6 A. Recognise it: 'what happens if' questions - plug the change into the formula and compare. Doubling it (the second choice) mixes up the direct and inverse relationship.","r":"I = E / R"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A split-phase or capacitor-start single-phase motor uses a starting winding because","o":["it increases the frequency","it eliminates slip","single-phase power alone produces no rotating field at standstill","the motor needs DC to start"],"a":2,"e":"A single-phase winding creates a pulsating field, not a rotating one. A second, phase-shifted winding (via resistance or capacitor) creates the rotating field to start the rotor. A centrifugal switch then removes it.","r":"Single-phase motors"},{"c":"Series and parallel circuits","d":1,"q":"What is true of current in a series circuit?","o":["The current equals the source voltage","The current is the same at every point","The current is highest at the load nearest the source","The current divides in proportion to the resistances"],"a":1,"e":"There is only one path, so the same current passes through every component. Voltage divides; current does not. Parallel circuits are the ones where current divides.","r":"Series circuit"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"In a wye-connected system the line-to-neutral voltage is 120 V. What is the line-to-line voltage, to the nearest volt? (Use 1.732.)","o":["69 V","208 V","240 V","360 V"],"a":1,"e":"Formula: E(L-L) = 1.732 x E(L-N) = 1.732 x 120 = 207.8 V, about 208 V. Recognise it: moving from phase to line voltage in a wye multiplies by 1.732. Why the other choices are wrong: 69 V - divided by 1.732 instead of multiplying; 240 V - doubled it (single-phase thinking); 360 V - multiplied by 3.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"Motors","d":2,"q":"A 2-pole motor on 60 Hz turns at 3,450 RPM under load. What is the percent slip, to the nearest tenth?","o":["150.0 percent","4.3 percent","4.2 percent","95.8 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Synchronous speed Ns = 120 x 60 / 2 = 3,600 RPM. Slip = (Ns - Nr) / Ns x 100 = (3,600 - 3,450) / 3,600 x 100 = 4.17 percent. Recognise it: slip is always based on synchronous speed; find Ns first. Why the other choices are wrong: 4.3 percent - divided by rotor speed instead of synchronous speed; 150.0 percent - reported the RPM difference as a percent; 95.8 percent - reported rotor speed as a percent of synchronous.","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Meters and measurement","d":2,"q":"Before relying on a voltage tester to prove a circuit is de-energized, you should","o":["assume it works if the display lights up","verify the tester on a known live source before and after the test","use a lower-rated meter","test only the neutral conductor"],"a":1,"e":"Live-dead-live testing confirms the tester works. A failed meter showing zero volts on an energized circuit is a classic cause of shock injuries.","r":"Test before touch"},{"c":"AC theory","d":3,"q":"A 240 V AC source feeds a series circuit of 12 ohms resistance and 16 ohms inductive reactance. What is the current, to the nearest tenth of an amp?","o":["20.0 A","12.0 A","15.0 A","8.6 A"],"a":1,"e":"Impedance Z = sqrt(12^2 + 16^2) = 20.00 ohms. Current I = E / Z = 240 / 20.00 = 12.00 A. Recognise it: Ohm's law for AC uses impedance instead of resistance; find Z first. Why the other choices are wrong: 8.6 A - added R and X directly; 20.0 A - ignored the reactance; 15.0 A - ignored the resistance.","r":"I = E / Z"},{"c":"Three-phase power","d":1,"q":"In a balanced wye system, the line-to-line voltage is","o":["1.732 times the line-to-neutral voltage","half the line-to-neutral voltage","3 times the line-to-neutral voltage","equal to the line-to-neutral voltage"],"a":0,"e":"E(L-L) = 1.732 x E(L-N). 120 V phase gives 208 V; 277 V gives 480 V. Do not use 3; the factor is the square root of 3.","r":"Wye relationships"},{"c":"AC theory","d":1,"q":"A single-phase motor draws 20 A at 208 V with a power factor of 0.88. How many watts of real power is it using, to the nearest watt?","o":["3,661 W","4,160 W","4,727 W","6,341 W"],"a":0,"e":"Formula: P = E x I x PF = 208 x 20 x 0.88 = 3,661 W. Recognise it: a motor or any inductive load has PF below 1, and real watts equal volt-amps times PF. Why the other choices are wrong: 4,160 W - left out power factor (that is VA); 4,727 W - divided by power factor; 6,341 W - applied 1.732 to a single-phase load.","r":"P = E I PF"}];